hadiths[600]{hadithnumber,text}:
"1","Musnad of Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him. 1 - ... Abu Bakr ibn Uways, and it was said his hearing from al-Zuhri resembles the hearing of Malik; both agreed upon the isnad of this hadith. Several narrated this hadith from al-Zuhri from Malik ibn Aws from Umar without saying: from Abu Bakr; among them Amr ibn Dinar from al-Zuhri. Abu Hurayrah also narrated it from Abu Bakr, and Aishah from Abu Bakr, and Hudhayfah narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"2","2 - Ahmad ibn Aban al-Qurashi narrated it to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah informed us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from al-Zuhri, from Malik ibn Aws, from Umar, that he adjured Talhah, al-Zubayr, Ali and al-Abbas, may Allah have mercy on them: Do you know that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: We are not inherited; whatever we leave is charity? They said: Yes. Others followed Amr in this narration from al-Zuhri, so we sufficed with Amr from al-Zuhri since he was trustworthy. Ikrimah ibn Khalid and Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir narrated it from Malik ibn Aws from Umar without mentioning Abu Bakr. Malik ibn Aws is a memorizer, and he added over those named; the addition of a memorizer is accepted when added over a memorizer, for he added by virtue of memory. A group narrated it from Abu Bakr from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), among them Aishah, Abu Hurayrah and others."
"3","3 - Muhammad ibn Mamar narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Asim ibn Kulayb, he said: A shaykh narrated to me, he said: So-and-so and so-and-so narrated to me, until he counted seven, one of them Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, from Umar, he said: I heard Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No prophet was ever taken until a man of his ummah leads him in prayer. This hadith, we know it not narrated from Abu Bakr except by this route with this isnad, and we know no one who named the man from whom Asim ibn Kulayb narrated, so we mentioned it for that."
"4","What Uthman ibn Affan narrated from Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with them both. 4 - Salamah narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, he said: Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri. (H) And Ibrahim ibn Ziyad al-Saigh narrated it to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Ibrahim ibn Sad narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to me, from Salih ibn Kaysan, from Ibn Shihab, he said: A man of the Ansar, of the people of fiqh, not accused, narrated to me; I heard him narrate to Said ibn al-Musayyab that he heard Uthman ibn Affan, may Allah be pleased with him, narrate: Some men among the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died, grieved until whispering seized some of them. Uthman said: I was among them. While I sat in a stone fort, Umar ibn al-Khattab passed and greeted but I did not notice; Umar went to Abu Bakr and said: Shall I not tell you something strange: I passed Uthman, greeted, and he did not return it. Abu Bakr and Umar came and both greeted. Abu Bakr said: Your brother Umar claims he passed you and greeted and you did not return it. I said: By Allah I was unaware of your passing or greeting. Abu Bakr said: Uthman spoke truth; a matter kept you from it. He said: Yes. What is it? I said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was taken before I asked him about the salvation of this affair. Abu Bakr said: I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about it. I said: May my father and mother be for you, inform me. Abu Bakr said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, what is the salvation of this affair? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever accepts from me the word I offered my uncle, it is salvation for him. Thus Mamar and Salih ibn Kaysan narrated, followed by more than one from al-Zuhri from an Ansari man. Abdullah ibn Bishr narrated it from al-Zuhri from Said ibn al-Musayyab from Uthman from Abu Bakr."
"5","5 - Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahim and al-Fadl ibn Sahl narrated it to us, they said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Salam ibn Harb narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Bishr, from al-Zuhri, from Said ibn al-Musayyab, from Uthman ibn Affan, from Abu Bakr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similarly. Abu Bakr (al-Bazzar) said: I think none but Abdullah ibn Bishr erred; the hadith is that of Mamar and Salih ibn Kaysan with their followers. Muhammad ibn Umar al-Waqidi narrated it from al-Zuhri's nephew from al-Zuhri from Said ibn al-Musayyab from Abdullah ibn Amr from Uthman from Abu Bakr. This hadith is of what Muhammad ibn Umar was not followed in; we mentioned it only so it be known he narrated it thus."
"6","And from what Ali ibn Abi Talib narrated from Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with them both. 6 - Al-Harith ibn al-Khidr al-Attar narrated to us, he said: Sad ibn Abi Said al-Maqburi narrated to us, from his brother Abdullah ibn Said, from his grandfather Abu Said al-Maqburi, he said: I heard Ali ibn Abi Talib narrate from Abu Bakr, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no Muslim who performs wudu excellently, then comes to the mosque and prays two rakahs therein, then seeks Allah's forgiveness, except Allah forgives him."
"7","7 - Abu Kurayb narrated it to us, he said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Said narrated to us, from his grandfather Abu Said al-Maqburi, he said: I heard Ali ibn Abi Talib say: I heard Abu Bakr say: The Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, then he mentioned similar. Abu Bakr (al-Bazzar) said: Sad ibn Said and Abdullah ibn Said, their hadith contains softness. A group narrated from them; we write from their hadith only what was already narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) even by another isnad. These words were narrated by Asma ibn al-Hakam from Ali from Abu Bakr."
"8","8 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated it to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Jafar narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Uthman ibn al-Mughirah, from Ali ibn Rabiah, from Asma or Abu Asma - Shubah doubted - from Ali, he said: I was a man who, when I heard a hadith from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), Allah benefited me as He willed, and when a Companion narrated to me I made him swear, and when he swore I believed him; and Abu Bakr narrated to me - and Abu Bakr spoke truth - that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: There is no Muslim who performs wudu excellently, then comes to the mosque and prays two rakahs, then seeks Allah's forgiveness, except Allah forgives him. Abu Bakr (al-Bazzar) said: Shubah, Misar, Sufyan al-Thawri, Sharik, Abu Awanah and Qays ibn al-Rabi narrated it; none doubted Asma or Abu Asma but Shubah."
"9","9 - Amr ibn Abdullah al-Awdi narrated to us, he said: Waki ibn al-Jarrah narrated to us, he said: Misar and Sufyan narrated to us, from Uthman ibn al-Mughirah, from Ali ibn Rabiah, from Asma ibn al-Hakam, from Ali, from Abu Bakr. Sufyan raised it, Misar did not raise it, and he mentioned similar."
"10","10 - Abd al-Wahid ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Uthman ibn al-Mughirah, from Ali ibn Rabiah, from Asma ibn al-Hakam al-Fazari, from Ali, from Abu Bakr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), like the hadith of Shubah."
"11","11 - Al-Fadl ibn Sahl narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Adam narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from Uthman ibn al-Mughirah, from Ali ibn Rabiah, from Asma ibn al-Hakam, from Ali, from Abu Bakr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similar to Shubah's hadith. Abu Bakr (al-Bazzar) said: These words, we know them not from Abu Bakr from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except by these two routes; Ali's saying 'I was a man who when I heard...' was only narrated by Asma ibn al-Hakam; Asma is unknown, narrated nothing else, none narrated from him but Ali ibn Rabiah; the words were not narrated from Ali except by this route."
"12","And from what Abdullah ibn Masud narrated from Abu Bakr. 12 - Shuayb ibn Ayyub narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Adam narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Asim, from Zirr, from Abdullah, from Abu Bakr and Umar, may Allah have mercy on them, that they gave him glad tidings that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Ask, you will be given. Abu Bakr (al-Bazzar) said: Zaidah narrated it from Asim from Zirr from Abdullah without saying: from Abu Bakr and Umar. None narrated it thus but Yahya ibn Adam from Abu Bakr (ibn Ayyash)."
"13","13 - Ahmad ibn Amr narrated it to us elsewhere with this isnad, adding in its text: from Abu Bakr and Umar that they gave him glad tidings that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever loves to recite the Quran fresh as revealed, let him recite upon the recitation of Ibn Umm Abd. Abu Bakr (al-Bazzar) said: None connected it from Abu Bakr but Yahya ibn Adam; Yahya is trustworthy, from Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash; Abu Bakr was not a memorizer, though scholars narrated from him and bore his hadith, and he added; for Zaidah said: from Asim from Zirr from Abdullah, without Abu Bakr and Umar. The addition belongs to the adder if a memorizer; I hope the hadith is sound, for Abu Bakr and Umar were with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) then, so Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash abridged it."
"14","What Abdullah ibn Abbas narrated from Abu Bakr. 14 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Ismail ibn Raja, from Umayr freedman of Ibn Abbas, from Ibn Abbas, he said: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was taken, al-Abbas disputed with Ali over things the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) left; they came to Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him; he asked him to divide between them but he refused, saying: Anything the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) left, I shall not introduce anything new in it. Abu Bakr (al-Bazzar) said: Its isnad is fair; I recall none narrating it but al-Amash from Ismail ibn Raja with this isnad."
"15","15 - Ibrahim ibn Said al-Jawhari narrated to us, he said: Abu Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Salam ibn Harb narrated to us, he said: Ata ibn al-Saib narrated to us, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, he said: When 'Perish the hands of Abu Lahab' was revealed, Abu Lahab's wife came while the Messenger of Allah sat with Abu Bakr. Abu Bakr said: Would you move aside lest she harm you, O Messenger of Allah. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A barrier shall be placed between me and her. She came and stood over Abu Bakr, saying: O Abu Bakr, did your companion lampoon us? Abu Bakr said: No, by the Lord of this building, he speaks no poetry nor utters it. She said: You are truthful. When she left, Abu Bakr said: Did she not see you? He said: No; an angel kept screening me until she left. Abu Bakr (al-Bazzar) said: Its isnad is fair; it enters Abu Bakr's Musnad since he quoted the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: By the Lord of this building he speaks no poetry; this was Abu Bakr quoting the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"16","16 - Al-Hasan ibn Khalaf al-Wasiti narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Muslim al-Batin, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, he said: When the polytheists expelled the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from Makkah, Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, said: They expelled their prophet; they shall perish. Then was revealed: Permission is given to those fought because they were wronged. Abu Bakr (al-Bazzar) said: None narrated it from al-Thawri but Ishaq al-Azraq."
"17","17 - Qays narrated it from al-Amash from Muslim from Said ibn Jubayr from Ibn Abbas. Umar ibn al-Khattab al-Sijistani narrated it to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Yusuf narrated to us, from Qays."
"18","18 - Muhammad ibn Uthman al-Uqayli narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Ala ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, he said: Husayn ibn Abdullah narrated to me, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was taken, they differed over his burial. Abu Bakr said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: No prophet was taken except he is buried where he was taken. Abu Bakr (al-Bazzar) said: None narrated these words but Abu Bakr; Ibn Abbas and Aishah narrated them from Abu Bakr."
"19","19 - Abu Kurayb narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Husam ibn Misakk narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Ibn Abbas, from Abu Bakr, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ate bread and meat, then prayed without wudu. Abu Bakr (al-Bazzar) said: Hisham ibn Hassan, Ashath ibn Abd al-Malik and others narrated it from Muhammad ibn Sirin from Ibn Abbas from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) without saying: from Abu Bakr. Only Husam said it from Ibn Abbas from Abu Bakr. Husam is not strong; moreover Muhammad ibn Sirin did not hear from Ibn Abbas."
"20","And from what Ibn Umar narrated from Abu Bakr. 20 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Ubaydah narrated to us, he said: The freedman of Ibn Siba informed me, he said: I heard Abdullah ibn Umar narrate from Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, he said: I was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when was revealed: Whoever does evil shall be recompensed and find no protector or helper besides Allah. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Abu Bakr, shall I teach you a verse revealed to me? I said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. He taught it; I felt a break in my back and stretched. He said: What is with you, O Abu Bakr? I said: May my father and mother be for you, which of us has not done evil, and we shall be recompensed. He said: As for you, O Abu Bakr, and your believing companions, you are recompensed in this world until you meet Allah sinless; as for others, it is gathered for them until the Day of Resurrection. Abu Bakr (al-Bazzar) said: It is narrated from Abu Bakr from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only by this route; the freedman of Ibn Siba is unnamed; we mentioned it since it comes only thus, and explained its defect. Musa ibn Ubaydah is devout, good in worship, not a memorizer; I think worship shortened him from memorizing."
"21","21 - Al-Fadl ibn Sahl narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahhab ibn Ata narrated to us, Ziyad al-Jassas - and he is Ziyad ibn Abi Ziyad - narrated to us, from Ali ibn Zayd, from Mujahid, from Ibn Umar, he said: I heard Abu Bakr al-Siddiq say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'Whoever does evil will be recompensed for it in this world.' Abu Bakr (al-Bazzar) said: This hadith was only narrated from Ali ibn Zayd by Ziyad al-Jassas; Ziyad is a Basran man, there is no harm in him, but he is not a memorizer; and as for Ali ibn Zayd, Shubah spoke about him, though eminent men narrated from him: Yunus ibn Ubayd, Ibn Awn and Khalid al-Hadhdha; and we know not that Ali ibn Zayd narrated from Mujahid except this hadith."
"22","22 - Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, he said: Abu Nasr al-Tammar narrated to us, he said: Kawthar ibn Hakim narrated to us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Abu Bakr, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'Whosever feet become dusty in the path of Allah, Allah forbids them to the Fire.' Abu Bakr (al-Bazzar) said: This hadith is only narrated from Abu Bakr by this route; it has been narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by other routes. Kawthar ibn Hakim was narrated from by Hushaym, Abu Nasr al-Tammar and more than one; some of his hadiths none narrated but he, and in some he was corroborated."
"23","What Abu Hurayrah narrated from Abu Bakr. 23 - Abdullah ibn al-Waddah al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: al-Husayn ibn Ali al-Jufi narrated to us, he said: Zaidah narrated to us, from Asim - meaning Ibn Bahdalah - from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: Abu Bakr, may Allah have mercy on him, stood among us and said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood among us as I stand among you today and said: 'People have not been given anything better than pardon and well-being, so ask Allah for them.' This hadith has a fair isnad; we know none who connected it except Zaidah from Asim from Abu Salih from Abu Hurayrah, nor from Zaidah except al-Husayn ibn Ali."
"24","24 - Muhammad ibn Miskin narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Haywah - meaning Ibn Shurayh - narrated to us, he said: I heard Abd al-Malik ibn al-Harith narrate from Abu Hurayrah, he said: I heard Abu Bakr say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: 'You have not been given, after the word of sincerity, anything better than well-being, so ask Allah for well-being.' Abd al-Malik ibn al-Harith - we know none narrated from him but Haywah."
"25","25 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid Hisham ibn Abd al-Malik narrated to us, he said: Hammad - meaning Ibn Salamah - narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Amr, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, from Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: 'We are not inherited; whatever we leave is charity.'"
"26","26 - And Ibrahim ibn Ziyad narrated it to us, he said: Abd al-Wahhab ibn Ata narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Amr narrated to us, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, from Abu Bakr and Umar, may Allah have mercy on them both, similar to it. This hadith - we know no one who narrated it connected except Hammad ibn Salamah and Abd al-Wahhab; others narrate it from Muhammad ibn Amr from Abu Salamah mursal."
"27","27 - Ali ibn al-Hasan al-Samman al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad al-Muharibi narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Ubaydullah, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, from Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, in the story of Abu al-Haytham ibn al-Tayyihan, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him, when he wanted to slaughter a sheep: 'Beware of the one with milk.' This hadith - we know none narrated it from Yahya ibn Ubaydullah except al-Muharibi, and it is not narrated from Abu Hurayrah from Abu Bakr except by this route. Yahya ibn Ubaydullah - Yahya ibn Said used to narrate from him then refrained from narrating from him; a large group of the people of knowledge narrated from him and bore his hadith."
"28","What Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr narrated from Abu Bakr. 28 - Ibrahim ibn Said al-Jawhari narrated to us, he said: al-Hakam ibn Nafi narrated to us, he said: al-Attaf ibn Khalid narrated to us, from Talhah ibn Abdullah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr, from his father, from his grandfather, that he heard Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, say: I said: O Messenger of Allah, do we act in a matter already finished or a matter newly begun? He said: 'No, rather in a matter already finished.' I said: Then what is action for? He said: 'Act, for each is made easy for what he was created for.' These words - we know them not narrated from Abu Bakr except by this route with this isnad; al-Attaf ibn Khalid was narrated from by a group, he is sound in hadith though he narrated hadiths from Nafi in which he was not followed."
"29","And what Abdullah ibn Amr narrated from Abu Bakr. 29 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Abu al-Khayr, from Abdullah ibn Amr, from Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: I said, O Messenger of Allah, teach me a supplication I may supplicate with. He said: Say: O Allah, I have wronged myself greatly, and none forgives sins but You, so forgive me and have mercy on me; indeed You are the Forgiving, the Merciful. This hadith - we know it not narrated from Abu Bakr from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except with this isnad; some companions of al-Layth narrated it from al-Layth with this isnad from Abdullah ibn Amr: that Abu Bakr said: O Messenger of Allah; and some said: from Abu Bakr; so we mentioned it from Abu al-Walid, and sufficed with him since he was trustworthy and had connected it."
"30","30 - Abdullah ibn Shabib narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Jabbar ibn Said al-Masahiq narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Muhammad ibn Abi Hakim narrated to me, from Hisham ibn Sad, from Said ibn Abi Hilal, from Abu Qabil, from Abdullah ibn Amr, he said: Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, wrote to Amr ibn al-As: To proceed, you have known the bequest of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning the Ansar at his death: accept from their good-doer and overlook their offender. This hadith - we know it not narrated from Abu Bakr except by this route with this isnad; Yahya ibn Muhammad ibn Abi Hakim is a man of Madina, there is no harm in him, and those before and after him are so famous their description is needless."
"31","What Zayd ibn Thabit narrated from Abu Bakr. 31 - Ali ibn al-Fadl al-Karabisi narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Sad narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Ubayd ibn al-Sabbaq, from Zayd ibn Thabit, he said: Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, sent to me and said: Collect the Quran, for you used to write the revelation for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). These words - we know no one who narrated them with this wording except Abu Bakr, and Ibrahim ibn Sad mentioned these words. Umarah ibn Ghaziyyah narrated this hadith from al-Zuhri from Kharijah ibn Zayd from his father. So we entered it in the Musnad of Abu Bakr for the fairness of its isnad and the rarity of what is narrated from Abu Bakr from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"32","What Sahl ibn Sad narrated from Abu Bakr. 32 - Abdullah ibn Shabib narrated to us, he said: Harun ibn Yahya ibn Harun ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Hatib narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Abdullah ibn al-Fudayl al-Jarmi narrated to us, from Abu Hazim, from Sahl ibn Sad, he said: Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, came to us while we were in the garden, ascended the minbar, praised Allah and extolled Him, then said: O people, I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say upon these sticks last year: No servant was given anything better than fine certainty and well-being, so ask Allah for fine certainty and well-being. We know not that Sahl ibn Sad connected from Abu Bakr from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except this hadith, nor is this hadith narrated from Sahl ibn Sad except by this route with this isnad; and Sahl ibn Sad narrated from Abu Bakr a mawquf hadith."
"33","33 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated it to us, he said: Umar ibn Ali narrated to us, from Abu Hazim, from Sahl ibn Sad, he said: Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, would not turn in his prayer."
"34","Rifaah ibn Rafi from Abu Bakr. 34 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna and Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, they said: Abd al-Malik ibn Amr narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil, from Muadh ibn Rifaah ibn Rafi, from his father, he said: I heard Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, upon the minbar of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say - and he wept when he mentioned the Messenger of Allah, then he recovered - then said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say, in this heat or in the like of this heat: Ask Allah for pardon, well-being and certainty in this world and the Hereafter. This hadith - we know it not narrated from Rifaah ibn Rafi from Abu Bakr except by this route, nor did Rifaah narrate from Abu Bakr except this hadith."
"35","Abu Said al-Khudri from Abu Bakr. 35 - Abdullah ibn Said al-Kindi narrated to us, he said: Uqbah ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, he said: al-Jurayri narrated to me, from Abu Nadrah, from Abu Said, he said: Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Am I not the most deserving of people for it, am I not the first who embraced Islam, am I not the companion of such-and-such, am I not the companion of such-and-such? This hadith - we know no one who said in it, from Shubah from al-Jurayri from Abu Nadrah from Abu Said, except Uqbah ibn Khalid. Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated it from Shubah from al-Jurayri from Abu Nadrah, he said: Abu Bakr addressed, and did not mention Abu Said."
"36","What Anas ibn Malik narrated from Abu Bakr. 36 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna and Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, they said: Hibban and Affan narrated to us, they said: Hammam narrated to us, from Thabit, from Anas ibn Malik, that Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to him, he said: I said, O Messenger of Allah, while we were in the cave: If a man looked he would see us. He said: What do you think of two, Allah is their third? One of them said in his hadith: If one of them looked at the place of his feet he would see us; he said: What do you think of two, Allah is their third? This hadith - we know it not narrated from Abu Bakr except by this route; Hammam is trustworthy and the isnad is a sound isnad."
"37","37 - Abd al-Quddus ibn Muhammad ibn Abd al-Kabir al-Attar narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Asim al-Kilabi narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn al-Mughirah narrated to us, from Thabit, from Anas, he said: Abu Bakr said after the death of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Come with us, let us visit Umm Ayman as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to visit her. This hadith - we know none narrated it from Sulayman ibn al-Mughirah except Amr ibn Asim, nor is it narrated from Abu Bakr except by this route; the isnad is a sound isnad."
"38","38 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Asim narrated to us, he said: Imran Abu al-Awwam narrated to us, from Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Anas, from Abu Bakr, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'I was commanded to fight people until they say: There is no god but Allah; when they say it, they protect from me their blood and wealth except by its right, and their reckoning is upon Allah.' This hadith - we know it not narrated from Anas from Abu Bakr except by this route; I think Imran erred in its isnad, for Mamar, Ibrahim ibn Sad, Ibn Ishaq and al-Numan ibn Rashid narrated it from al-Zuhri from Ubaydullah from Abu Hurayrah: that Umar said to Abu Bakr: How do you fight people when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I was commanded to fight people until they say: There is no god but Allah? He said: If they withheld from me a young goat or a rope they used to render to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), I would fight them over it. So Imran inverted the isnad of this hadith and made it from Mamar from al-Zuhri from Anas from Abu Bakr. Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Ansari narrated to us, from his father, from Thumamah, from Anas."
"39","39 - Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Musa ibn Ubaydah, from Hud ibn Ata, from Anas, that Abu Bakr, may Allah have mercy on him, said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade killing those who pray. Hud ibn Ata - we know none narrated from him except Musa ibn Ubaydah. Our mention of Musa ibn Ubaydah, of his being occupied with worship to the neglect of guarding hadith, has preceded."
"40","The remainder of what Anas narrated from Abu Bakr. 40 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Ansari narrated to us, from his father, from Thumamah ibn Abdullah ibn Anas, from Anas ibn Malik, that Abu Bakr, may Allah have mercy on him, when he was made caliph, sent him to Bahrayn and wrote for him this letter: In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate. This is the obligation of sadaqah which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) imposed upon the Muslims, which Allah commanded His Messenger with. Whoever among the believers is asked for it in its proper form, let him give it; whoever is asked for more than it, let him not give it. For twenty-four camels and less, for every five: a sheep; when they reach twenty-five to thirty-five, therein is a she-camel in its second year (bint makhad); if there is no bint makhad, then a male two-year-old (ibn labun); when they reach thirty-six to forty-five, therein is a she-camel in its third year (bint labun); when they reach forty-six to sixty, therein is a four-year-old she-camel (hiqqah); when they reach sixty-one to seventy-five, therein is a five-year-old (jadhah); when they reach seventy-six to ninety, therein are two bint labun; when they reach ninety-one to one hundred twenty, therein are two hiqqah, full-grown she-camels; when they exceed one hundred twenty, for every forty: a bint labun, and for every fifty: a hiqqah. Whoever owes a jadhah and has no jadhah but has a hiqqah, it is accepted from him with two sheep if easy for him, or twenty dirhams; whoever owes a hiqqah and has no hiqqah but has a jadhah, the jadhah is accepted from him and the collector gives him twenty dirhams or two sheep; whoever owes a hiqqah and has only a bint labun, it is accepted from him and he gives the collector two sheep or twenty dirhams; whoever owes a bint labun and has a hiqqah, the hiqqah is accepted from him and the collector gives him two sheep or twenty dirhams. He said: There is no sadaqah on grazing sheep until they reach forty, nor on their excess until they reach one hundred twenty; when they exceed one hundred twenty by one, therein are two sheep up to two hundred; when they exceed two hundred, therein are three sheep up to three hundred; when they exceed three hundred, for every hundred sheep: a sheep."
"41","41 - Al-Fadl ibn Sahl narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Muhammad and Surayj ibn al-Numan narrated to us, they said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Thumamah, from Anas, that Abu Bakr, when he was made caliph, wrote for him a letter containing the obligation of sadaqah which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) imposed upon the Muslims, and he mentioned the hadith."
"42","42 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Asim al-Kilabi narrated to us, he said: Imran al-Qattan narrated to us, he said: Mamar narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Anas, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - [the report breaks off here, literally translated as it stands]."
"43","Zayd ibn Arqam from Abu Bakr. 43 - Ahmad ibn Abdullah ibn al-Husayn ibn al-Kurdi narrated to us, he said: Abu Ubaydah Ismail ibn Sinan al-Usfuri narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahid ibn Zayd narrated to us, he said: Aslam al-Kufi narrated to us, from Murrah al-Tayyib, from Zayd ibn Arqam, from Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No body nourished by the unlawful shall enter Paradise; no evil-mannered master shall enter Paradise; cursed is whoever harms a Muslim or deceives him."
"44","44 - Ahmad ibn Abdullah ibn al-Husayn narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahid ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Aslam al-Kufi, from Murrah al-Tayyib, from Zayd ibn Arqam, he said: We were with Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, when he asked for drink; water and honey were brought; when he placed it on his hand he wept and sobbed until we thought something ailed him, yet we asked nothing; when he finished we said: O Caliph of the Messenger of Allah, what made you weep thus? He said: While I was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), I saw him pushing something from himself though I saw nothing. I said: O Messenger of Allah, what do I see you pushing from yourself though I see nothing? He said: The world stretched out to me, so I said: Away from me. It said to me: You shall not escape me. Abu Bakr said: It grieved me, and I feared I had opposed the command of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and that the world had caught up with me. Abd al-Wahid ibn Zayd is a Basran, a devout man, I think he inclined to qadar despite intense worship; Aslam al-Kufi - none narrated from him but Abd al-Wahid; Murrah al-Tayyib is famous, more than one narrated from him. The two hadiths - none narrated them from Zayd ibn Arqam from Abu Bakr but with this isnad. As for 'cursed is whoever harms a Muslim or deceives him,' Farqad narrated it from Murrah from Abu Bakr, but Murrah did not meet Abu Bakr."
"45","Abu Rafi from Abu Bakr. 45 - Ahmad ibn Abdah and al-Hasan ibn Yahya al-Aruzzi narrated to us - the wording is al-Hasan's - they said: al-Husayn ibn al-Ashqar narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr - meaning Ibn Muawiyah - narrated to us, from Musa ibn Abi Aishah, from Hafs ibn Abi Hafs, from Abu Rafi, he said: I heard Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Gold for gold and silver for silver, like for like; the increaser and the one seeking increase are in the Fire. This hadith is known only from the hadith of al-Kalbi from Salamah from Abu Rafi from Abu Bakr. We did not mention it due to al-Kalbi's defect and the consensus of scholars of transmission on abandoning his hadith. We mentioned it with this isnad; Hafs ibn Abi Hafs from whom Musa ibn Abi Aishah narrated - al-Suddi narrated from him too - and Musa ibn Abi Aishah's obscurity has been lifted."
"46","What Amr ibn Hurayth narrated from Abu Bakr. 46 - Al-Abbas ibn Abdullah al-Baksa'i and Umar ibn al-Khattab al-Sijistani narrated to us, they said: Muhammad ibn Kathir al-Missisi narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Shawdhab narrated to us, from Abu al-Tayyah, from al-Mughirah ibn Subay, from Amr ibn Hurayth, from Abu Bakr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - [the matn breaks off here in this route; the completion comes in the next report, literally translated as it stands]."
"47","47 - Bishr ibn Khalid al-Askari narrated it to us, he said: Abu Usamah informed us, from Abu Ishaq al-Fazari, from Ibn Shawdhab, from Abu al-Tayyah, from al-Mughirah ibn Subay, from Amr ibn Hurayth, from Abu Bakr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The Dajjal shall emerge from a land called Khurasan in the east; peoples whose faces are like hammered shields shall follow him. These words - none narrated them from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) but Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him; al-Mughirah ibn Subay - none narrated from him but Abu al-Tayyah. Ibn Abi Arubah narrated this hadith from Abu al-Tayyah."
"48","48 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated it to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Abi Arubah narrated to us, from Abu al-Tayyah, from al-Mughirah ibn Subay, from Amr ibn Hurayth, from Abu Bakr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similarly. Said ibn Abi Arubah did not hear from Abu al-Tayyah; they think he heard it from Ibn Shawdhab or it reached him from him, so he narrated it from Abu al-Tayyah. Ibn Abi Arubah narrated from a group, transmitting from them without hearing from them, without saying 'narrated to us' or 'I heard' from any of them, like Mansur ibn al-Mutamir, Asim ibn Bahdalah and others he narrated from without hearing; when he said 'I' and 'I heard,' he was trusted in what he said."
"49","Abu Barzah from Abu Bakr. 49 - Abu Kamil al-Fudayl ibn al-Husayn narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Ubayd narrated to us, from Humayd ibn Hilal, from Abdullah ibn Mutarrif, from Abu Barzah, he said: I was with Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, when he became angry at a man; I said: Shall I not strike his neck? He said: It belongs to no one after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). This hadith was narrated from Abu Barzah by various routes: Abu al-Sawwar narrated it from Abu Barzah; Amr ibn Murrah narrated it from Salim ibn Abi al-Jad and from Abu Nasr from Abu Barzah. The best isnad in this is the hadith of Yunus from Humayd ibn Hilal; none narrated it from Yunus but Yazid ibn Zuray. Some scholars entered it in Abu Bakr's Musnad though he quoted nothing therein from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); but since Abu Bakr said: It belongs to no one after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), it indicates this act belonged to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) alone, as if quoting the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"50","Al-Bara from Abu Bakr. 50 - Hawtharah ibn Muhammad al-Minqari narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Muhammad al-Anqazi narrated to us, he said: Israil narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Bara, he said: Abu Bakr bought a riding saddle from Azib for thirteen dirhams. Abu Bakr, may Allah have mercy on him, said: Tell al-Bara to carry it to my saddle. He said: No, unless you narrate to us when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) left while you were with him. Abu Bakr said: We left while the polytheists sought us; we traveled our night and day until noon stood, and I looked for shade to shelter in; we found a rock's shade, so I spread a fur for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) therein and said: Lie down, O Messenger of Allah. Then I went to look around for any seeker; I saw a shepherd driving his sheep to the rock seeking what we sought. I asked: Whose are you, boy? He said: A Qurashi man's, naming until I knew him. I said: Is there milk in your sheep? He said: Yes. I said: Will you milk for me? He said: Yes. I ordered him, he held a sheep, then I ordered him and he shook dust from its udder, then I ordered him to shake his hands - Abu Ishaq said: al-Bara said: He shook one hand with the other - he said: He milked for me a cupful of milk, and I had quenched; with me was a water-skin for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with a cloth on its mouth, so I poured over the milk until it cooled. Then I brought it to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: Drink. He drank until I was pleased. I said: Departure, O Messenger of Allah. We departed while they sought us; none caught us but Suraqah ibn Malik on a horse. I said: This seeker has caught us, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Grieve not, Allah is with us. When he neared, two or three spear-lengths between us, I said: The seeker has caught us. I wept. He said: Why do you weep? I said: By Allah I weep not for myself, but for you. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) supplicated against him: Suffice us against him. His horse sank into the earth to its belly; he leapt to the ground and called: O Muhammad, this I think is from you or your doing; pray Allah to save me from this, and by Allah I shall blind those seekers behind me. Take an arrow from me, for you shall pass by camels of mine at such-and-such place; take what you wish. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: We have no need of them. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed for him, so he left and returned to his companions, while I stayed with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) until we reached Madina. People met him on the roads - women and servants - saying: Allah is Great, Muhammad has come, the Messenger of Allah has come. The people disputed who he would stay with. The Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Tonight we stay with Banu al-Najjar, the maternal uncles of Abd al-Muttalib, to honor them thereby. In the morning he went where commanded. Al-Bara said: The first who came to us was Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, with twenty riders. We said: What did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) do? He said: He is behind me. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came; I had already recited surahs of the Mufassal before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came."
"51","51 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated it to us, he said: Uthman ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: Israil narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Bara, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similarly."
"52","52 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Jafar narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Bara, he said: Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Then I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: Drink. He drank until I was pleased. Abu Bakr (al-Bazzar) said: This passage Shubah narrated apart from the rest of the hadith; the hadith in full was narrated by Israil, and Zuhayr ibn Muawiyah and Hudayj ibn Muawiyah, Zuhayr's brother, narrated it; we know not that al-Bara narrated from Abu Bakr except this hadith."
"53","What Uqbah ibn al-Harith narrated from Abu Bakr. 53 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna and Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, they said: Abu Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Said narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Mulaykah, from Uqbah ibn al-Harith, he said: I saw Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, meet al-Hasan ibn Ali and say: May my father be for the one resembling the Prophet, not resembling Ali; and his father Ali was laughing, or smiling. Abu Bakr (al-Bazzar) said: These words were narrated from more than one Companion, that al-Hasan ibn Ali resembled the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); but none narrated it from the Messenger of Allah higher than Abu Bakr; and the one who narrated it from Abu Bakr is a Companion who heard hadiths from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), namely Uqbah ibn al-Harith; we know not that Uqbah narrated from Abu Bakr except this hadith."
"54","What Abu al-Tufayl, named Amir ibn Wathilah, narrated from Abu Bakr. 54 - Abdullah ibn Said al-Kindi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Fudayl narrated to us, he said: al-Walid ibn Jumay narrated to us, from Abu al-Tufayl, he said: Fatimah, may Allah have mercy on her, sent to Abu Bakr, may Allah have mercy on him: O Caliph of the Messenger of Allah, did you inherit the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) or did his family? He said: Rather his family. She said: Then what of the Prophet's share? He said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: When Allah feeds a prophet a portion then takes him, it belongs to the one who stands after him; so I deemed it be returned upon the Muslims. She said: You and the Messenger of Allah know best. Abu Bakr (al-Bazzar) said: This hadith, none narrated it with this wording but Abu Bakr from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), nor know we a route for it from Abu Bakr but this route; Abu al-Tufayl narrated hadiths from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); al-Walid ibn Jumay is a Kufan, a group narrated from him and bore his hadith."
"55","Abd al-Rahman ibn Abza from Abu Bakr. 55 - Muhammad ibn Bashshar and Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, they said: Abu Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Israil narrated to us, from Jabir, from al-Shabi, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abza, from Abu Bakr: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) turned Maiz away four times, then ordered his stoning. We know not that Ibn Abza narrated from Abu Bakr except this hadith, nor has it a route from Abu Bakr but this route."
"56","Tariq ibn Shihab from Abu Bakr. 56 - Al-Fadl ibn Sahl narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Husayn ibn Umar narrated to us, from Mukhariq, from Tariq ibn Shihab, from Abu Bakr, he said: When this verse was revealed: O you who believe, raise not your voices above the Prophet's voice, I said: O Messenger of Allah, by Allah I shall not speak to you but as a secret-confidant. This hadith - none narrated it from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) connected except from Abu Bakr, may Allah have mercy on him; Husayn ibn Umar narrated hadiths not followed in; as for those above Husayn, Mukhariq is famous, and those above him are beyond need of description for their eminence."
"57","What Aishah narrated from Abu Bakr. 57 - Salamah ibn Shabib narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, he said: Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from Aishah, from Abu Bakr, may Allah have mercy on him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: We are not inherited; whatever we leave is charity. Abu Bakr (al-Bazzar) said: These words were narrated from Abu Bakr by more than one route, so we mentioned them from Aishah from Abu Bakr as well."
"58","58 - Al-Hasan ibn Yahya al-Aruzzi narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Ali ibn Zayd, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from Aishah, may Allah have mercy on her, she said: I recited verse about my father; my father said: That is the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Abu Bakr (al-Bazzar) said: This hadith enters the description of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); its isnad is fair; none narrated it but Hammad ibn Salamah with this isnad."
"59","59 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Umar ibn Abi al-Wazir narrated to us, he said: Zanfal Abu Abdullah al-Arafi - who lived at Arafat - narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Mulaykah narrated to us, from Aishah, from Abu Bakr: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he intended a matter, said: O Allah, choose good for me and choose for me. This hadith - we know it not from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except by this route with this isnad; Zanfal was narrated from by more than one, but we know none narrated this hadith but he, so we mentioned it."
"60","60 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Ubayd ibn Aqil narrated to us, he said: My grandfather Ubayd ibn Aqil narrated to me, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Mulaykah, from Aishah, from Abu Bakr, he said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: No prophet was taken except he is buried where he was taken."
"61","Abu Bakr (al-Bazzar) said: 61 - I found in my book from Abu Kurayb, he said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Mulaykah, from Aishah, from Abu Bakr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similarly. Abu Bakr said: These words, none narrated them from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) but Abu Bakr; Ibn Abbas and Aishah narrated them from Abu Bakr."
"62","62 - Ahmad ibn Aban al-Qurashi narrated to us, he said: Anas ibn Iyad narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Yazid narrated to us, from al-Hakam ibn Abdullah, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from Aishah, she said: My father, may Allah be pleased with him, said to me: Shall I not teach you a supplication the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) taught me; and he said: Isa (peace be upon him) used to teach it to the disciples: If you had debt like Uhud then you said it, Allah would settle it for you. I said: Yes. He said: Say: O Allah, Reliever of anxiety, Remover of distress, Answerer of the desperate, Merciful of this world and the Hereafter, You are my Merciful, so have mercy on me with a mercy making me needless of any besides You. Abu Bakr (al-Bazzar) said: This hadith, none narrated it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) but Abu Bakr, nor know we a route for it from Abu Bakr but this route; al-Hakam ibn Abdullah is very weak; we mentioned it only since we preserved it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by no other route, though scholars narrated it despite its content and bore it."
"63","63 - Al-Fadl ibn Sahl narrated to us, he said: Shababab ibn Sawwar narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Yahya ibn Talhah narrated to us, he said: Isa ibn Talhah narrated to me, from Aishah, may Allah have mercy on her, she said: My father narrated: When people turned from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on Uhud day, I was the first to return to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); I looked at a man fighting before him and said: Be Talhah. Then I looked and a man behind me as if a bird; before I knew he reached me - it was Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah; Talhah lay fallen before him. He said: To your brother, he has earned it. We left him and turned to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); two arrows had struck the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his face. I wanted to pull them; Abu Ubaydah kept asking and entreating until I left him; he pulled one arrow, biting with his teeth, and one of his front teeth fell out. He kept asking to let him pull the other; he placed his tooth on the arrow and bit, lest moving harm the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and pulled it; his tooth or one of his teeth fell out. Abu Ubaydah was toothless in front. This hadith, none narrated it from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) but Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, nor know we an isnad for it but this; Ishaq ibn Yahya was narrated from by Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak and a group, and they bore his hadith though it contains; none shared it with him."
"64","64 - Salamah ibn Shabib and al-Fadl ibn Sahl narrated to us, they said: Muhammad ibn al-Hasan al-Madani narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, from Abd al-Hakim ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Farwah, from Yaqub ibn Utbah, from Urwah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: When Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr died he was wept over; Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, came out and said: I apologize to you for these women, they are fresh from jahiliyyah; I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The dead is showered with boiling water by the weeping of the living. Abu Bakr (al-Bazzar) said: This hadith, we know it not from Abu Bakr from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by other than this route; Abd al-Hakim ibn Abdullah is a famous Madinan, sound in hadith; Yaqub ibn Utbah is famous; this Muhammad ibn al-Hasan is soft in hadith, for he narrated hadiths not followed in, though a group of scholars narrated from him; he is known as Muhammad ibn al-Hasan ibn Zubalah al-Makhzumi."
"65","What Qays ibn Abi Hazim narrated from Abu Bakr. 65 - Yahya ibn Habib ibn Arabi narrated to us, he said: al-Mutamir ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, he said: I heard Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah have mercy on him, say: O people, you recite this verse: O you who believe, upon you is yourselves; he who strays harms you not when guided; and I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: When my ummah see the wrongdoer and do not seize his hand, Allah shall soon envelop them all in punishment from Him. These words, we know them not from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with this wording but from Abu Bakr from him. A group connected this hadith from Abu Bakr from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and a group stopped it; among connectors: Shubah, Zaidah ibn Qudamah, al-Mutamir ibn Sulayman, Yazid ibn Harun and others."
"66","As for Shubah's hadith: 66 - Muhammad ibn Mamar narrated it to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Ismail, from Qays, from Abu Bakr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"67","As for Zaidah's hadith: 67 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated it to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, from Zaidah, from Ismail, from Qays, from Abu Bakr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"68","68 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated it to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Ismail informed us, from Qays, from Abu Bakr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similar to al-Mutamir's hadith from Ismail from Qays from Abu Bakr from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Muadh ibn Muadh, Rawh ibn Ubadah and Uthman ibn Umar connected this hadith from Shubah. Bayan narrated it from Qays from Abu Bakr mawquf. Mujalid narrated it from Qays from Abu Bakr; Said ibn Zayd, brother of Hammad, connected it from him."
"69","69 - Al-Hasan ibn Yahya al-Aruzzi narrated it to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Idris narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Zayd, brother of Hammad ibn Zayd, narrated to us, he said: Mujalid narrated to us, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, from Abu Bakr, he said: I heard him say: O people, you recite this verse: O you who believe, upon you is yourselves; he who strays harms you not when guided; and I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: When my ummah see the wrongdoer and do not seize his hand, Allah shall soon envelop them all in punishment from Him."
"70","70 - Abu Kurayb narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Jafar al-Ahmar narrated to us, he said: al-Sari ibn Ismail narrated to us, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, he said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and found him taken; I heard Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Disavowal of lineage, even if slight, is disbelief in Allah. These words, we know them not from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) but from Abu Bakr from him. Qays ibn Abi Hazim narrated it from Abu Bakr with this isnad. Abu Mamar narrated it from Abu Bakr; they differed over raising Abu Mamar's hadith: a group narrated it from al-Amash from Abdullah ibn Murrah from Abu Mamar from Abu Bakr mawquf; some raised it, and the raiser is no proof in hadith. Al-Sari ibn Ismail is not strong; al-Zuhri and a large group narrated from him and bore his hadith."
"71","Abd al-Rahman ibn Yarbu from Abu Bakr. 71 - Abu Kurayb narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ismail ibn Abi Fudayk narrated to us, he said: al-Dahhak ibn Uthman narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yarbu, from Abu Bakr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - [the matn is completed in the next report: What is the righteousness of hajj? He said: Loud talbiyah and shedding blood; literally translated as it stands]."
"72","72 - Rizqullah ibn Musa narrated it to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ismail ibn Abi Fudayk narrated to us, he said: al-Dahhak ibn Uthman narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Said ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Yarbu, or from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yarbu, from Abu Bakr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he was asked: What is the righteousness of hajj? He said: Loud talbiyah and shedding blood (of sacrifice). This hadith, we know it not from Abu Bakr except by this route; Abd al-Rahman ibn Yarbu is early; Ata ibn Yasar, Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir and others narrated from him. Abd al-Rahman ibn Yarbu lived through jahiliyyah."
"73","73 - Al-Abbas ibn Abi Talib and Ibrahim ibn Hani al-Naysaburi narrated to us, they said: Said ibn Sallam narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Sabrah narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yarbu, from Abu Bakr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Between my house and my prayer place is a garden of Paradise's gardens. Abu Bakr (al-Bazzar) said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Sabrah narrated hadiths not followed in, though a group of scholars narrated from him; Said ibn Sallam narrated hadiths not followed in; we mentioned this hadith since we preserved it not from Abu Bakr's hadith, and explained its defect."
"74","Awsat al-Bajali from Abu Bakr. 74 - Ibrahim ibn Hani narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Mughirah Abd al-Quddus ibn al-Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, from Habib ibn Ubayd, from Awsat al-Bajali. 75 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated it to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Jafar narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Khumayr, from Sulaym ibn Amir, from Awsat al-Bajali, close in wording, that Abu Bakr al-Siddiq stood among people, praised Allah and extolled Him, then said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood among us last year, his eyes flowed and he wept, then sat; then he stood again and said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood among us last year and said: Upon you is truthfulness, for it is of righteousness; beware lying, for it is of wickedness; hate not one another, turn not backs, sever not ties; be servants of Allah, brethren, as Allah commanded you; and ask Allah for well-being, for no servant is given better than soundness after certainty. Abu Bakr (al-Bazzar) said: This hadith, we know it not with these wordings from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) but from Abu Bakr from him; this isnad is of the fair isnads from Abu Bakr; we know not that Awsat narrated from Abu Bakr from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) but this hadith. Awsat al-Bajali - none narrated but from Abu Bakr, none narrated from Awsat but Sulaym ibn Amir."
"75","Hudhayfah from Abu Bakr. 76 - Khallad ibn Aslam al-Marwazi narrated to us, he said: al-Nadr ibn Shumayl narrated to us, he said: Abu Naamah - named Amr ibn Isa - narrated to us, he said: Abu Hunaydah al-Bara ibn Nawfal narrated to us, from Walan al-Adawi, from Hudhayfah, from Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) rose one morning, prayed dawn, sat until forenoon when he laughed, then sat in his place until he prayed noon, or he said the first, and Asr and Maghrib, likewise silent, until he prayed the last Isha; then he rose to his family. The Muslims said to Abu Bakr: Ask the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what is with him; today he did what he never did. He said: Yes; he asked him and he said: Yes, there was displayed to me what shall be until the Day of Resurrection of this world and the Hereafter; the first and last were gathered on one plain; people were stricken thereby until they went to Adam (peace be upon him), sweat almost bridling them. They said: O Adam, father of mankind, Allah chose you; intercede for us to your Lord. He said: I met the like of what you met; go to your father, go to Nuh. 'Allah chose Adam and Nuh and the family of Ibrahim and the family of Imran above the worlds.' They go to Nuh and say: Intercede for us to your Lord, for Allah chose you, answered your supplication, and left none of the disbelievers on earth. He says: That is not with me; go to Ibrahim. They go to Ibrahim and say likewise; he says: That is not with me; go to Musa, for Allah spoke to him directly. Musa says: That is not with me; go to Isa, for he heals the blind and leper and revives the dead by Allah's leave. Isa says: That is not with me; but go to the master of Adam's children, Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), for he is the first over whom earth shall split on Resurrection; go to Muhammad to intercede for you to your Lord. Jibril (peace be upon him) comes to his Lord; the Blessed and Exalted says: Permit him and gladden him with Paradise. I think he said: Jibril brings him; he falls prostrate for a Friday's length. Allah says: Raise your head, speak you shall be heard, intercede you shall be granted intercession. He raises his head; when he looks to his Lord he falls prostrate another Friday's length. He says: O Muhammad, raise your head, intercede you shall be granted, ask you shall be given. He goes and falls prostrate; Jibril takes his upper arms; Allah opens upon him supplications He never opened upon any; he says: My Lord, You made me master of Adam's children and first over whom earth splits on Resurrection, without pride. He mentioned the Hawd and said: Its width, I think he said, is between Sanaa and Aylah. Then it is said: Call the truthful; they intercede. Then: Call the prophets; a prophet comes with a group, a prophet with five or six, a prophet with none. Then: Call the martyrs; they intercede for whom they will. When the martyrs do that, Allah says: I am the Most Merciful of the merciful; admit to Paradise whoever associated nothing with Allah. They enter Paradise. Allah says: Look in the Fire, is any left who ever did good? They say: No, I think. A man is brought; He says: Did you ever do good? He says: No, except I was lenient with people in sale. Allah says: Be lenient to My servant as he was lenient to My servants. Then another man is brought from the Fire; Allah says: Did you ever do good? He says: No, except I commanded my children: when I die burn me with fire, then grind me until like kohl, then take me to the sea and scatter me in wind. Allah says: Why did you do that? He says: From fear of You. He says: Look to the kingdom of the greatest king; you have its like. He said: That is what I laughed at in forenoon. This hadith contains two men we know narrated only this: Abu Hunaydah al-Bara ibn Nawfal, we know he narrated nothing else; likewise Walan, we know he narrated only this; though this isnad despite its content was narrated and borne by eminent scholars of transmission."
"76","Bilal from Abu Bakr. 77 - Harun ibn Sufyan al-Mustamli narrated to us, he said: Usayd ibn Zayd narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Abi al-Miqdam narrated to us, he said: Imran ibn Muslim narrated to us, from Suwayd ibn Ghafalah, from Bilal, he said: My master Abu Bakr narrated to me that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: None of you should perform wudu for food he ate whose eating was lawful for him. This hadith, we know it not from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with this wording but by this route with this isnad; Amr ibn Abi al-Miqdam is Amr ibn Thabit; Abu Dawud and a group of scholars narrated from him, though he inclined to Shiism, and his hadith was not abandoned for that. Imran ibn Muslim and Suwayd ibn Ghafalah are beyond need of mention for fame. Usayd ibn Zayd narrated hadiths not followed in; we mentioned this hadith only since we preserved it by no other route, and explained its defect."
"77","What Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr narrated from his father Abu Bakr. 78 - Muhammad ibn al-Layth Abu al-Sabbah narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Makhlad narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Said al-Ansari, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from his father, from Abu Bakr, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded Asma bint Umays, when she gave birth to Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr, to bathe and enter ihram. This is how Yahya ibn Said narrated it, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad from his father from his grandfather; Ubaydullah ibn Umar narrated it from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim from his father from Aishah. He narrated from al-Qasim from Asma. Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr was small when Abu Bakr died, may Allah be pleased with him; he was less than three years old."
"78","79 - Ibrahim ibn Said narrated to us, he said: al-Husayn ibn Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Abd al-Malik al-Fihri narrated to us, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad. Abu Bakr (al-Bazzar) said: I think not that Abdullah ibn Abd al-Malik heard anything from al-Qasim; rather I found this written with me from al-Qasim from his father from his grandfather, he said: I brought Abu Quhafah to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); he said: Would you have left the old man until I come to him. I said: Rather he is worthier to come to you. He said: We honor him for his son's favors to us. This hadith, we know it not from Abu Bakr except by this route. 80 - Musab ibn Abi Dhib narrated from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad from his father or his uncle from Abu Bakr, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When it is the night of mid-Shaban, Allah the Blessed and Exalted descends to the lowest heaven and forgives His servants except the polytheist or the one quarreling with his brother. These hadiths mentioned from Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr from his father contain weakness in some isnads; in my view, Allah knows best, Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr did not hear them from his father due to small age; but people of knowledge narrated them, so we mentioned and explained the defect. Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, was of the most knowledgeable of creation about the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and earliest in companionship; but he remained after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only briefly and was occupied, may Allah have mercy on him, so his hadith from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is little; though he narrated many hadiths, some are mursal so we left them for irsal, some were rejected so we left them, their rejection coming from the men who narrated them; some hadiths have no pleasing isnads nor connected isnads, so we refrained lest speech lengthen."
"79","81 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna - a man of sunnah - said: The nephew of Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir narrated to us, from his uncle Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Jabir, he said: Umar said to Abu Bakr: O best of people. Abu Bakr said: Since you said that, I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The sun never rose upon anyone better than Umar. This hadith, we know it not from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) but by this route; the nephew of Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir - none narrated from him but Abdullah ibn Dawud al-Wasiti; this hadith was borne despite its isnad since it is a virtue of Umar, may Allah be pleased with him. 82 - Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Ghasil narrated from Shurahbil ibn Sad from Jabir from Abu Bakr from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Fear the Fire even with half a date. This hadith was only narrated by a Basran man from Zayd ibn al-Hubab, accused therein; it is said it has no origin by this route, so we refrained from mentioning it. 83 - Wahshi ibn Harb ibn Wahshi narrated from his father from his grandfather from Abu Bakr from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: Khalid ibn al-Walid is a sword of Allah's swords. Abu Wahshi - none narrated from him but his son; he has rejected hadiths narrated by none else; he is unknown in narration though known in lineage. 84 - Zayd ibn Aslam narrated from his father from Abu Bakr, he said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Nothing of the body but complains to Allah of the sharpness of the tongue on Resurrection. Abd al-Samad narrated this from Abd al-Aziz al-Darawardi; we were narrated from al-Darawardi from Zayd ibn Aslam from his father: that Umar entered upon Abu Bakr while he held his tongue saying: This is what brought me to ruinous places. We did not mention Abd al-Samad's hadith since rejected. Yahya ibn Jadah, Abdullah ibn Abi al-Hudhayl and Urwah ibn al-Zubayr narrated from him with sound isnads, but they did not hear from him, may Allah be pleased with him; the hadiths they narrated were narrated by others who heard from him, so we sufficed without mentioning them except Ibn Abi al-Hudhayl's hadith, for we know it not from Abu Bakr but by his narration. 85 - It is what Abu Sinan narrated from Abdullah ibn Abi al-Hudhayl from Abu Bakr, he said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, show me the place of the lower garment; he pointed to mid-shin. This hadith we withheld since Ibn Abi al-Hudhayl did not hear from Abu Bakr though it comes from Abu Bakr only thus. 86-87 - Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla narrated from Abu Bakr two hadiths; both mursal, for Ibn Abi Layla did not hear from Abu Bakr. 88 - Abu Bakr ibn Abi Zuhayr narrated from Abu Bakr. We refrained since Abu Bakr ibn Zuhayr did not hear from Abu Bakr though famous. Hadiths came from places with displeasing, disconnected isnads, so we refrained lest speech lengthen. 89 - Among them a hadith Abu Kabshah al-Anmari narrated from Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, that he said: Whoever lies upon me deliberately. This was narrated by Jariyah ibn Harim from Abdullah ibn Busr from Abu Kabshah. The isnad was obscure, for this Abdullah ibn Busr - none narrated from him but Jariyah ibn Harim and Yusuf ibn Khalid besides this hadith. We heard this hadith only from Amr ibn Malik, so we refrained. 90 - Among them a hadith Abu Mamar narrated from Abu Bakr: Whoever builds for Allah a mosque. This hadith has no isnad, nor do I think this Abu Mamar heard from Abu Bakr; in its isnad were two men not famous in transmission, so we left it. 91 - Also among what we left: a hadith narrated from Abdullah ibn Murrah from Abu Mamar from Abu Bakr; some companions of Hammad raised it from al-Hajjaj from al-Amash, while the trustworthy memorizers stop it: Disavowal of lineage, even slight, is disbelief in Allah. We left it since unsound with us from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). 92 - Also a hadith Zaidah ibn Abi al-Ruqad narrated from Ziyad al-Numayri from Anas from Abu Bakr that he said: O Messenger of Allah, you have grayed. He said: Hud and its sisters grayed me. This hadith has two defects: Zaidah is rejected in hadith; and more than one narrated it from Zaidah from Ziyad from Anas: that Abu Bakr said to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so the report became from Anas, hence we omitted it. It was narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by various routes that Abu Bakr said to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): I see you have grayed. Israil narrated that from Abu Ishaq from Abu Maysarah, and they said from Ikrimah. Shayban ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated it from Abu Ishaq from Ikrimah from Ibn Abbas. Ali ibn Salih narrated it from Abu Ishaq from Abu Juhayfah. Some narrated it from Zakariyya from Abu Ishaq from Masruq from Abu Bakr. The reports are confused in isnads from Abu Ishaq; most: that Abu Bakr said to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so they became from the transmitters, not from Abu Bakr, since Abu Bakr was the addresser. 93 - A hadith was narrated from Samurah from Abu Bakr, from Bakir ibn Shihab, which we rejected and left. It is a hadith narrated from a freedman of Abu Bakr from Abu Bakr that he said: He does not persist who seeks forgiveness, even if he returns seventy times a day. I saw in this isnad two unknown men, so I left it. 94 - Abu Bakr (al-Bazzar) said: A hadith narrated from Hammad ibn Salamah from Thabit from Anas from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Seeking knowledge is an obligation. This is a lie with no origin from Thabit from Anas. As for what is mentioned from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: Seeking knowledge is an obligation upon every Muslim, it was narrated from Anas by various routes; all narrated therein from Anas is unsound. 95 - The hadith of Abu al-Atikah: Seek knowledge even in China. Abu al-Atikah is unknown, nor known whence he is; this hadith has no origin."
"80","96 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahhab ibn Abd al-Majid al-Thaqafi narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah - meaning Ibn Umar - narrated to us, from Wahb ibn Kaysan, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, he said: My father died owing debt; I offered his creditors to take the dates for what was owed, but they refused and saw no sufficiency therein; I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and mentioned it. He said: When you harvest and place it in the drying yard, inform me. When I harvested and placed it in the yard, I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); he came with Abu Bakr and Umar, may Allah have mercy on them both; he sat, invoked blessing therein, then said: Call your creditors and pay them. I left none owed by my father unpaid, and thirteen wasq remained: seven ajwah and six lawn, or six ajwah and seven lawn. I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and mentioned it; he laughed and said: Go to Abu Bakr and Umar and inform them. I came and informed them; they said: We knew, when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did what he did, that it would be so. This hadith of Wahb ibn Kaysan, we refrained from entering in Abu Bakr's hadith, may Allah be pleased with him, since Abu Bakr and Umar quoted nothing from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); had we narrated every hadith whose body is from a Companion with an interpretable word of Abu Bakr, it would not enter Abu Bakr's Musnad, for their multitude. 97 - Of that is what we left, not entering Abu Bakr's Musnad: what Bakkar nephew of Musa ibn Ubaydah narrated from Musa ibn Ubaydah from Iyas ibn Salamah from Abu Dharr: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: O Abu Dharr, tribulation shall befall you; and he mentioned something the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed him with; when the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was taken, Abu Bakr said to him: I knew what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to you; but Abu Bakr quoted nothing from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to be written, besides that the hadith has two defects: Musa ibn Ubaydah, we mentioned his hadith contains rejection and error; he had worship occupying him from guarding hadith; others among hadith scholars weaken Musa ibn Ubaydah and do not adduce him, but we mentioned him for worship in the best terms, hoping thereby safety. Bakkar his nephew is weak in hadith; Yahya ibn Main - though many scholars adduce him and deem him an imam - spoke about Ishaq ibn Idris, that his hadith is not written; but we refrained on this point since what Yahya said was unclear to us, so we did not advance upon Ishaq what he advanced. 98 - It was narrated from Muhammad ibn Ali from Jabir; from Ibn al-Munkadir from Jabir; from al-Shabi from Jabir, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: If wealth comes to me I shall give you thus and thus. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was taken [the report continues in the source with the Abu Bakr caliphate fulfillment account; literally translated as it stands]."
"81","102 - Al-Fadl ibn Sahl narrated to us, he said: Khalaf ibn Tamim narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Mutayr al-Qurashi narrated to us, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, that Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, said to his son: O my son, if trouble breaks out among people, go to the cave where you saw me hide with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); stay therein, for your provision shall come to you therein morning and evening. This hadith, none narrated it but Khalaf ibn Tamim."
"82","103 - Ali ibn al-Mundhir narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Fudayl narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to me, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was taken, Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, was in a suburb of Madina. He said: He entered upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), placed his mouth on the Messenger of Allah's forehead, kissing him, saying: May my father and mother be for you, you were pure alive and dead. When he came out he passed Umar, may Allah have mercy on him, saying: By Allah the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) has not died, nor shall he die until we kill the hypocrites. He said: They had rejoiced at the Messenger of Allah's death and raised their heads. Abu Bakr passed him and said: O man, restrain yourself, for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) has died. Have you not heard Allah say: You shall die and they shall die; We granted no human before you eternity; if you die, are they eternal? He said: He came to the minbar, ascended, praised Allah, then said: O people, if Muhammad was your god whom you worship, your god has died; if your god is Allah in heaven, your god lives and dies not. He said: Then he recited: Muhammad is but a messenger, messengers passed before him, to the verse's end. He said: Then he descended; the believers rejoiced thereby intensely, gloom seized the hypocrites. Abdullah ibn Umar said: By Him in whose Hand is my soul, as if covers were on their faces and were lifted. This hadith, none narrated it from Nafi from Ibn Umar but Fudayl ibn Ghazwan. End of the first juz; praise to Allah and His blessings upon His Prophet."
"83","1 (m) - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: Malik narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Malik ibn Aws ibn al-Hadathan, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, from Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: We are not inherited; whatever we leave is charity. This hadith was narrated from Abu Bakr by more than one route, and Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, narrated it from him; so we mentioned Umar's hadith from Abu Bakr and sufficed without repeating it from Aishah. None narrated this hadith from Malik thus but Bishr ibn Umar; a group narrated it from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so we sufficed with Abu Bakr's hadith. The hadith of Malik ibn Aws ibn al-Hadathan is disputed: more than one narrated it without saying: from Abu Bakr; the hadith belongs to whoever added."
"84","64 (m) - Salamah ibn Shabib narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Hasan al-Madani narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, from Abd al-Hakim ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Farwah, from Yaqub ibn Utbah, from Urwah, from Aishah, she said: When Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr died he was wept over; Abu Bakr came out and said: I apologize to you for these women, they are fresh from jahiliyyah; I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The dead is showered with boiling water by the weeping of the living. This hadith, we know it not from Abu Bakr by other than this route, nor know we this wording from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) like these words and meaning; so we mentioned Abu Bakr's hadith, may Allah be pleased with him, with its differing wording narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Abd al-Hakim ibn Abdullah is a Madinan, sound; Yaqub ibn Utbah is famous; Muhammad ibn al-Hasan al-Madani is soft in hadith, narrated hadiths not followed in, though scholars narrated from him and bore his hadith; we mentioned it despite its defect since we preserved its wording only by this route with this isnad."
"85","58 (m) - Al-Hasan ibn Yahya al-Aruzzi narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Ali ibn Zayd, from al-Qasim, from Aishah, she said: I recited verse about my father; he said: That is the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). This hadith enters the Prophet's description; its isnad is fair; we know not that Ali ibn Zayd connected from al-Qasim but this hadith, nor narrated this description but Abu Bakr with this isnad."
"86","59 (m) - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: Zanfal Abu Abdullah narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Mulaykah narrated to us, from Aishah, from Abu Bakr, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he intended a matter, said: O Allah, choose good for me and choose for me. This hadith, we know it not from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) but by this route with this isnad; Zanfal was narrated from by more than one, but he was not followed in this hadith; since we preserved these words from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only by Zanfal's narration, we had to write it and explain its defect."
"87","60 (m) - Ahmad ibn Aban al-Qurashi narrated to us, he said: Anas ibn Iyad narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Yazid narrated to us, from al-Hakam ibn Abdullah, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from Aishah, she said: My father said to me: Shall I not teach you a supplication the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) taught me; Isa used to teach it to the disciples: If you had debt like Uhud and you said it, Allah would settle it for you. She said: I said: Yes. He said: Say: O Allah, Reliever of anxiety, Remover of distress, Answerer of the desperate, Merciful of this world and the Hereafter, You are my Merciful, have mercy on me with a mercy making me needless of any besides You. This hadith, none narrated it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) but Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, nor know we a route but this; none narrated it from Yunus ibn Yazid but Anas ibn Iyad, Sulayman ibn Bilal and Abdullah ibn Umar; al-Hakam ibn Abdullah is weak; we mentioned it only since we preserved it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by no other route, hence we wrote it."
"88","61 (m) - Abu Kurayb narrated to us, he said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Mulaykah, from Aishah, from Abu Bakr, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: No prophet was taken except he is buried where he was taken. This hadith, none narrated it from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) but Abu Bakr; Ibn Abbas narrated it from Abu Bakr too, with Aishah."
"89","63 (m) - Al-Fadl ibn Sahl narrated to us, he said: Shababab ibn Sawwar narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Yahya ibn Talhah narrated to us, he said: Isa ibn Talhah narrated to me, from Aishah, she said: My father narrated to me, he said: When people turned from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on Uhud day, I was first to come to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); I looked at a man fighting before him and said: Be Talhah. Then a man behind me as a bird overtook me - Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah; Talhah lay fallen before him. He said: To your brother, he has earned it. We left him and turned to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); two arrows had struck him in his face. I wanted to pull them; Abu Ubaydah entreated until I left him; he pulled one, biting with teeth, and one front tooth fell out. He entreated to pull the other; he placed his tooth on the arrow and bit, lest moving harm the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and pulled it; his tooth or one tooth fell out. Abu Ubaydah was toothless in front. This hadith, none narrated it from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) but Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, nor know we an isnad but this; Ishaq ibn Yahya is soft in hadith, though a group narrated from him, among them al-Thawri, Ibn al-Mubarak and others, and they bore his hadith."
"90","8 (m) - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Jafar narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Uthman ibn al-Mughirah, from Ali ibn Rabiah, from Asma or Abu Asma, from Ali, he said: I heard him say [the wudu two-rakah forgiveness report; literally translated as it stands, the full matn in no. 93]."
"91","9 (m) - (H) Amr ibn Abdullah al-Awdi narrated to us, he said: Waki narrated to us, he said: Misar and Sufyan narrated to us, from Uthman ibn al-Mughirah, from Ali ibn Rabiah, from Asma ibn al-Hakam, from Ali [the wudu two-rakah report; literally translated as it stands, full matn in no. 93]."
"92","11 (m) - (H) Hawtharah ibn Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Adam narrated to us, Sharik narrated to us, from Uthman, from Ali ibn Rabiah, from Asma ibn al-Hakam, from Ali [the wudu two-rakah report; literally translated as it stands, full matn in no. 93]."
"93","10 (m) - (H) Abd al-Wahid ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Uthman ibn al-Mughirah, from Ali ibn Rabiah, from Asma ibn al-Hakam, from Ali ibn Abi Talib, he said: I was a man who, when I heard a hadith from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), Allah benefited me as He willed; when one of his Companions narrated to me I made him swear, and when he swore I believed him; and Abu Bakr narrated to me - and Abu Bakr spoke truth - that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: There is no Muslim who performs wudu excellently, then comes to the mosque and prays two rakahs, then seeks Allah's forgiveness, except Allah forgives him."
"94","7 (m) - Abu Kurayb narrated to us, he said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Said, from his grandfather Abu Said al-Maqburi, from Ali, from Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similarly."
"95","6 (m) - Al-Harith ibn al-Khidr al-Attar narrated to us, he said: Sad ibn Said narrated to us, from his brother Abdullah ibn Said, from his grandfather Abu Said al-Maqburi, he said: I heard Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, narrate from Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah have mercy on him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similar to Ali's hadith which Asma ibn al-Hakam narrated. This hadith, we know it not from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) but by the isnad mentioned; both isnads are defective: Asma ibn al-Hakam is unknown, narrated nothing else, none narrated from him but Ali ibn Rabiah, and no such hadith is adduced, besides Shubah doubted his name; Abdullah ibn Said is rejected in hadith, scholars of transmission unanimously weaken him, so he must not be taken as proof in his singulars; where the trustworthy share, we sufficed with the trustworthy without his narration."
"96","23 (m) - Abdullah ibn al-Waddah narrated to us, he said: al-Husayn ibn Ali al-Jufi narrated to us, he said: Zaidah narrated to us, from Asim, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: Abu Bakr, may Allah have mercy on him, stood among us and said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood among us as I stand among you today and said: People have not been given anything better than pardon and well-being, so ask Allah for them. This hadith was narrated from Abu Bakr by various routes with differing wordings; we mention each in its place with its wording. None connected it from Abu Salih from Abu Hurayrah from Abu Bakr but al-Husayn ibn Ali; they differed from Husayn: some said from Abu Salih from Abu Bakr, some from Abu Salih from Abu Hurayrah from Abu Bakr; the hadith belongs to the adder if trustworthy."
"97","24 (m) - Muhammad ibn Miskin narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Haywah narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn al-Harith, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: I heard Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, upon the minbar say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: You have not been given, after the word of sincerity, better than well-being, so ask Allah for well-being. This hadith, we know it not from any with this wording but from Abu Bakr; it was narrated from Abu Bakr by various routes; we mention all narrated from Abu Bakr by a route when one of its narrators adds a word, each as a separate hadith; none narrated from Abd al-Malik ibn al-Harith but Haywah."
"98","32 (m) - Abdullah ibn Shabib narrated to us, he said: Harun ibn Yahya ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Hatib narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Abdullah ibn Fudayl al-Jarmi narrated to us, from Abu Hazim, from Sahl ibn Sad, he said: Abu Bakr came to us while we were in the garden, ascended the minbar, praised Allah, then said: O people, I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say upon these sticks last year: No servant was given better than fine certainty and well-being, so ask Allah for fine certainty and well-being. We mentioned this hadith from Abu Bakr though we mentioned similar in meaning with other wording for two reasons: its wording differs from Abu Hurayrah's wording; and Sahl ibn Sad connected from Abu Bakr nothing but this hadith."
"99","34 (m) - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna and Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, they said: Abd al-Malik Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil, from Muadh ibn Rifaah, from his father, from Abu Bakr: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say in this heat or the like: Ask Allah for pardon, well-being and certainty in this world and the Hereafter. Similar words were narrated by various routes with other wording from Abu Bakr, so we mentioned each wording with its isnad in its place. Zuhayr ibn Muhammad was narrated from by trustworthy, among them Ibn Mahdi, Abu Amir al-Aqadi, Abdullah ibn Wahb, al-Walid ibn Muslim and others. Abdullah ibn Muhammad was narrated from by scholars and they bore his hadith."
"100","21 (m) - Al-Fadl ibn Sahl narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahhab ibn Ata narrated to us, he said: Ziyad al-Jassas narrated to us, from Ali ibn Zayd, from Mujahid, from Ibn Umar, he said: I heard Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever does evil shall be recompensed in this world. This hadith, none narrated it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) but Abu Bakr; Ali ibn Zayd narrated from Mujahid nothing but this; Ziyad narrated from Ali ibn Zayd from Mujahid nothing but this. Ziyad is a Basran, no harm in him; Ali ibn Zayd was spoken about, though they bore his hadith."
"101","22 (m) - Amr ibn Ali and Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, they said: Abu Nasr al-Tammar narrated to us, he said: Kawthar ibn Hakim narrated to us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whosever feet become dusty in Allah's path, Allah forbids them to the Fire. This hadith, we know it not from Abu Bakr but by this route; it was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by various routes, the highest narrating it being Abu Bakr; Kawthar ibn Hakim was narrated from by Hushaym and others; he narrated hadiths not followed in, from Nafi, though scholars narrated his hadiths."
"102","20 (m) - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Ubaydah narrated to us, he said: The freedman of Ibn Siba narrated to me, he said: I heard Abdullah ibn Umar narrate from Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: I was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when was revealed: Whoever does evil shall be recompensed and find no protector or helper besides Allah. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Abu Bakr, shall I teach you a verse revealed to me? I said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. He taught it; I felt my back break and stretched. He said: What is with you, O Abu Bakr? I said: May my father and mother be for you, which of us has not done evil, and we shall be recompensed. He said: As for you, O Abu Bakr, and your believing companions, you are recompensed in this world until you meet Allah the Blessed and Exalted sinless; as for others, it is gathered for them until recompensed on Resurrection. This hadith, none narrated it with this wording but Abu Bakr, nor know we an isnad for it from Abu Bakr but this; the freedman of Ibn Siba is unknown, none narrated from him but Musa ibn Ubaydah; Musa had no harm but was no memorizer, though scholars narrated from him."
"103","37 (m) - Abd al-Quddus ibn Muhammad ibn Abd al-Kabir al-Attar narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Asim narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn al-Mughirah narrated to us, from Thabit, from Anas, he said: Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, said after the Messenger of Allah's death: Come with us, let us visit Umm Ayman as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to visit her. This hadith, we know it not from Abu Bakr but by this route, and it is sound in isnad."
"104","36 (m) - Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, he said: Affan narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, from Thabit, from Anas: that Abu Bakr narrated to him [the cave report: What do you think of two, Allah is their third; literally translated as it stands, full matn in no. 105]."
"105","(H) Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Hibban narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, from Thabit, from Anas ibn Malik, that Abu Bakr narrated to him, he said: I said, O Messenger of Allah, while we were in the cave: If a man looked he would see us. He said: What do you think of two, Allah is their third? This hadith, we know it not from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) but from Abu Bakr, nor know we a route but this; none narrated it from Thabit but Hammam alone; Hammam was narrated from by scholars, they bore his hadith and counted him among those adduced."
"106","39 (m) - Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Ubaydah narrated to us, from Hud ibn Ata, from Anas, from Abu Bakr, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade striking those who pray. This hadith was narrated from more than one; the highest narrating it from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is Abu Bakr; we know no route for Abu Bakr but this. Hud ibn Ata - none narrated from him but Musa ibn Ubaydah; our mention of Musa ibn Ubaydah preceded, so we sufficed without repeating."
"107","16 (m) - Al-Hasan ibn Khalaf al-Wasiti narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Yusuf narrated to us, from Sufyan, from al-Amash, from Muslim al-Batin, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, he said: When the polytheists expelled the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from Makkah, he said: Abu Bakr said: They expelled their prophet; they shall perish. Then this verse was revealed: Permission is given to those fought because they were wronged, the verse. This hadith has a fair isnad; we entered it in Abu Bakr's hadith for the rarity of Abu Bakr's hadith and fairness of its isnad, though most enter it in Ibn Abbas's hadith."
"108","81 (m) - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Dawud al-Tammar narrated to us, he said: Abu Musa - a man of sunnah - narrated to us, he said: The nephew of Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir narrated to us, from his uncle Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Jabir, he said: Umar said to Abu Bakr: O best of people. He said: Since you said that, I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The sun never rose upon a man better than Umar. This hadith, we know it not from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by any route but from Abu Bakr with this isnad; the nephew of Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir is not well known, but we mentioned it since it is preserved from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only by this route, and scholars narrated it."
"109","82 (m) - Muhammad ibn Ismail narrated to us, he said: Zayd ibn al-Hubab narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Ghasil narrated to us, from Shurahbil ibn Sad, from Jabir, from Abu Bakr, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Fear the Fire even with half a date, for it straightens crookedness and withholds from the hungry what it withholds from the full. This hadith, none narrated it from Zayd ibn al-Hubab but this Muhammad ibn Ismail, none followed him; it is not narrated from Abu Bakr but by this route, nor preserved with this wording but by this route alone, hence we wrote it and explained its defect."
"110","29 (m) - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Abu al-Khayr, from Abdullah ibn Amr, from Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, teach me a supplication. He said: Say: O Allah, I have wronged myself greatly, and none forgives sins but You, so forgive me and have mercy on me; none forgives sins but You. This, we know it not from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) but from Abu Bakr by this route; its isnad is fair; more than one narrated it from al-Layth ibn Sad, so we confined to Abu al-Walid's narration."
"111","30 (m) - Abdullah ibn Shabib narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Jabbar ibn Said al-Masahiq narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Muhammad ibn Abi Hakim narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Sad, from Said ibn Abi Hilal, from Abu Qabil, from Abdullah ibn Amr, he said: Abu Bakr al-Siddiq wrote to Amr ibn al-As, may Allah be pleased with him: To proceed, you have known the Messenger of Allah's bequest concerning the Ansar at his death: accept from their good-doer and overlook their offender. This hadith, we know it not from Abu Bakr but by this route, and its isnad is fair."
"112","44 (m) - Ahmad ibn Abdullah ibn al-Husayn narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahid ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Aslam al-Kufi, from Murrah al-Tayyib, from Zayd ibn Arqam, he said: We were with Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, when he asked for drink; water and honey were brought; when he placed it on his hand he wept and sobbed until we thought something ailed him, yet we asked nothing; when he finished we said: O Caliph of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), what made you weep thus? He said: While I was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), I saw him pushing something from himself though I saw nothing. I said: O Messenger of Allah, what did I see you pushing from yourself though I see nothing? He said: The world stretched out to me, so I said: Away from me. It said: You shall not escape me. It grieved me, and I feared I had opposed the Messenger of Allah's command and that the world caught me. This hadith, we know it not from the Messenger of Allah but by this route; none narrated it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) but Abu Bakr, nor from Abu Bakr but Zayd ibn Arqam, nor from Zayd but Murrah, nor from Murrah but Aslam al-Kufi; Aslam is unknown, none narrated from him but Abd al-Wahid ibn Zayd; Abd al-Wahid was not strong in hadith, a devout Basran, not a memorizer with scholars; we mentioned it despite its defect since we found no other route from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), hence we wrote it."
"113","43 (m) - Ahmad ibn Abdullah ibn al-Husayn narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Sinan Abu Ubaydah narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahid ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Aslam al-Kufi, from Murrah al-Tayyib, from Zayd ibn Arqam, from Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No evil-mannered master shall enter Paradise; cursed is whoever betrays a Muslim or deceives him. This hadith, we know it not from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with this wording but from Abu Bakr; none said: from Murrah from Zayd ibn Arqam but Aslam al-Kufi; our mention of him and of Abd al-Wahid ibn Zayd preceded, so we sufficed without repeating; others than Abd al-Wahid and Aslam narrated it from Murrah al-Tayyib from Abu Bakr, but none said: from Murrah from Zayd but Aslam; Murrah did not meet Abu Bakr."
"114","49 (m) - Abu Kamil narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Ubayd narrated to us, from Humayd ibn Hilal, from Abdullah ibn Mutarrif, from Abu Barzah, he said: I was with Abu Bakr when he became angry at a man; I said: Shall I not strike his neck? He said: Hush, it belongs to no one after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). This hadith, we know it not but from Abu Barzah from Abu Bakr; it has many routes from Abu Barzah, this route among the best; scholars entered it in Abu Bakr's Musnad though nothing was quoted therein from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); but since Abu Bakr said: It belongs to no one after the Messenger of Allah, it indicates this act belonged to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) alone, as if quoting the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"115","47 (m) - Bishr ibn Khalid al-Askari narrated to us, he said: Abu Usamah narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq al-Fazari narrated to us, from Ibn Shawdhab, from Abu al-Tayyah, from al-Mughirah ibn Subay, from Amr ibn Hurayth, from Abu Bakr, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The Dajjal shall emerge from a land called Khurasan in the east; peoples whose faces are like hammered shields shall follow him."
"116","46 (m) - Al-Abbas ibn Abdullah narrated it to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Shawdhab narrated to us, from Abu al-Tayyah, from al-Mughirah ibn Subay, from Amr ibn Hurayth, from Abu Bakr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) [the Dajjal Khurasan report; literally translated as it stands, full matn in no. 115]."
"117","48 (m) - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated it to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Abi Arubah narrated to us, from Abu al-Tayyah, from al-Mughirah ibn Subay, from Amr ibn Hurayth, from Abu Bakr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similar to Abu Ishaq al-Fazari's hadith. This hadith, none narrated it from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with this wording but Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him; none narrated it from Abu Bakr but Amr ibn Hurayth, nor from Amr but al-Mughirah ibn Subay; al-Mughirah, we preserve not that any narrated from him but Abu al-Tayyah, nor know we he narrated aught but this; Ibn Abi Arubah did not hear from Abu al-Tayyah, rather it is said he heard it from Ibn Shawdhab from Abu al-Tayyah."
"118","55 (m) - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna and Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, they said: Abu Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Israil narrated to us, from Jabir, from al-Shabi, from Ibn Abza - namely Abd al-Rahman ibn Abza - from Abu Bakr: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) turned Maiz away four times, meaning: he stoned him. This hadith, none narrated it from Abu Bakr but Abd al-Rahman ibn Abza, a man who narrated other hadiths from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); none narrated it from Ibn Abza but al-Shabi, nor from al-Shabi but Jabir, though scholars spoke about him yet narrated from him: Shubah, al-Thawri, Zuhayr, Sharik, Abu Awanah, Ibn Uyaynah, Hushaym, Israil and others narrated from him. We mentioned this hadith from Abu Bakr for Abu Bakr's eminence, though similar about turning Maiz back is narrated from others than Abu Bakr."
"119","53 (m) - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna and Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, they said: Abu Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Said narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Mulaykah, that Uqbah ibn al-Harith said: I saw Abu Bakr meet al-Hasan ibn Ali and say: May my father be for the Prophet's likeness, not Ali's likeness; his father Ali laughing or smiling. These words are narrated from more than one: that al-Hasan ibn Ali resembled the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); but none narrated it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) higher than Abu Bakr; and its narrator from Abu Bakr heard hadiths from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and narrated them, so we mentioned this from Abu Bakr for this reason; its isnad is sound."
"120","56 (m) - Al-Fadl ibn Sahl narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Husayn ibn Umar narrated to us, from Mukhariq, from Tariq, from Abu Bakr, he said: When this verse was revealed: O you who believe, raise not your voices above the Prophet's voice, I said: O Messenger of Allah, by Allah I shall speak to you only as a secret-confidant. This hadith, we know it not from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) connected but from Abu Bakr, may Allah have mercy on him; Husayn ibn Umar narrated hadiths not followed in; we mentioned it despite Husayn's softness since it comes from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with connected isnad only by this route, hence we mentioned it."
"121","54 (m) - Abdullah ibn Said narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Fudayl narrated to us, he said: al-Walid ibn Jumay narrated to us, from Abu al-Tufayl, he said: Fatimah, may Allah be pleased with her, sent to Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him: O Caliph of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), did you inherit the Messenger of Allah or did his family? He said: Rather his family. She said: Then what of the Prophet's share? He said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: When Allah feeds a prophet a portion then takes him, it belongs to the one who stands after him; so I deemed it be returned upon the Muslims. She said: You and the Messenger of Allah know best. This hadith, none narrated it with this wording but Abu Bakr from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), nor know we a route for it from Abu Bakr but this; Abu al-Tufayl narrated hadiths from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); al-Walid ibn Jumay is a Kufan, more than one narrated from him and bore his hadith."
"122","28 (m) - Ibrahim ibn Said al-Jawhari narrated to us, he said: al-Hakam ibn Nafi narrated to us, he said: al-Attaf ibn Khalid narrated to us, from Talhah ibn Abdullah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr, from his father, from his grandfather, that he heard Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, say: I said, O Messenger of Allah: Do we act in a finished matter or a begun one? He said: Rather in a finished matter. I said: Then what is action for? He said: Act, for each is made easy for what he was created for. This hadith, we know it not from Abu Bakr but by this route, no other route; these words were narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by various routes with differing wordings, the most eminent narrating it being Abu Bakr; its isnad is fair, except Attaf ibn Khalid was spoken about, though a group of scholars narrated from him and bore his hadith."
"123","71 (m) - Abu Kurayb narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ismail ibn Abi Fudayk narrated to us, he said: al-Dahhak ibn Uthman narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yarbu, from Abu Bakr, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked: What is the righteousness of hajj? He said: Loud talbiyah and shedding blood. This hadith is not narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by a higher route than this; a group narrated it from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the highest being Abu Bakr; we know no other route; Abd al-Rahman ibn Yarbu is known; Ata ibn Yasar and others narrated from him."
"124","74 (m) - Ibrahim ibn Hani narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Mughirah Abd al-Quddus ibn al-Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, from Habib ibn Ubayd, from Awsat al-Bajali. 75 (m) - (H) Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Jafar narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Khumayr, from Sulaym ibn Amir, from Awsat al-Bajali, that Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, stood among people, praised Allah, then said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood among us last year - his eyes flowed and he wept, then sat - then he stood again and said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood among us last year and said: Upon you is truthfulness, for it is of righteousness; beware lying, for it is of wickedness; hate not, turn not backs, sever not ties; be brethren as Allah commanded you; ask Allah for well-being, for no servant is given better than soundness after certainty. This hadith, we know it not with these wordings from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) but from Abu Bakr from him; this isnad is of the best isnad narrated from Abu Bakr thereof."
"125","65 (m) - Al-Abbas ibn al-Walid and Yahya ibn Habib ibn Arabi narrated to us, they said: Mutamir ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, he said: I heard Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, say: O people, you recite this verse: O you who believe, upon you is yourselves; he who strays harms you not when guided; and I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: When my ummah see the wrongdoer and do not seize his hand, they shall soon be enveloped by Allah in punishment from Him. This hadith, we know it not from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with this wording by a higher route nor with a fairer isnad from Abu Bakr; a group connected it, among them al-Mutamir and Shubah."
"126","66 (m) - Muhammad ibn Mamar narrated it to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, from Shubah, from Ismail, from Qays, from Abu Bakr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) [the wrongdoer-seizing report; literally translated as it stands, full matn in no. 65]; Zaidah also connected it."
"127","67 (m) - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, from Zaidah, from Ismail, from Qays, from Abu Bakr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) [the wrongdoer-seizing report; literally translated as it stands, full matn in no. 65]. A group stopped it; the hadith belongs to the adder if trustworthy; Shubah, Zaidah, al-Mutamir and others connected it, so we confined to those mentioned."
"128","70 (m) - Abu Kurayb narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Jafar al-Ahmar narrated to us, he said: al-Sari ibn Ismail narrated to us, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, he said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and found him taken; I heard Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Disavowal of lineage, even slight, is disbelief in Allah. This hadith, we know it not from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) but from Abu Bakr, may Allah have mercy on him, from him; al-Sari ibn Ismail is not strong, though scholars narrated from him and bore his hadith."
"129","93 (m) - Musa ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Abu Yahya al-Himmani narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Waqid narrated to us, from Abu Nusayrah, from a freedman of Abu Bakr, from Abu Bakr, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: He does not persist who seeks forgiveness, even if he returns seventy times a day. This hadith, we preserve it not from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by any route but from Abu Bakr by this route; Uthman ibn Waqid is famous, Abu Muawiyah, Abu Yahya al-Himmani and others narrated from him; Abu Nusayrah and Abu Bakr's freedman are unknown; but since this hadith is known only by this route, we had to write it and explain its defect."
"130","12 (m) - Shuayb ibn Ayyub al-Wasiti narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Adam narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Asim, from Zirr, from Abdullah, from Abu Bakr and Umar, may Allah have mercy on them both, that they gave him glad tidings that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever loves to recite the Quran fresh as revealed, let him recite upon Ibn Umm Abd's recitation. This hadith, none connected it from Abu Bakr but Yahya ibn Adam; Yahya is trustworthy, from Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash; Abu Bakr was no memorizer, but scholars narrated from him and bore his hadith; he added in this hadith, for Zaidah said: from Asim from Zirr from Abdullah without saying: from Abu Bakr and Umar; the addition belongs to the adder if a memorizer; I hope the hadith is sound, for Abu Bakr and Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) then, so Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash abridged it."
"131","31 (m) - Ali ibn al-Fadl al-Karabisi narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Sad narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Ubayd ibn al-Sabbaq, from Zayd ibn Thabit, he said: Abu Bakr said to me: You used to write revelation for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so collect the Quran. He mentioned much speech in this hadith. This hadith, none narrated it with this wording but Ibrahim ibn Sad, for he said: from Ubayd ibn al-Sabbaq; Umarah ibn Ghaziyyah said: from al-Zuhri from Kharijah ibn Zayd from Zayd; we mentioned it only since Abu Bakr, may Allah have mercy on him, informed that Zayd ibn Thabit used to write revelation for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so we entered it in Abu Bakr's Musnad for fairness of its isnad and rarity of Abu Bakr's hadith."
"132","80 (m) - Amr ibn Malik narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Malik ibn Abd al-Malik narrated to me, from Musab ibn Abi Dhib, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from his father or his uncle, from Abu Bakr, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When it is the night of mid-Shaban, Allah the Blessed and Exalted descends to the lowest heaven and forgives His servants except the polytheist or the one quarreling with his brother. This hadith, we know it not from Abu Bakr but by this route; it was narrated from others than Abu Bakr, the highest narrating it from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) being Abu Bakr, though its isnad contains something, Abu Bakr's eminence renders it fair; Abd al-Malik ibn Abd al-Malik is unknown; scholars narrated this hadith, transmitted and bore it, so we mentioned it."
"133","77 (m) - Harun ibn Sufyan al-Mustamli narrated to us, he said: Usayd ibn Zayd narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Abi al-Miqdam narrated to us, he said: Imran ibn Muslim narrated to us, from Suwayd ibn Ghafalah, from Bilal, he said: My master Abu Bakr narrated to me that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: None of you should perform wudu for food he ate whose eating was lawful for him. This hadith, we know it not from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with this wording but by this route with this isnad. Amr ibn Abi al-Miqdam is Amr ibn Thabit; scholars narrated from him and reported, though he inclined to Shiism, and his hadith was not abandoned. Imran ibn Muslim, Suwayd and the rest mentioned in this hadith are famous. Usayd ibn Zayd had no harm. This wording is not narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), as we preserved from him, but by this route; we know not that Bilal connected from Abu Bakr aught but this hadith."
"134","79 (m) - Ibrahim ibn Said al-Jawhari narrated to us, he said: Husayn ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Abd al-Malik al-Fihri, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr, from his father, from his grandfather Abu Bakr, he said: I brought Abu Quhafah to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); he said: Would you have left the old man until we come to him? I said: Rather he is worthier to come to you. He said: We honor him for his son's favors to us. This hadith, we preserve it with this wording only by this route from Abu Bakr, may Allah have mercy on him. Similar words were narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by various routes with other wording. Abdullah ibn Abd al-Malik, we know not he heard from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad; Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr - Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, died while he was small. We mentioned it despite its defect since it contains wording not narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) but by this route, hence we mentioned and explained its defect."
"135","45 (m) - Al-Hasan ibn Yahya and Ahmad ibn Abdah narrated to us, they said: al-Husayn ibn al-Hasan narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr narrated to us, from Musa ibn Abi Aishah, from Hafs ibn Abi Hafs, from Abu Rafi, he said: I heard Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Gold for gold and silver for silver, like for like; the increaser and the one seeking increase are in the Fire. This hadith was narrated from Abu Bakr by another route. This isnad is fairer than the other, for Zuhayr is trustworthy, Musa ibn Abi Aishah trustworthy famous; Hafs ibn Abi Hafs was narrated from by al-Suddi and Musa ibn Abi Aishah, so obscurity lifted from him since two men narrated from him. Abu Rafi is known; this wording is preserved only from Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah have mercy on him, alone; similar words with other wording were narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by various routes, so we mentioned each hadith in its place with its wording."
"136","4 (m) - Ibrahim ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to me, from Salih ibn Kaysan, from Ibn Shihab, he said: A man of the Ansar, of the people of fiqh, not accused, narrated to me, he said: I heard him narrate to Said ibn al-Musayyab that he heard Uthman ibn Affan narrate, he said: Men, when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died, grieved over him until whispering seized some of them. Uthman said: I was among them. While I sat in the shade of a stone fort, Umar ibn al-Khattab passed and greeted but I did not notice, nor greet. Umar went to Abu Bakr and said to him: Shall I not tell you something strange: I passed Uthman, greeted, and he did not return greeting. Abu Bakr and Umar came and both greeted. Abu Bakr said: Your brother Umar claims he passed you and greeted and you did not return it. Uthman said: I said: By Allah I was unaware of your passing or greeting. Abu Bakr said: Uthman spoke truth; a matter kept you from it. He said: Yes. What is it? I said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was taken before I asked him about this affair's salvation. Abu Bakr said: I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about it. Uthman said: I said: May my father and mother be for you, inform me. Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I said, O Messenger of Allah, what is this affair's salvation? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever accepts from me the word I offered my uncle, it is salvation for him."
"137","5 (m) - Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahim and al-Fadl ibn Sahl narrated it to us, they said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Salam narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Bishr, from al-Zuhri, from Said ibn al-Musayyab, from Uthman, from Abu Bakr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similarly. The hadith is but the first; Abdullah ibn Bishr erred therein, mentioning it from al-Zuhri from Said ibn al-Musayyab; the correct is what Salih said from al-Zuhri: An Ansari man narrated to me, he said: I heard him narrate to Said ibn al-Musayyab."
"138","50 (m) - Hawtharah ibn Muhammad al-Minqari narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Israil narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Bara, he said: Abu Bakr bought from Azib a saddle for thirteen dirhams. Abu Bakr said to my father: Tell al-Bara to carry it to my saddle. He said: No, unless you narrate to us when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) left while you were with him. Abu Bakr said: We left while the polytheists sought us; we traveled our night and day until noon; my sight went seeking shade to shelter in; we found a rock's shade, so I spread a fur for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) therein, then said: Lie down, O Messenger of Allah. He lay down. Then I went to look around for any seeker; I saw a shepherd driving sheep to the rock seeking what we sought. I asked: Whose are you, boy? He said: A Qurashi man's, naming until I knew him. I said: Is there milk in your sheep? He said: Yes. I said: Will you milk for me? He said: Yes. He said: I ordered him, he held a sheep, then ordered him and he shook dust from its udder, then ordered him to shake his hands. Abu Ishaq said: al-Bara ibn Azib said: He shook one hand with the other. He milked for me a cupful of milk, and I had quenched; with me was a water-skin for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with a cloth on its mouth, so I poured over the milk until it cooled. Then I brought it to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: Drink. He drank until I was pleased. Then I said: Departure, O Messenger of Allah. We departed while they sought us; none caught us but Suraqah ibn Malik on a horse. I said: This seeker has caught us, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Grieve not, Allah is with us [the report continues in the source with the Suraqah and Madina arrival account; literally translated as it stands, full matn in no. 50]."
"139","52 (m) - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Jafar narrated to us, from Shubah, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Bara, from Abu Bakr, with that. Israil's hadith was shared in similar narration by a group, among them Zuhayr, Hudayj ibn Muawiyah and others; it is of the finest isnads narrated from Abu Bakr, may Allah have mercy on him and His pleasure."
"140","15 (m) - Ibrahim ibn Said and Ahmad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, they said: Abu Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Salam ibn Harb narrated to us, from Ata ibn al-Saib, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, he said: When 'Perish the hands of Abu Lahab' was revealed, Abu Lahab's wife came while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sat with Abu Bakr. Abu Bakr said to him: Would you move aside, she will not harm you with anything. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A barrier shall be placed between me and her. She came until she stood over Abu Bakr and said: O Abu Bakr, did your companion lampoon us? Abu Bakr said: No, by the Lord of this building, he speaks no poetry nor utters it. She said: You are truthful. When she left, Abu Bakr said: She did not see you. He said: No, an angel kept screening me until she left. This hadith, none narrates it with a fairer isnad from Abu Bakr than this; a group narrated it from Ata ibn al-Saib, all from Ata from Said mursal, except Abd al-Salam; we know none narrated it from Abd al-Salam but Abu Muhammad; we entered it in Abu Bakr's Musnad for fairness of its isnad and for his saying: He speaks no poetry nor utters it, so this passage became from Abu Bakr."
"141","3 (m) - Muhammad ibn Mamar narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Asim ibn Kulayb, he said: A shaykh narrated to me, he said: So-and-so and so-and-so narrated to me, until he counted seven, one of them Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, from Umar, he said: I heard Abu Bakr say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No prophet was ever taken until a man of his ummah leads him in prayer. This hadith, we know it not from Abu Bakr but by this route; we know not either from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by a fairer route; we know none named the man from whom Asim ibn Kulayb narrated; had we known him by another route with connected isnad fairer than this we would have produced it, though Abu Bakr, may Allah have mercy on him, is more eminent than him. End of the second juz, the end of Abu Bakr al-Siddiq's hadith, may Allah be pleased with him."
"142","Musnad of Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him. What Ibn Umar narrated from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him. Musnad of Umar ibn al-Khattab. What Ibn Umar narrated from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him. 104 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna and Muhammad ibn Bashshar narrated to us, they said: Muhammad ibn Jafar narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Qatadah, he said: I heard Said ibn al-Musayyab, from Ibn Umar, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The dead is punished by his family's weeping over him. This hadith was narrated by more than one from Qatadah from Said from Ibn Umar from Umar, so we sufficed with Shubah's hadith. Hammam narrated it from Qatadah from Said ibn al-Musayyab from Ibn Umar from Umar; Hammam also narrated it from Qatadah from Qazah and Yahya ibn Rawbah from Ibn Umar from Umar from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). None narrated this hadith from Qazah and Yahya ibn Rawbah but Hammam from Qatadah. Imran ibn Husayn, Abu Musa and others narrated it from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"143","105 - Ibrahim ibn Said and Muhammad ibn Marzuq ibn Bukayr narrated to us, they said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, he said: Ibn Shihab informed me, from Said ibn al-Musayyab, from Ibn Umar, from Umar, that it reached him that Samurah sold wine. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: May Allah curse the Jews; fats were forbidden for them to eat, so they melted them, sold them and ate their prices. This hadith is narrated from Umar by various routes. This isnad is a sound isnad; none narrated it from al-Zuhri from Said from Ibn Umar from Umar but Rawh ibn Ubadah from Ibn Jurayj."
"144","106 - Salamah ibn Shabib narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, he said: Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father, he said: Umar said: If I leave you, one better than me left you: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - meaning the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not appoint a successor; if I appoint, one better than me appointed: Abu Bakr. This hadith was narrated from Umar by various routes. This isnad is of the soundest in isnad."
"145","107 - Salamah ibn Shabib narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, he said: Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, that he asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): May one of us sleep while junub? He said: Yes, when he performs wudu for prayer. This hadith was narrated from Ibn Umar from Umar by various routes. This isnad is of the finest narrated from Umar by the route. Some companions of al-Zuhri narrated it from al-Zuhri from Salim from his father: that Umar said: O Messenger of Allah, without saying: from Umar."
"146","108 - Salama ibn Shabib narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, he said: Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father, that Umar ibn al-Khattab, while he was delivering the sermon, Uthman ibn Affan entered, and that was on Friday, so Umar said to him: At such a time as this? He said: It was nothing but that I heard the call (adhan) so I performed ablution then came. He said: And ablution as well? You indeed knew that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to command us to perform ghusl."
"147","109 - Ubaydullah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyayna narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father, from Umar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) caught up with him while he was swearing by his father, and he was among a caravan, so he said: Do not swear by your fathers. Umar said: So I never returned to it."
"148","110 - Umar ibn al-Khattab narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Yaman al-Hakam ibn Nafi narrated to us, he said: Shuayb ibn Abi Hamza informed me, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father, from Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whatever wealth Allah brings to you without asking and without the soul aspiring to it, it is but provision that Allah has driven to you."
"149","111 - Salama ibn Shabib and Harun ibn Ishaq narrated to us, they said: Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, he said: Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father - Harun said: from Umar - that he had given a horse in the path of Allah, then he saw it being sold afterwards, or its owner had neglected it, so he asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he did not permit him to take back his charity. And this hadith, some of them narrated it from Salim, from his father: that Umar gave a horse in the path of Allah."
"150","112 - al-Hasan ibn Arafa narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Husayn narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father, from Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever sells a slave who has wealth, his wealth belongs to the seller, unless the buyer stipulates; and whoever sells date-palms that have been pollinated, their fruit belongs to the seller unless the buyer stipulates. And this hadith - we do not know anyone who said in it: from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father, from Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), except Sufyan ibn Husayn, and he erred in it; the memorizers narrate it from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and that is the correct version."
"151","113 - Muhammad ibn Ismail ibn Samura narrated to us, he said: Waki narrated to us, from Salih ibn Abi al-Akhdar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father: that a man of Thaqif divorced his wives and freed his slaves, so Umar said to him: You shall surely take back your wealth and your wives, otherwise if you die I shall surely stone your grave just as the Messenger of Allah stoned the grave of Abu Righal. And this hadith, the memorizers narrate it: otherwise if you die I shall stone your grave just as the grave of Abu Righal is stoned. And none narrated it with isnad except Salih ibn Abi al-Akhdar, and he narrated it with isnad, and Salih is not strong in hadith."
"152","114 - And Umar ibn al-Khattab al-Sijistani narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Ghaffar ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Razzaq ibn Umar al-Ayli narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, from Umar, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Every nation has a trustworthy one, and the trustworthy one of this nation is Abu Ubayda ibn al-Jarrah. And this hadith - we do not know anyone who narrated it from al-Zuhri except Abd al-Razzaq ibn Umar, and he is a man from whom more than one narrated: Yahya ibn Hassan, Abd al-Ghaffar ibn Dawud and others; and we do not know anyone who corroborated him in narrating this hadith from al-Zuhri, although Umar ibn Hamza did narrate it - Salim from his father, from Umar."
"153","115 - And Salama ibn Shabib narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, he said: Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father."
"154","116 - And Umar ibn al-Khattab narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Yaman narrated to us, he said: Shuayb narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, he said: Salim ibn Abdullah narrated to me that he heard Abdullah, meaning Ibn Umar, that Umar ibn al-Khattab said: When Hafsa became widowed from Khunays ibn Hudhafa al-Sahmi, and he was among the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he had witnessed Badr with him, then died in Madina - Umar said: So I met Uthman ibn Affan and offered him Hafsa, saying: If you wish I will marry Hafsa bint Umar to you. He said: I shall consider my affair. So I waited some nights, then he met me and said: I shall not marry these days of mine. Then I met Abu Bakr and said to him: If you wish I will marry Hafsa bint Umar to you. So Abu Bakr fell silent and did not return anything to me, and I was more aggrieved with him than I was with Uthman. So I waited some nights, then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) proposed her to me, so I married her to him. Then Abu Bakr met me and said: Perhaps you resented me when you offered me Hafsa and I did not return anything to you. I said: Yes. He said: Nothing prevented me from responding to what you offered me except that I knew that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had mentioned Hafsa, and I would not disclose the secret of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); and had he left her, I would have accepted her."
"155","And from the hadith of Umar ibn Hamza from Salim. 117 - Muhammad ibn Salih al-Adawi narrated to us, he said: Abu Usama narrated to us, from Umar ibn Hamza, from Salim, from his father, from Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Every nation has a trustworthy one, and the trustworthy one of this nation is Abu Ubayda ibn al-Jarrah. And this hadith - we do not know anyone who narrated it from Umar ibn Hamza except Abu Usama."
"156","118 - Bishr ibn Khalid al-Askari narrated to us, he said: Abu Usama narrated to us, from Umar ibn Hamza, from Salim, from his father, from Umar, he said: I saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in a dream, and I saw him not looking at me, so I said: O Messenger of Allah, what is the matter with me? He said: Are you not the one who kisses while fasting? So I said: By the One in whose Hand is the soul of Umar, I shall never kiss while fasting ever. And this hadith - we do not know it narrated from Umar with this wording except from this angle, and there has been narrated from Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), contrary to this narration."
"157","And from what Asim ibn Ubaydullah narrated from Salim. 119 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna, Muhammad ibn Bashshar and al-Walid ibn Sufyan narrated to us, they said: Muhammad ibn Jafar narrated to us, he said: Shuba narrated to us, from Asim ibn Ubaydullah, from Salim ibn Abdullah ibn Umar, from his father, from Umar, that he sought permission from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for Umra, so he permitted him and said: Do not forget us in your supplication, O my brother. And this hadith - we do not know it narrated with this wording except from Umar with this isnad; and Shuba and al-Thawri narrated it from Asim ibn Ubaydullah."
"158","120 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Muammal narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Asim, from Salim, from his father, from Umar, that he sought permission from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the like of it; and others besides Muammal narrated it and did not say: from Umar."
"159","121 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna and Muhammad ibn Bashshar narrated to us, they said: Muhammad ibn Jafar narrated to us, he said: Shuba narrated to us, from Asim ibn Ubaydullah, from Salim, from his father, from Umar, that he said to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Have you seen what we do - is it in a matter already concluded or in a matter newly begun? He said: Rather in a matter already concluded. He said: Then why not rely? He said: Act, O son of al-Khattab, for each is facilitated to that for which he was created; as for whoever is among the people of felicity, he acts with the deeds of the people of felicity, and as for whoever is among the people of wretchedness, he acts with the deeds of the people of wretchedness. And this hadith has been narrated from Umar from more than one angle."
"160","122 - Salih ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn Salih narrated to us, from Asim ibn Ubaydullah, from Salim, from his father, from Umar, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wiped over his leather socks. Thus al-Hasan ibn Salih narrated it from Asim ibn Ubaydullah, and it has been narrated from Asim with other than this isnad."
"161","And from what Salih ibn Muhammad ibn Zaida narrated from Salim from his father. 123 - Yusuf ibn Salman narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Salih ibn Muhammad ibn Zaida, from Salim, from his father, from Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whomever you find guilty of misappropriation (ghulul), burn his goods. And this hadith - we do not know it narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except from this angle with this isnad; and Salih ibn Muhammad, he is Ibn Zaida - Hatim ibn Ismail, Wuhayb ibn Khalid and al-Darawardi narrated from him. And this hadith - we do not know anyone who narrated it from Salih except al-Darawardi; and Salih did not narrate from Salim, from his father, from Umar, except this hadith."
"162","And from what Amr ibn Dinar, steward of the house of al-Zubayr, narrated from Salim from his father from Umar. 124 - Bishr ibn Muadh al-Aqadi, Muhammad ibn Abd al-Malik and Abd al-Wahid ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, they said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, steward of the house of al-Zubayr, from Salim, from his father, from Umar: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever sees an afflicted one and says: Praise be to Allah who has spared me from what He has afflicted you with, and favored me over many of those He created with great favor, Allah will spare him from that affliction, whatever it may be, forever as long as he lives."
"163","125 - Ahmad ibn Abda, Muhammad ibn Abd al-Malik and Abd al-Wahid ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, they said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Salim, from his father, from Umar, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever enters one of the markets and says: There is no god but Allah alone, He has no partner; to Him belongs dominion and to Him belongs praise, and He is over all things Powerful - Allah records for him a million good deeds, erases from him a million evil deeds, and builds for him a house in Paradise. And these two hadiths were narrated by Amr ibn Dinar, steward of the house of al-Zubayr, and he is their freedman, his kunya is Abu Yahya; Hammad ibn Zayd, Hammad ibn Salama, Abd al-Warith, Kharija ibn Musab, Said ibn Zayd and others narrated from him, and he was not corroborated in them."
"164","126 - Abd al-Wahid ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar al-Awar - and he is the steward of the house of al-Zubayr - from Salim, from his father, from Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The angels do not accompany a company in which there is a bell. And this hadith - we do not know it narrated from Umar except from this angle; Hammad ibn Salama was alone in it, and in it there is a defect: Salim narrated it from Abu al-Jarrah, from Umm Habiba."
"165","127 - al-Fadl ibn Sahl and Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahim narrated to us, they said: al-Hasan ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Salim, from his father, from Umar, he said: Prices rose in Madina and hardship became severe, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Be patient and receive glad tidings, for I have blessed your sa and your mudd for you; so eat together and do not separate, for the food of one suffices two, and the food of two suffices four, and the food of four suffices five and six; and indeed blessing is in the congregation. Whoever endures its distress and hardship, I shall be for him an intercessor or a witness on the Day of Resurrection; and whoever leaves it out of aversion to what is in it, Allah will replace him therein with one better than him; and whoever intends it with evil, Allah will melt him as salt melts in water. And this hadith is not narrated from Umar ibn al-Khattab except from this angle; Amr ibn Dinar was alone in it, and he is lenient in hadith, although a group narrated from him, and most of his hadiths are not shared with him by others."
"166","And from what Khalid ibn Abi Bakr narrated from Salim. 128 - Salama ibn Shabib and Bishr ibn Adam narrated to us, they said: Zayd ibn al-Hubab narrated to us, from Khalid ibn Abi Bakr, from Salim, from his father, from Umar, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The traveler wipes over the leather socks for three days and their nights, and the resident for a day and a night. And this hadith was not narrated from Umar concerning the time limit except from this angle; and a group narrated it from Umar - Abdullah ibn Umar, Ubaydullah ibn Umar and others - and they did not mention in it a time limit. And Khalid ibn Abi Bakr is lenient in hadith, and more than one of the people of knowledge narrated from him."
"167","Hanzala from Salim from his father from Umar. 129 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Isa narrated to us, he said: Hanzala narrated to us, from Salim, from his father, from Umar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he raised his hands in supplication, would not return them until he wiped his face with them. And this hadith was only narrated from Hanzala by Hammad ibn Isa, and he is lenient in hadith; his hadith was only declared weak because of this hadith, and we found no alternative to bringing it out since it is not narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except from this angle or from an angle weaker than it."
"168","130 - Yahya ibn Habib and al-Jarrah ibn Makhlad narrated to us, they said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Hanzala narrated to us, from Salim, from his father, from Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Only one who has no share in the Hereafter wears silk."
"169","131 - And al-Hasan ibn Yahya and Abd al-Quddus ibn Muhammad narrated to us, they said: Mualla ibn Asad narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Umar. 132 - And from Ayyub, from Abu Qilaba, from Umar, that he asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Does one of us sleep while he is in major impurity? He said: When he performs ablution as for prayer. And this hadith - we do not know anyone who narrated it from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Umar, except Wuhayb; and from Wuhayb except Mualla."
"170","133 - Zuhayr ibn Muhammad ibn Qumayr narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Umar, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) heard me while I was saying: by my father, so he said: Indeed Allah forbids you to swear by your fathers and by the taghuts."
"171","134 - And Zuhayr narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, from Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father, from Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with the like of it."
"172","What Umar ibn Muhammad narrated from Salim from his father from Umar. 135 - al-Fadl ibn Sahl narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Aban narrated to us, he said: Mansur ibn Dinar narrated to us, from Umar ibn Muhammad, from Salim ibn Abdullah, from Ibn Umar, he said: Umar ascended the pulpit, praised Allah and extolled Him, then said: What is the matter with people marrying this muta, when the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade it - I think he said: from it - I shall not be brought anyone who contracted it except that I shall stone him with stones."
"173","136 - And Yahya ibn Habib ibn Arabi and al-Jarrah ibn Makhlad narrated to us - the wording is al-Jarrah's - they said: Rawh ibn Ubada narrated to us, he said: Shuba narrated to us, from Abu Bakr ibn Hafs, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, from Umar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Only one who has no share wears silk. And this hadith - we do not know anyone who narrated it from Shuba connected except Rawh ibn Ubada."
"174","137 - al-Fadl ibn Sahl narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Zufar narrated to us, from Safwan ibn Abi al-Sahba - thus he said - from Salim, from his father, from Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Allah Almighty says: When My servant's remembrance of Me occupies him from asking Me, I give him better than what I give those who ask."
"175","Nafi from Ibn Umar from Umar. 138 - Imran ibn Musa al-Qazzaz narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sawa narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Abi Aruba narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Umar, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wiped over the leather socks. And this hadith was narrated by Ibn Abi Aruba from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Umar. And Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr - a shaykh of the people of Basra - narrated it from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Umar. And Ibn Abi Aruba from Ayyub from Nafi is the better chain, so for that we mentioned it."
"176","139 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Hafs ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, that Umar said to the Stone: I kiss you and I know that you are a stone, and had I not seen the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) kissing you, I would not have kissed you. And this hadith - we do not know anyone who narrated it from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Umar, except Hammad ibn Zayd."
"177","Ubaydullah ibn Umar from Nafi from Ibn Umar from Umar. 140 - Yusuf ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Mihran ibn Abi Umar narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Umar, that he had vowed itikaf of a night in the Jahiliyya. He said: So when I became Muslim I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: Fulfill your vow."
"178","141 - And Abdullah ibn Said narrated it to us, he said: Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Umar."
"179","142 - Amr ibn Ali narrated it to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Budayl, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar, from Umar, that he had a vow upon him, then he mentioned the like of it."
"180","143 - And Ibrahim ibn Said narrated it to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Numayr narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Umar - all of them: that he had upon him a vow of itikaf of a night in the Jahiliyya, and when he became Muslim he asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: Fulfill your vow. And this hadith was narrated by more than one from Ubaydullah, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Umar; and the highest who narrated it from Ubaydullah is Sufyan al-Thawri."
"181","144 - Ibrahim ibn Said narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Numayr narrated to us, from Ubaydullah, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Umar, that he saw a striped silk garment being sold, so he came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: If only you would buy it for the delegations that come to you. He said: Only one who has no share wears silk. And this hadith has been narrated by more than one from Ubaydullah, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar: that Umar saw a garment; and none said: from Umar, except Ibn Numayr."
"182","145 - Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said narrated to us, from Ubaydullah, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Umar."
"183","146 - And Abda ibn Abdullah narrated it to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Bishr informed me, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Umar narrated to us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Umar, he said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The dead is punished for what is lamented over him, or what is wept over him."
"184","147 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said narrated to us, from Ubaydullah, he said: Nafi informed me, from Ibn Umar, from Umar, that he asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: Major impurity befalls me in the night. So he commanded him to perform ablution as for prayer."
"185","148 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Ubaydullah al-Umayri narrated to us, he said: Mubarak ibn Fadala narrated to us, from Ubaydullah, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Umar: that he said: Suspect opinion in matters of religion; I saw myself on the day of Abu Jandal rejecting the command of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by my opinion, and I did not fall short of the truth. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was writing between himself and the people of Makka, saying: Write: In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate. They said: Had we seen that, we would have believed in what you say; but write as we write: In Your name, O Allah. He said: So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) agreed, but I refused, until he said to me: O Umar, do you see that I have agreed while you refuse? He said: So I agreed. And this hadith - we do not know it narrated from Umar except from this angle; none shared with Mubarak in narrating it from Ubaydullah in this hadith, and others besides Umar narrated it."
"186","149 - Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, he said: Yahya narrated to us, from Ubaydullah, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Umar, he said: I have not urinated standing since I became Muslim."
"187","150 - Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Salt narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Ubaydullah, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Umar, that he favored Usama in giving, so I said: Do you favor him over me? He said: He was more beloved to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) than you. And this hadith was narrated by Ubaydullah, and we do not know anyone who narrated it from Ubaydullah except al-Darawardi; and others besides Ubaydullah also narrated it from Nafi."
"188","151 - Muhammad ibn Abd al-Malik al-Wasiti narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Muslim narrated to us, from Ubaydullah, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Do not prevent women from the mosques. Thus Ismail ibn Muslim and Bishr ibn Mansur narrated it from Ubaydullah, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And Yahya al-Qattan and others among the memorizers narrated it from Ubaydullah, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"189","152 - al-Fadl ibn Sahl narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz al-Qurashi narrated to us, he said: Sharik ibn Abdullah narrated to us, from Ubaydullah, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Umar, he said: I saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wiping over the leather socks. And this hadith - we do not know Sharik narrated it from Ubaydullah except Abd al-Aziz; and Abd al-Aziz is lenient in hadith."
"190","153 - Rizqullah ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Shababa narrated to us, from Mubarak ibn Fadala, from Ubaydullah, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, that Umar said: If I appoint a successor, then one better than me appointed a successor - Abu Bakr; and if I leave you, then one better than me left you - the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); and the affair is among these six with whom the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died while he was pleased with them."
"191","And from what Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated from Nafi from Ibn Umar from Umar. 154 - Ibrahim ibn Said al-Jawhari narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said al-Umawi narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Umar, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stipulated upon the Jews of Khaybar: When we wish, we expel them. And this hadith - we do not know it narrated from Umar except from this angle."
"192","155 - Zuhayr ibn Muhammad ibn Qumayr narrated to us, he said: Sadaqa ibn Sabiq informed me, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, he said: Nafi narrated to me, from Ibn Umar, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, he said: When we gathered for the Hijra, I agreed with Ayyash ibn Abi Rabia and Hisham ibn al-Asi at Maydaa, the Maydaa of Banu Ghifar above Sarif, and we said: Whichever of you does not appear with it in the morning has been detained, so his two companions shall proceed. So Hisham ibn al-Asi was detained from us. When we arrived in Madina and settled among Banu Amr ibn Awf at Quba, Abu Jahl ibn Hisham and al-Harith ibn Hisham went out to Ayyash ibn Abi Rabia - and he was their cousin and their maternal brother - until they came to us in Madina and spoke to him, saying to him: Your mother has vowed that she will not touch her head with a comb until she sees you. So he softened toward her. I said to him: O Ayyash, by Allah, the people intend by you nothing but to turn you from your religion, so beware of them; by Allah, if lice were to harm your mother she would have combed, and if the heat of Makka were to become severe upon her she would have sought shade. He said: I have wealth there, so I shall take it. He said: I said: By Allah, you know that I am among the wealthiest of Quraysh, so half my wealth is yours; do not go with them. He said: But he refused me except to go out with them. So I said to him, when he refused me: Since you have done what you have done, take this she-camel of mine, for she is a docile she-camel; stay on her back, and if you sense any suspicion from the people, escape upon her. So he went out with them upon her until, when they were on part of the road, Abu Jahl ibn Hisham said: By Allah, this camel of mine has become slow; will you not carry me upon this she-camel of yours? He said: Yes. So he made her kneel, and they made theirs kneel to transfer onto her; and when they were level with the ground they attacked him and bound him, then brought him into Makka and put him to trial, and he was tried. He said: And we used to say: By Allah, Allah does not accept from one who is tried any compensation nor equivalent, and the repentance of a people who knew Allah then returned to disbelief because of a trial that befell them is not accepted. He said: And they used to say that to themselves. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) arrived in Madina, Allah revealed concerning them and concerning our saying to them and their saying to themselves: Say: O My servants who have transgressed against themselves, do not despair of the mercy of Allah; indeed Allah forgives all sins - up to His saying: while you perceive not. Umar said: So I wrote it on a sheet and sent it to Hisham ibn al-Asi. Hisham said: I kept reading it at Dhu Tuwa, ascending with it therein until I understood it. He said: So it was cast into my soul that it was only revealed concerning us, and concerning what we used to say to ourselves, and it is said: concerning us. So I returned and sat upon my camel and joined the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in Madina. And this hadith - we do not know anyone who narrated it from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except Umar; and we do not know it narrated from Umar connected except from this angle with this isnad."
"193","156 - Abdullah ibn Said narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Idris narrated to us, from Ibn Ishaq, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: When Umar became Muslim he said: Who is the most talkative of the people? They said: So-and-so. He said: So he came to him and said: I have become Muslim, so do not inform anyone. He said: Then he went out dragging his lower garment, with its edge upon his shoulder, saying: Know that Umar has apostatized. He said: And I say: You lie, but I have become Muslim; and upon him was a shirt. So a crowd of Quraysh rose against him and fought him, and he fought them until he fell and they crowded upon him. Then a man upon whom was a shirt came and said: What is the matter with you and the man? Do you think Banu Adi ibn Kab will leave you and their companion - you kill a man who chose for himself following Muhammad? He said: So the people uncovered from him. I said to my father: Who is the man? He said: al-As ibn Wail al-Sahmi."
"194","What Abdullah ibn Umar narrated from Nafi from Ibn Umar. 157 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Salama narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Umar narrated to us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Umar, he said: Had I not heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: I wish to enlarge your qibla, I would not have enlarged it. And this hadith - we do not know it narrated from Umar except from this angle; and we do not know anyone who narrated it except al-Umari from Nafi."
"195","158 - Yusuf ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: al-Fadl ibn Dukayn narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Umar narrated to us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Umar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not give any of his wives a dower more than twelve uqiyyas. And this hadith - we do not know anyone who narrated it from al-Umari except al-Fadl ibn Dukayn; and we do not know it narrated from Ibn Umar, from Umar, except from this angle."
"196","159 - Muhammad ibn Isa narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Muhammad al-Farwi narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Umar narrated to us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded us to give charity, and that coincided with wealth I had, so I said: Today I shall surpass Abu Bakr, if I ever surpass him on a day. So I came with half my wealth, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What have you left for your family? I said: The like of it. And Abu Bakr came with all the wealth he had, so he said: O Abu Bakr, what have you left for your family? He said: Allah and His Messenger. I said: I shall never compete with you in anything ever. And this hadith - we do not know it narrated from Ibn Umar, from Umar, except from this angle; and Zayd ibn Aslam narrated it from his father, from Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"197","160 - Muhammad ibn Isa narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Muhammad al-Farwi narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Umar narrated to us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, that Ibn Abbas asked Umar about the two who had joined against the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: That was Aisha and Hafsa. And this hadith - we do not know anyone who narrated it from al-Umari except Ishaq ibn Muhammad; and we do not know it narrated from Ibn Umar, from Umar, except from this angle."
"198","161 - Ibrahim ibn Hani narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Umar, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Seven places in which there is no prayer: the back of the House of Allah, the graveyard, the refuse heap, the slaughterhouse, the bathhouse, the camel resting-place, and the middle of the road. And this hadith - we do not know it narrated from Ibn Umar, from Umar, except from this angle; and we do not know anyone who narrated it except al-Layth, from Abdullah ibn Umar."
"199","162 - And Ibrahim ibn Ziyad al-Saigh narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Umar narrated to us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Whoever buys food, let him not sell it until he takes full possession of it."
"200","163 - He said: And Yunus ibn Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Umar narrated to us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When they are three, two shall not converse privately excluding their companion. And these two hadiths are only narrated by the trustworthy memorizers from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And we do not know anyone who said: from Ibn Umar, from Umar, except Abdullah ibn Umar al-Umari, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Umar; and he was not corroborated in it."
"201","164 - Ismail ibn Masud narrated to us, he said: Fudayl ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Uqba narrated to us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Umar: that he asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about major impurity. He said: Perform ablution as for prayer."
"202","165 - al-Husayn ibn Mahdi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, he said: Mamar informed us, from Abd al-Karim, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Umar, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw me while I was urinating standing, so he said: Mah. Umar said: So I never returned to it afterwards. And this hadith was narrated by Abd al-Razzaq from Ibn Jurayj, from Abd al-Karim, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Umar; and from Mamar, from Abd al-Karim, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Umar."
"203","What Abdullah ibn Dinar narrated from Ibn Umar from Umar. 166 - Muhammad ibn al-Walid al-Fahham and al-Hasan ibn Arafa narrated to us, they said: al-Nadr ibn Ismail narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Suqa narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar, from Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood among us as I stand among you and said: Be good to my companions, then those who follow them, then those who follow them. Whoever loves the middle of Paradise, let him adhere to the congregation; and whomever his good deed pleases and his evil deed grieves, he is a believer. And this hadith has been narrated by more than one from Abdullah ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar, from Umar; and we do not know Ibn Suqa narrated with isnad from Abdullah ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar, except this hadith."
"204","167 - Bishr ibn Muadh narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Jafar ibn Nujayh narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Dinar narrated to us, from Ibn Umar, from Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Let no man be alone with a woman, for Satan is the third of them; and whomever his good deed pleases and his evil deed grieves, he is a believer. And this hadith has been narrated by more than one from Abdullah ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar, from Umar."
"205","168 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us, from Sulayman ibn Sufyan, from Abdullah ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar, from Umar, he said: When: Among them are wretched and felicitous, was revealed, they said: O Messenger of Allah, have you seen what we do - is it something already concluded or something we newly begin? He said: Rather something already concluded. They said: Then why the deeds? He said: Each is facilitated to that for which he was created."
"206","And from what Yahya ibn Yamar narrated from Ibn Umar from Umar. 169 - Yusuf ibn Wadih narrated to us, he said: al-Mutamir ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from his father, from Yahya ibn Yamar, from Ibn Umar, from Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): that a man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and asked him: What is Islam? He said: You bear witness that there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad is His servant and His Messenger, establish prayer, give zakat, fast Ramadan, and perform Hajj to the House. He said: If I do that, have I submitted? He said: Yes. He said: You have spoken the truth, O Messenger of Allah. Then what is faith? He said: You believe in Allah, the Last Day, resurrection after death, Paradise and Hell, and the Decree, all of it, its good and its evil. He said: If I do that, am I a believer? He said: Yes. He said: You have spoken the truth. Thus he narrated it to us, or its meaning, or close to it."
"207","170 - Ahmad ibn Abda narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd informed me, from Matar al-Warraq, from Ibn Burayda, from Yahya ibn Yamar, from Ibn Umar, from Umar, that a man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, may I come near you? He said: Yes. He said: So he came near until he placed his hand upon his knees and said: What is Islam? He said: You bear witness that there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad is His servant and His Messenger, establish prayer, give zakat, fast Ramadan, and perform Hajj to the House. He said: If I do that, have I submitted? He said: Yes. He said: You have spoken the truth, O Messenger of Allah. Then what is faith? He said: You believe in Allah and the Last Day, resurrection after death, Paradise and Hell, and the Decree, all of it, its good and its evil. He said: If I do that, am I a believer? He said: Yes. He said: You have spoken the truth. He said: Then what is ihsan? He said: That you fear Allah as though you see Him, for if you do not see Him, He sees you. He said: If I do that, have I done well? He said: Yes. He said: You have spoken the truth. He said: So he affirmed him, and the man turned and left, so he said: Bring him to me. So he was looked for but not found. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: This is Gabriel, upon him be peace - he came to teach you your religion."
"208","171 - Ahmad ibn Abda narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Matar al-Warraq, from Ibn Burayda, from Yahya ibn Yamar, from Ibn Umar, from Umar, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): that Musa met Adam and said: O Adam, you whom Allah created with His Hand and made His angels prostrate to you, and settled you in Paradise - had it not been for what you did, many of your offspring would have entered Paradise. He said: O Musa, you whom Allah chose with His messengership and His speech, then you blame me for something that had been decreed upon me before He created me? So they argued before Allah, and Adam overcame Musa."
"209","172 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated it to us, he said: Abd al-Malik ibn al-Sabbah narrated to us, he said: Imran, meaning Ibn Hudayr, narrated to us, from al-Rudayni, meaning Ibn Abi Mijlaz, from Yahya ibn Yamar, from Ibn Umar, from Umar, raised: Adam and Musa argued, then he mentioned the like of the hadith of Matar, from Ibn Burayda, from Yahya ibn Yamar, from Ibn Umar, from Umar."
"210","173 - And Yusuf ibn Wadih narrated it to us, he said: al-Mutamir narrated to us, from his father, from Yahya ibn Yamar, from Ibn Umar, from Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And this hadith was narrated by Kahmas from Abdullah ibn Burayda, from Ibn Umar, from Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"211","And from the hadith of Ibn Aqil from Ibn Umar from Umar. 174 - Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, he said: Badal ibn al-Muhabbar Abu al-Munir narrated to us, he said: Zaida narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil, from Ibn Umar, from Umar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded that it be announced among the people that whoever bears witness that there is no god but Allah shall enter Paradise. So Umar said: Then they will rely. He said: Leave them to rely. And we do not know Ibn Aqil narrated from Ibn Umar except this hadith. And Zaida alone narrated it from him; and Hasan ibn Ali narrated from Zaida, from Ibn Aqil, from Jabir, differing from Badal in his narration."
"212","And from what Asim ibn Muhammad ibn Zayd narrated from his father from Ibn Umar from Umar. 175 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Asim ibn Muhammad ibn Zayd informed me, from his father, from Ibn Umar, from Umar - and I know it only as raised - he said: Allah Almighty said: Whoever humbles himself for Me thus - and he gestured with the inside of his palms toward the earth - I raise him thus - and he gestured with the inside of his palms toward the heaven. And this hadith - we do not know it narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with this wording except from Umar with this isnad; and there is not with this isnad from Umar except this hadith."
"213","And what Zayd al-Ammi narrated from Abu al-Siddiq al-Naji from Ibn Umar from Umar. 176 - Ahmad ibn Uthman ibn Hakim and al-Abbas ibn Jafar narrated to us, they said: Malik ibn Ismail narrated to us, he said: Masud ibn Sad al-Jufi narrated to us, from Mutarrif, from Zayd al-Ammi, from Abu al-Siddiq al-Naji, from Ibn Umar, from Umar, he said: I mentioned the women of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - what they trail of garments. He said: A span. They said: A span is little; the private parts show from it. He said: Then a cubit. They said: Their feet show. He said: A cubit; they shall not exceed that. And this hadith - we do not know it narrated from Umar except from this angle with this isnad; and there has been disagreement from Ibn Umar, but thus Mutarrif narrated it from Zayd al-Ammi."
"214","al-Shabi from Ibn Umar from Umar. 177 - Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said al-Qattan narrated to us, from Abu Hayyan, from al-Shabi, from Ibn Umar, from Umar, that he stood as a preacher upon the pulpit of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), praised Allah and extolled Him, then said: To proceed: the prohibition of wine came down on the day it came down, and it is from five: grapes, dates, honey, wheat and barley; and wine is whatever veils the mind. O people, I wished that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had not left us until he had entrusted to us concerning it an entrustment we would adhere to: the grandfather, the kalala, and doors of the doors of usury. I said: What do you think about the sixth made from rice - the ignorant is called and a man drinks a draught of it and it fells him; made from rice. He said: This did not exist in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); had it existed he would have forbidden it. Do you not see that he generalized all drinks? He said: Wine is whatever veils the mind. And this speech - we do not know anyone who narrated it from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except Umar, and its isnad is sound."
"215","Atiyya al-Awfi from Ibn Umar from Umar. 178 - Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Abd al-Majid narrated to us, he said: Fudayl ibn Marzuq narrated to us, from Atiyya, from Ibn Umar, that al-Zubayr sought permission from Umar for jihad, so he said: Sit; you have already fought with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"216","And from what the elders narrated from Ibn Umar from Umar. 179 - Ali ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Asim narrated to us, he said: Yahya al-Bakka narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Umar narrated to me, he said: I heard Umar ibn al-Khattab say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Four rakats before Zuhr after the sun's decline equal the prayer of the pre-dawn. And this hadith - we do not know it narrated from Umar except from this angle with this isnad; and Yahya al-Bakka - more than one narrated from him, and he is not a memorizer."
"217","180 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Imran ibn Hittan, he said: I asked Aisha about silk, so she said: Go to Ibn Abbas and ask him. So I went to Ibn Abbas and asked him, so he said: Ask Ibn Umar. So I asked him, so he said: Hafsa bint Umar narrated to me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Only one who has no share wears silk. 181 - Ibn Umar said: And Abu Hafs, meaning Umar ibn al-Khattab, narrated to me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Only one who has no share in the Hereafter wears silk. So I said: He did not lie against Umar, and Abu Hafs did not lie against the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And this hadith has been narrated from Umar from more than one angle."
"218","182 - Ibrahim ibn al-Junayd narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahim ibn Mutarrif narrated to us, he said: Isa ibn Yunus narrated to me, from Ibrahim ibn Yazid, from Muhammad ibn Abbad ibn Jafar, from Ibn Umar, he said: We came with Umar until, when we were at Dhu al-Hulayfa, he entered ihram and we entered ihram. Then a rider passed by us with the scent of perfume wafting from him, so Umar said: Who is this? They said: Muawiya. He said: What is this, O Muawiya? He said: I passed by Umm Habiba bint Abi Sufyan and she did this to me. He said: Return and wash it off yourself, for I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The pilgrim is disheveled, dusty. And this hadith - we do not know it narrated except from Umar; and we do not know for it an isnad from Umar except this isnad; and Ibrahim ibn Yazid is not strong, though al-Thawri and many others narrated from him."
"219","183 - Umar ibn al-Khattab narrated to us, he said: al-Faryabi narrated to us, he said: Aban ibn Abi Hazim narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Hafs narrated to me, from Ibn Umar, from Umar, he said: When Umar took charge, he praised Allah and extolled Him, then said: O people, the Messenger of Allah permitted muta for us, then forbade it to us. And this hadith - we do not know for it an isnad from Umar better than this isnad."
"220","Abdullah ibn Abbas from Umar. 184 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abi Adi narrated to us, from Said ibn Abi Aruba, from Qatada, from Abu al-Aliya, from Ibn Abbas."
"221","185 - And al-Hasan ibn Arafa narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Mansur ibn Zadhdhan informed us, from Qatada, from Abu al-Aliya, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Trustworthy men bore witness before me, among them Umar - and the most pleasing of them to me was Umar - that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade prayer after Fajr until the sun rises, and after Asr until the sun sets. And this hadith was narrated from Qatada by Said, Shuba, Hisham, Hammam, Aban and Mansur ibn Zadhdhan - all of them mentioned it, so we sufficed with those we mentioned. And the prohibition of prayer after Asr and after Fajr has been narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by many."
"222","186 - Abd al-Warith ibn Abd al-Samad ibn Abd al-Warith narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to me, he said: Hushaym and Hammam narrated to us, from Qatada, from Abu al-Aliya, from Ibn Abbas, from Umar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade fasting on the day of Fitr and the day of Sacrifice. And we do not know this hadith narrated from Ibn Abbas, from Umar, except from this angle with this isnad; and it has been narrated from Ali, Abu Said, Abu Hurayra, Anas and others: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade fasting on the day of Fitr and the day of Sacrifice."
"223","187 - Yahya ibn Muhammad ibn al-Sakan narrated to us, he said: Habban ibn Hilal narrated to us - and he dictated it to us from his book - from Hammam, from Qatada, from Abu al-Aliya, from Ibn Abbas, from Umar, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Journeys are not undertaken except to three mosques: the Sacred Mosque, this mosque of mine, and al-Aqsa Mosque. And this hadith - we do not know it narrated from Umar except from this angle, from this isnad; and it is an error whose error came from Habban, because this hadith is only narrated by Hammam and others from Qatada, from Qazaa, from Abu Said."
"224","188 - Ahmad ibn al-Mualla al-Adami and al-Jarrah ibn Makhlad narrated to us, they said: Khalid ibn Yazid ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: al-Bara ibn Yazid al-Ghanawi narrated to us, from al-Hasan ibn Abi al-Hasan, he said: Abu al-Aliya al-Riyahi narrated to me, he said: Ibn Abbas narrated to me, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade prayer after Fajr until the sun rises or shines, and after Asr until the sun sets. And he said: You shall surely command the good and forbid the evil, or Allah shall surely empower your wicked ones over you, then your best ones will supplicate and it will not be answered for them. And this hadith - Qatada narrated part of it from Abu al-Aliya, from Ibn Abbas, from Umar, namely that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade prayer after Fajr and after Asr. As for: you shall surely command the good and forbid the evil, none narrated it except al-Bara from al-Hasan, from Abu al-Aliya, from Ibn Abbas, from Umar; and we do not know al-Hasan narrated with isnad from Abu al-Aliya any hadith except this hadith, and none narrated it from him except Khalid ibn Yazid; and Abu al-Aliya did not narrate from Ibn Abbas, from Umar, except this hadith. And al-Bara ibn Yazid is not strong, though his hadith was tolerated, and a group narrated from him."
"225","And from what Said ibn Jubayr narrated from Ibn Abbas from Umar. 189 - Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Mutawakkil narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Zakariyya ibn Abi Zaida narrated to us, from Salih ibn Salih, from Salama ibn Kuhayl, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, from Umar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) divorced Hafsa then took her back. And this hadith - we do not know anyone who narrated it from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, except Salama; nor from Salama except Salih ibn Salih. And it has been narrated from Ammar ibn Yasir and from Anas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"226","190 - Muhammad ibn Uthman ibn Karama and al-Fadl ibn Sahl narrated to us, they said: Ubaydullah ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Shayban ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Habib, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, from Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said: Allah cursed the Jews; fats were forbidden to them, so they rendered them, sold them and ate their price. And this hadith - we do not know anyone who narrated it from al-Amash, from Habib, except Ubaydullah ibn Musa, from Shayban."
"227","191 - Bishr ibn Muadh al-Aqadi narrated to us, he said: Fudayl ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Uthman ibn Khuthaym, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, from Umar, that he said to the Stone: I kiss you and I know that you are a stone; had I not seen the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) kissing you, I would not have kissed you. And this hadith - we do not know anyone who narrated it from Ibn Khuthaym, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, from Umar, except Fudayl ibn Sulayman."
"228","192 - Ziyad ibn Ayyub narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Abu Bishr, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Umar used to admit the people of Badr and admit me with them, so some of them said: Do you admit this youth with us while among our sons are those like him? So Umar said: He is among those who know. So he admitted them one day and admitted me with them, then asked them about this sura: When the help of Allah and the victory comes. They said: Allah Almighty commanded His Prophet, when Allah grants victory, to seek forgiveness and turn to Him. So he said: What do you say, O Ibn Abbas? I said: It is not so; rather He informed His Prophet of the nearness of his term. He said: When the help of Allah and the victory comes - the conquest of Makka - and you see people entering the religion of Allah in crowds - that is the sign of your death - so glorify with the praise of your Lord and seek His forgiveness; indeed He is Most Relenting. So Umar said to them: How do you blame me for him after what you have seen? And this hadith - its speech is only from Ibn Abbas, but people took it as being from Umar since Ibn Abbas asked him and he affirmed what he said. And Habib ibn Abi Thabit narrated it from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas."
"229","And from what Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utba narrated from Ibn Abbas from Umar. 193 - Musa ibn Abdullah al-Khuzai narrated to us, he said: Bakr ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah, from Ibn Abbas, from Umar, he said: When Abdullah ibn Ubayy ibn Salul died, his son Abdullah ibn Abdullah came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, my father has died, so pray over him. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood, and his companions stood with him, and I stood. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood to pray over him, I stood before him and said: O Messenger of Allah, do you pray over the enemy of Allah who said on such-and-such day such-and-such and such-and-such, and who said on such-and-such day such-and-such and such-and-such - counting his wicked days? He said: When I pressed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) much, he said: Leave me, O Umar, for I have been given a choice: Whether you ask forgiveness for them or do not ask forgiveness for them, if you ask forgiveness for them seventy times, Allah will never forgive them - and had I known that if I exceeded seventy times they would be forgiven, I would have exceeded. He said: So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed, then stood over his grave. He said: So I marveled at my boldness toward the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). I had not moved until the verse came down: And never pray over any of them who dies, ever, nor stand over his grave. He said: So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) never prayed over any of them, nor stood over his grave. And this hadith - we do not know anyone who narrated it from al-Zuhri except Muhammad ibn Ishaq; and there has been narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning the story of praying over Abdullah ibn Ubayy from various angles with different wordings."
"230","194 - Ahmad ibn Abda and Abu Bakr ibn Khallad narrated to us - the wording is Abu Bakr's, and most of the speech of this hadith is Abu Bakr ibn Khallad's - they said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utba, from Ibn Abbas, he said: I used to teach Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf in the last of Umar's caliphate, the last Hajj he performed, while we were at Mina; Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf came to us and said: Had you witnessed the Commander of the Faithful today - a man came to him and said: I heard so-and-so say: If the Commander of the Faithful dies, we shall pledge allegiance to so-and-so. So Umar said: I shall surely stand this evening among the people and warn them against these people who wish to usurp people's affairs. So I said: O Commander of the Faithful, the season gathers the rabble of the people, and they are those who dominate your gathering; so if you delayed that until you arrive in Madina, you would say what you say while settled, and they would retain it from you and place it in its place. He said: So we arrived in Madina, Friday came, and I remembered what Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf had told me, so I went early to the mosque and found Said ibn Zayd ibn Amr ibn Nufayl had preceded me in going early, so I sat beside him with my knee touching his knee. When the sun declined and Umar entered, I said to Said ibn Zayd: The Commander of the Faithful shall surely say today a statement never said before him. So Said became angry and said: And what statement shall he say never said before him? When Umar ascended the pulpit, the caller began his call; when he finished, he stood, praised Allah, extolled Him and invoked blessings upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then said: To proceed: I wish to say a statement decreed for me to say, and I do not know, perhaps it is before my appointed term; so whoever memorizes and comprehends it, let him narrate it wherever his mount takes him; and whoever does not memorize nor comprehend it, I do not permit anyone to lie upon me. Indeed Allah blessed and exalted sent Muhammad and revealed to him the Book, and revealed to him the verse of stoning. Know that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stoned, and we stoned after him. Know that I feared time would grow long upon people and they would say: We do not know the verse of stoning, and they would go astray abandoning an obligation Allah Almighty revealed. Know that stoning is true for whoever commits adultery being married, when proof is established, or pregnancy, or confession. Know that we used to recite: Do not turn away from your fathers, for it is disbelief in you to turn away from your fathers. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not extol me as the Christians extolled Jesus son of Mary; I am but His servant, but say: His servant and His Messenger. Know that among our news when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died: Ali and al-Abbas and those with them stayed away from us in the house of Fatima; the Emigrants gathered to Abu Bakr, and the Ansar gathered in the Saqifa of Banu Saida. So I said to Abu Bakr: Come with us to our brothers of the Ansar. So we went out and met two righteous men of them - al-Zuhri said: They were Uwaym ibn Saida and Man ibn Adi - and they said: Where do you intend, O company of Quraysh? We said: We intend our brothers of the Ansar. They said: Wait until you settle your affair among yourselves. We said: We shall surely come to them. So we came to them, and they were gathered in the Saqifa of Banu Saida, and there was a man wrapped up. I said: Who is this? They said: This is Sad. I said: What is his condition? They said: He is ill. And a preacher of the Ansar stood and said: A band of you has come pouring to us, O company of Quraysh - you are our brothers, and we are the phalanx of Islam - you wish to cut us off and dispute the affair, or monopolize the affair without us. I had rehearsed a statement to say before Abu Bakr's speech; when I went to speak it, he said to me: Gently. By Allah, he left nothing of what I intended to speak that he did not bring, and better than it. He said: O company of Ansar, whatever good you mention concerning yourselves, you are worthy of it; but the Arabs know this affair only for this clan of Quraysh. And I am pleased for you with one of these two men, so pledge allegiance to whichever of them you wish. And he took my hand and the hand of Abu Ubayda ibn al-Jarrah. I would rather be brought forward and my neck struck - which would not bring me near sin - more beloved to me than to rule, or govern a people among whom is Abu Bakr. So Hubab ibn al-Mundhir stood and said: I am their well-rubbed rubbing-post and their propped-up fruit-laden palm; from us an amir and from you an amir; otherwise we shall renew war between us and you as at its beginning. So I said: Two swords do not fit in one scabbard; but from us the rulers and from you the ministers. Extend your hand, O Abu Bakr, I pledge allegiance to you. So he extended his hand, I pledged allegiance to him, the Emigrants and the Ansar pledged allegiance to him, voices rose, clamor increased, and they sprang upon Sad, saying: You killed Sad. So I said: Allah killed Sad. Whoever claims the pledge of Abu Bakr was a sudden lapse - it was indeed a sudden lapse, but Allah protected from its evil. So whoever among you has necks stretched to him like Abu Bakr - may Allah be pleased with him - except whoever pledges allegiance to a man without consultation of the Muslims, he is not to be pledged allegiance to, neither he nor the one pledged to, for fear he be killed. And this hadith - we do not know it narrated from Umar with this wording except from this angle; and others narrated it from al-Zuhri, from Ubaydullah, from Ibn Abbas, from Umar; and Ibn Uyayna is good in presenting it."
"231","195 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ikrima ibn Ammar narrated to us, he said: Abu Zumayl narrated to me, he said: Ibn Abbas narrated to me, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab narrated to me, he said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) withdrew from his wives, I entered the mosque and the people were striking the pebbles saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) divorced his wives - and that was before they were commanded with hijab. He said: So I entered upon Aisha and said: O daughter of Abu Bakr, has your affair reached that you harm the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? She said: What have I to do with you, O son of al-Khattab; attend to your own household. So I entered upon Hafsa bint Umar and said: O Hafsa, has your affair reached that you harm Allah and His Messenger? You know that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) does not love you, and were it not for me he would have divorced you. He said: So she wept most severely. I said: Where is the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: In his storeroom in the loft. And there was the servant of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), Rabah, sitting on the threshold of the loft with his legs dangling over a notched piece of wood which is a trunk by which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) climbs. So I called: O Rabah, seek permission for me upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So he looked at the room then looked at me and said nothing. So I said: O Rabah, seek permission for me upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), for I think the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) thought I came concerning Hafsa. By Allah, had he commanded me to strike her neck I would have struck her neck. So he gestured to me with his hand, and I entered upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was reclining upon a mat. So I sat, and there was upon him a waist-wrapper, nothing else upon him, and the mat had marked his body. So I began casting my sight in the storeroom of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and there was half a sa of barley and acacia pods in a corner of the room, so my eyes hastened to tears. He said: What makes you weep, O son of al-Khattab? I said: O Messenger of Allah, should I not weep when this mat has marked your body, and this is your storeroom - I see in it nothing but what I see - while Caesar and Khosrow are amid fruits and rivers, and you are the Messenger of Allah and His chosen, and this is your storeroom? He said: Are you not pleased that the world be theirs and the Hereafter ours? I said: Yes. And I entered upon him while I saw anger in his face. So I said: O Messenger of Allah, whatever distressed you of the affair of women - if you divorced them, then Allah is with you, His angels, Gabriel, Michael, me and Abu Bakr. And seldom did I speak - and I praise Allah - a speech except I hoped Allah would confirm my saying. And this verse came down: Perhaps his Lord, if he divorces you, will give him in exchange wives better than you. And: And if you both support each other against him, then Allah is his Protector and Gabriel - to the end of the verse. And the daughter of Abu Bakr and Hafsa used to support each other against the rest of the wives of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So I said: O Messenger of Allah, have you divorced them? He said: No. I said: Shall I go down and inform them that you did not divorce them? He said: Yes, if you wish. So I kept speaking to him until anger uncovered from his face, and he uncovered laughing, and he was among the best of people in teeth. So the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) descended, and I descended clinging to the trunk; and he descended as though walking upon the earth, not touching it with his hand. So I said: O Messenger of Allah, were you in the room twenty-nine days? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The month may be twenty-nine. So I stood at the door of the mosque and called with the loudest voice: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not divorce his wives. And the verse came down: And when there comes to them a matter of security or fear, they spread it; had they referred it to the Messenger and to those in authority among them, those who derive it among them would have known it. He said: So I was the one who derived that from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And this hadith - we do not know it narrated with this wording except from Umar; and part of this speech has been narrated from Umar with another isnad, and this isnad is better than the other isnad and more complete in speech; and Abu Zumayl is well-known - Misar, Ikrima ibn Ammar and others narrated from him."
"232","196 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Yunus al-Yamami narrated to us, he said: Ikrima ibn Ammar narrated to us, he said: Abu Zumayl narrated to me, he said: Ibn Abbas narrated to me, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab narrated to me, he said: On the day of Badr, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) looked at the polytheists - they were a thousand - and at his companions - they were three hundred and seventeen men. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) faced the qibla, then extended his hands and began crying out to his Lord, saying: Fulfill for me what You promised me; O Allah, if You destroy this band of the people of Islam, You shall never be worshipped on earth. He said: He kept crying out to his Lord, extending his hands, until his cloak fell from his shoulders. So Abu Bakr came to him, took his cloak and cast it upon his shoulders, then embraced him from behind and said: O Prophet of Allah, your beseeching your Lord suffices you, for He shall surely fulfill for you what He promised you. So Allah Almighty revealed: When you sought help of your Lord, He answered you: I shall reinforce you with a thousand of the angels, rank upon rank. He said: And Allah reinforced him with the angels. Abu Zumayl said: Ibn Abbas narrated to me, he said: While a man that day was pressing in pursuit of a man of the polytheists, he heard a strike of a whip above him, and the voice of the horseman saying: Advance, Hayzum - when the polytheist looked before him, he fell on his back looking at him, and he had been branded on the side of his face like the strike of a whip. So the Ansari came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and informed him, so he said: You have spoken the truth; that is the reinforcement of the third heaven. So they killed on that day seventy and captured seventy. Abu Zumayl said: Ibn Abbas said: When they captured the captives, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Abu Bakr and O Umar, what do you think concerning these captives? Abu Bakr said: They are sons of uncles and kin; we think you take, or we take, ransom from them, so it shall be strength for us against the disbelievers. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Umar, what do you think? He said: I said: No, by Allah, I do not think what Abu Bakr said. O Prophet of Allah, I think you enable us over them so we strike their necks; enable Ali over Aqil so he strikes his neck, and enable me over so-and-so, a relative of Umar, so I strike his neck; for these are the leaders of disbelief and its chiefs. He said: So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) inclined to what Abu Bakr said, and did not incline to what I said. When it was the next day, I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he and Abu Bakr were sitting weeping. So I said: O Messenger of Allah, inform me for what thing you and your companion weep; if I find weeping I shall weep, and if I do not find weeping I shall feign weeping for your weeping? He said: I weep for what your companions presented to me of taking ransom; your punishment was presented to me nearer than this tree - for a tree near the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So Allah Almighty revealed: It is not for a prophet to have captives until he has made slaughter in the land; you desire the goods of the world, and Allah desires the Hereafter; and Allah is Mighty, Wise. Were it not for a prior decree from Allah, there would have touched you for what you took a great punishment. So eat of what you gained, lawful, good. So Allah made booty lawful for you. And this hadith - we do not know it narrated with this wording from Umar except from this angle."
"233","197 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ikrima ibn Ammar narrated to us, he said: Abu Zumayl narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abbas narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab said: Hatib ibn Abi Baltaa wrote a letter to the people of Makka, so Allah informed His Prophet of it. So he sent Ali and al-Zubayr in pursuit of the letter, and they caught a woman upon a camel and extracted from a lock of her locks what the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had told them. So he sent to Hatib and said: O Hatib, did you write this letter? He said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. He said: What prompted you to that? He said: By Allah, I am indeed sincere to Allah and His Messenger, but I was a stranger among the people of Makka, and my family were among them, so I feared for them, so I wrote a letter that harms Allah and His Messenger nothing, and perhaps there may be in it benefit for my family. Umar said: So I drew my sword and said: O Messenger of Allah, enable me over Hatib, for he has disbelieved, so I strike his neck. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O son of al-Khattab, what do you know - perhaps Allah looked upon this band of the people of Badr and said: Do whatever you wish, for I have forgiven you. And this hadith concerning the story of Hatib has been narrated from more than one angle from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And we do not know it narrated from Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), except from this angle with this isnad."
"234","198 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Abd al-Malik narrated to us, he said: Ikrima narrated to us, he said: Abu Zumayl narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abbas narrated to me, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab narrated to me, he said: On the day of Khaybar, some of the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) were killed, so they said: So-and-so is a martyr and so-and-so is a martyr, until they passed by a man and said: So-and-so is a martyr. He said: No; I saw him in the Fire in a cloak he misappropriated, or a garment he misappropriated. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O son of al-Khattab, go and announce among the people that none shall enter Paradise except the believers. So I announced among the people. And this hadith - we do not know it narrated from Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), except from this angle."
"235","199 - al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Azim narrated to us, he said: al-Nadr ibn Muhammad al-Jurashi narrated to us, he said: Ikrima ibn Ammar narrated to us, he said: Simak Abu Zumayl narrated to us, from Ibn Abbas, he said: When al-Madain was conquered, people turned to the world, and I turned to Umar; and most of his hadith was from Umar."
"236","200 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Masud narrated to us, he said: Ikrima ibn Ammar narrated to us, he said: Abu Zumayl Simak narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abbas narrated to us, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made peace with the people of Makka, a peace - had it been applied to another and judged upon what I heard - and that is that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made peace with the people of Makka that whoever comes to us of them, we shall return him to them. And this hadith has been narrated from Umar with other than this wording in the story of Abu Jandal."
"237","And from the hadith of Ikrima from Ibn Abbas from Umar. 201 - Muhammad ibn Miskin narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Bakr narrated to us, he said: al-Awzai narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, he said: Ikrima narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abbas narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab narrated to us, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A comer from my Lord came to me tonight and said: Pray in this blessed valley, and say: Umra in Hajj. And this hadith - we do not know it narrated from Umar except from the hadith of Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Ikrima, from Ibn Abbas, from Umar. And others besides al-Awzai narrated it - Ali ibn al-Mubarak narrated it from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Ikrima, from Ibn Abbas, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"238","202 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated it to us with it, he said: Harun ibn Ismail narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Ikrima, from Ibn Abbas, from Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with the like of it."
"239","203 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Abu Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Israil narrated to us, from Simak, from Ikrima, from Ibn Abbas, from Umar, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) caught up with him while he was in a caravan, swearing by his father, so he said: Do not swear by your fathers. He said: So I never returned to it afterwards. And this hadith - we do not know it narrated from Ibn Abbas, from Umar, except from this angle with this isnad."
"240","204 - al-Fadl ibn Sahl narrated to us, he said: Malik ibn Ismail narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Abdullah al-Qummi narrated to us, from Hafs ibn Humayd, from Ikrima, from Ibn Abbas, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I hold your waist-wrappers - come away from the Fire - while you plunge into it, or rush into it like the rushing of moths into the Fire and locusts - meaning: into the Fire - while I hold your waist-wrappers, and I am your predecessor at the Hawd. So you shall come to me together and scattered, and I shall recognize you by your marks and names, as a man recognizes a horse - and another said: as a man recognizes the strange camel among his camels. Then you shall be taken to the left, so I shall say: To me, O Lord - my nation, my nation. So He shall say, or it shall be said: O Muhammad, you do not know what they innovated after you; they walked backwards after you. So I shall surely not know one of you coming on the Day of Resurrection carrying a sheep with its bleating, calling: O Muhammad, so I shall say: I own nothing for you; you were informed. And I shall surely not know one of you coming on the Day of Resurrection carrying a camel with its grumbling, calling: O Muhammad, so I shall say: I own nothing for you from Allah; you were informed. And I shall surely not know one of you coming on the Day of Resurrection carrying a bundle, saying: O Muhammad, O Muhammad, so I shall say: I own nothing for you from Allah; you were informed. And this hadith - we do not know it narrated from Umar except from this angle with this isnad; and Hafs ibn Humayd - we do not know anyone who narrated from him except Yaqub al-Qummi."
"241","205 - Muhammad ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Abu Khalaf Abdullah ibn Isa narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Ubayd narrated to us, from Ikrima, from Ibn Abbas: Umar heard that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out one day at noon and found Abu Bakr - may Allah be pleased with him - sitting in the mosque. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What brought you out at this hour? He said: O Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), what brought you out? He said: What brought you out brought me out. Then Umar - may Allah be pleased with him - came, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O son of al-Khattab, what brought you out at this hour? He said: What brought you two out brought me out, O Messenger of Allah. So he sat with them, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) began narrating to them. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to them: Have you strength to go to these date-palms and partake of food and drink? We said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. So we went until we came to the house of Malik ibn al-Tayyihan, Abu al-Haytham al-Ansari. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) advanced before us and sought permission upon them, while the mother of Abu al-Haytham heard the greeting, wishing the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would increase them in greeting. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended to leave, the mother of Abu al-Haytham came out hastening and said: O Messenger of Allah, I heard your greeting, but I wished you would increase us in your greeting. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to her: Where is Abu al-Haytham? She said: Near, O Messenger of Allah; he went to fetch us sweet water. Enter; he shall come presently. So she spread for them a mat under a tree until Abu al-Haytham came with his donkey, upon it two water-skins of water. Abu al-Haytham rejoiced at them, offered them his greeting, and climbed a date-palm and cut clusters. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Enough, O Abu al-Haytham. He said: O Messenger of Allah, eat of its unripe and ripe dates, and enjoy them. Then he brought them water and they drank upon it. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: This is of the bliss you shall be asked about. Then Abu al-Haytham stood to a sheep to slaughter it, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Beware of the milch one. Then Abu al-Haytham stood, kneaded for them, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), Abu Bakr and Umar laid their heads and slept. They awoke with their food ready, so he placed it before them; they ate, were satiated and praised Allah. And Abu al-Haytham brought them the remainder of the clusters, and they partook of it; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) greeted and supplicated good for them, then said to Abu al-Haytham: When it reaches you that slaves have come to us, come to us. Abu al-Haytham said: When it reached me that slaves had come to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), I came to Madina, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave me a head; I contracted him for forty thousand dirhams, and I never saw a head greater in blessing than him. Abdullah ibn Isa said: So I narrated it to Ismail al-Makki, and he narrated to me the like of it, adding in it: So the mother of Abu al-Haytham said to him: Had you supplicated for us. He said: The fasting break their fast with you, the righteous eat your food, and the angels invoke blessings upon you. And this hadith - we do not know it narrated from Umar except from this angle with this isnad; and none narrated it from Yunus except Abdullah ibn Isa."
"242","206 - Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn al-Junayd narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Ibrahim ibn Sad narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to me, from Salih ibn Kaysan, from Ibn Shihab, that Ubaydullah ibn Abi Thawr narrated to him from Abdullah ibn Abbas, he said: I remained keen to ask Umar about the two women of the wives of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to whom it was said: If you both turn to Allah, your hearts have inclined - until I went out with him; he alighted one day, and I turned aside with him with the water-vessel, and he relieved himself, then came, and I poured upon his hands and he performed ablution. So I said: O Commander of the Faithful, who are the two women of the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to whom it was said: If you both turn to Allah, your hearts have inclined? He said: How strange of you, O Ibn Abbas - she is Hafsa and Aisha. Then Umar proceeded with the hadith, driving it, saying: I used to stay with a clan of the Ansar, or with a house of the Ansar, of Banu Umayya ibn Zayd; and we used to take turns staying - he would stay one day and I would stay one day. When I stayed, I would bring him the news of my day of what came down of revelation and other than it; and when he stayed, he would do the like of that. And we, the company of Quraysh, used to dominate women. When we came to the Ansar, there were a people dominated by their women, so our women began taking from the manners of the women of the Ansar. So I shouted at my wife and she answered me back; I disapproved that she answered me back, so she said: Why do you disapprove that I answer you back - by Allah, the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) answer him back, and one of them forsakes him the day until the night. That weighed heavily upon me, so I said: Whoever of them does that has failed. So I gathered my garments and entered upon Hafsa bint Umar, saying to her: O Hafsa, does one of you anger the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) until the night? She said: Yes. I said: You have failed and lost. Do you feel secure that Allah Almighty may anger for the anger of His Messenger and you perish - do not vie with, or do not rival, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), nor answer him back in anything, nor forsake him; and ask me whatever occurs to you; and let not your neighbor deceive you - if she is fairer than you and more beloved to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - meaning: Aisha. Umar said: And we used to talk that Ghassan were shoeing horses to raid us. He said: So my Ansari companion alighted on the day of his turn, returned to me in the evening, knocked my door violently, then said: Is he asleep? He said: So I was startled and went out to him. He said: A great matter has occurred. I said: What is it? Has Ghassan come? He said: Rather greater than that - the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) divorced his wives. So I said: Hafsa has failed and lost; I thought this was about to be. So I gathered my garments and performed Fajr prayer with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered a loft of his in which he secludes himself. He said: So I entered upon Hafsa while she was weeping. I said: What is the matter with you? Tell me your news - has the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) divorced you? She said: I do not know; here he is, secluded in this loft. So I went out until I came to the pulpit, and there was a group weeping, some of them. So I sat with them a while, then what I felt overcame me, so I came to the loft in which was the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said to a black servant of his: Seek permission for Umar ibn al-Khattab. He said: So the servant entered upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then the servant went out to me and said: I mentioned you, and he was silent. So I left and went until I sat with the group at the pulpit. Then what I felt overcame me, so I said to the servant: Seek permission for Umar ibn al-Khattab. So he entered, then returned to me and said: I mentioned you to him, and he was silent. He said: So I returned and sat with the group at the pulpit. Then what I felt overcame me, so I came and said to the servant: Seek permission for Umar. So he entered, then went out to me and said: I mentioned you to him, and he was silent. When I turned to leave, the servant called me, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) has permitted you. So I entered upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he was reclining upon the strands of a mat with nothing between him and it; the strands of the mat had marked his side, reclining upon a cushion of leather stuffed with fiber. So I greeted the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying while standing: O Messenger of Allah, have you divorced your wives? He said: So he raised his sight to me and said: No. So I said: Allah is Greatest - while standing: O Messenger of Allah, had you seen me - we, the company of Quraysh, used to dominate women; when we came to Madina, we came upon a people dominated by their women. So I became angry at my wife, and she answered me back, and I disapproved that of her. She said: Do you disapprove that I answer you back - by Allah, the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) answer him back, and one of them forsakes him until the night. He said: I said: Hafsa has failed and lost; does one of them feel secure that Allah may anger upon her for the anger of His Messenger, and she perishes? He said: So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) smiled. He said: So I said: Had you seen me when I entered upon Hafsa and said to her: Let not your neighbor deceive you - if she is more beautiful than you and more beloved to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - meaning: Aisha. So he smiled another smile. He said: So I sat until I saw him smile. Then I raised my sight in his house; by Allah, I saw nothing to return the sight except three hides. He said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, supplicate Allah to widen for your nation, for Persia and Rome have been widened for and given the world, though they do not worship Allah. He said: So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sat upright, having been reclining, and said: Are you in doubt, O son of al-Khattab - those are a people whose good things were hastened for them in the life of the world. So I said: O Messenger of Allah, seek forgiveness for me. He said: So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) withdrew from his wives because of that talk. Aisha said: And he had said: I shall not enter upon you for a month, due to the severity of his displeasure (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when Allah informed him their talk. When twenty-nine had passed, he entered upon Aisha, beginning with her. Aisha said to him: You had sworn, O Messenger of Allah, not to enter upon us for a month, and we have reached twenty-nine nights, counting them. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to her: The month is twenty-nine nights. Aisha said: So Allah revealed the choice; he began with me, the first woman of his wives, saying: I present to you an affair - do not hasten in it until you consult your parents. And he knew my parents would not command me to part from him. She said: So I said: And what is it? He said: Allah said: O Prophet, say to your wives: If you desire the life of the world and its adornment, come, I shall provide for you and release you a fair release; and if you desire Allah and His Messenger. She said: So I said: In which of this shall I consult my parents - why did you not present this to one older than me of your wives? She said: He said: Rather you. She said: So I said: Before consulting my parents, I desire Allah, His Messenger and the abode of the Hereafter. He said: And it is said Aisha said: I am pleased with Allah and His Messenger in hardship and ease. Aisha said: Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon his wives and gave them the choice; they were upon one word as Aisha said."
"243","And from what Tawus narrated from Ibn Abbas from Umar. 207 - Ahmad ibn Abda narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyayna narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Tawus, from Ibn Abbas, from Umar, that it reached him a man sold wine, so Umar said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah cursed the Jews; fats were forbidden to them, so they sold them and ate their prices. Abu Bakr said: And this hadith has been narrated from Umar from more than one angle with different isnads, so we mentioned each hadith in its place with its isnad. And it has been narrated from Aisha, Abu Hurayra, Usama and others, so we mentioned it with this isnad from Umar."
"244","208 - Umar ibn al-Khattab narrated to us, he said: Safwan, meaning Ibn Salih, narrated to us, he said: al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Hanzala narrated to us, from Tawus, from Ibn Abbas, from Umar, that he saw him kissing the Stone and saying: Had I not seen the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) kissing you, I would not have kissed you. Abu Bakr said: Others narrated it from Hanzala, from Tawus, from Umar, except al-Walid, for he connected it from Hanzala, from Tawus, from Ibn Abbas, from Umar."
"245","And from what Kulayb Abu Asim narrated from Ibn Abbas from Umar. 209 - Ibrahim ibn Said and Ahmad ibn Aban narrated to us, they said: Sufyan ibn Uyayna narrated to us, from Asim ibn Kulayb, from his father, from Ibn Abbas, that Umar ibn al-Khattab, whenever he prayed a prayer, would sit for the people; whoever had a need would speak to him, otherwise he would stand. So I attended the door one day and said: O Yarfa. He went out, and Uthman was at the door. So Yarfa went out and said: Stand, O son of Affan; stand, O son of Abbas. So we entered upon Umar, and with him were heaps of wealth. He said: I looked among the people of Madina and saw you two among the most claned of its people, so take this wealth and divide it; if there is surplus in it, return it. I said: And if there is deficiency, will you increase us? He said: A quiver from a rough one - you know Muhammad and his family used to eat dried meat. I said: Yes, by Allah - had Allah opened this upon Muhammad, he would have done in it other than what you did. So he became angry and swelled until his ribs differed, saying: Then, what would he have done in it? I said: Then he would have eaten and fed us. So it was uncovered from him. Abu Bakr said: And this hadith - we do not know anyone who narrated it from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with this wording other than Umar; and we do not know for it a path from Umar except this path."
"246","210 - Yaqub ibn Ibrahim ibn Kathir narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Idris narrated to us, from Asim ibn Kulayb, from his father, from Ibn Abbas, from Umar, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioned Laylat al-Qadr and said: Seek it in the last ten, in an odd of them."
"247","And from what Ali ibn Husayn narrated from Ibn Abbas from Umar. 211 - Abdullah ibn Shabib narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Muhammad al-Farwi narrated to us, he said: Malik narrated to us, from Salim Abu al-Nadr, from Ali ibn Husayn, from Abdullah ibn Abbas, he said: I loved to know the two who had joined against the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So it was said to me: Umar ibn al-Khattab knows that. So I remained a year wanting to ask him, then fearing him. Then I asked him, so he said: They are Aisha and Hafsa. So I said: By Allah, I wanted to ask you for a year, fearing you. He said: So why that? Or from what that? Abu Bakr said: And this hadith - we do not know anyone who narrated it from Salim Abu al-Nadr except Malik; and Salim Abu al-Nadr did not narrate with isnad from Ali ibn Husayn except this hadith."
"248","And from what Ubayd ibn Hunayn narrated from Ibn Abbas from Umar. 212 - Ahmad ibn Dawud al-Wasiti narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyayna narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Said, from Ubayd ibn Hunayn, from Ibn Abbas, he said: I asked Umar about the two who had joined against the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: Aisha and Hafsa. Abu Bakr said: And this hadith was narrated by Hammad ibn Salama and Hammad ibn Zayd also from Yahya ibn Said, from Ubayd ibn Hunayn, from Ibn Abbas, from Umar."
"249","And from what Muhammad ibn Sirin narrated from Ibn Abbas from Umar. 213 - Muhammad ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Ala ibn Abd al-Ala al-Sami narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Hassan narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Ibn Abbas, from Umar, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to command ghusl on Friday. And this hadith has been narrated from Umar from more than one angle; and it has been narrated from Ibn Abbas, also from Umar, from another path - Asim al-Ahwal narrated it from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Ibn Abbas, from Umar; al-Qasim ibn Malik al-Muzani narrated it from Asim."
"250","And from what Nafi ibn Jubayr narrated from Ibn Abbas from Umar. 214 - Umar ibn al-Khattab narrated to us, he said: Asbagh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, from Amr ibn al-Harith, from Said ibn Abi Hilal, from Utba ibn Abi Utba, from Nafi ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, he said: It was said to Umar ibn al-Khattab: Narrate to us of the affair of hardship. Umar said: We went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Tabuk in severe heat. We alighted a station in which severe thirst befell us, until we thought our necks would break; until one of us would go seeking privacy and not return until he thought his neck would break; and until a man would slaughter his camel, squeeze its dung and drink it, and place it upon his belly. So Abu Bakr al-Siddiq said: O Messenger of Allah, Allah has accustomed you to good in supplication, so supplicate for us. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do you love that, O Abu Bakr? He said: Yes. He said: So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) raised his hands and did not return them until the sky leaned, shaded, then poured. So they filled what they had with them. Then we went to look and found it had not passed the camp. And this hadith - we do not know it narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with this wording except from Umar with this isnad."
"251","215 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim informed me, he said: Jafar ibn Abdullah ibn Uthman al-Makhzumi narrated to us, he said: I saw Muhammad ibn Abbad ibn Jafar kiss the Stone then prostrate upon it. I said: What is this? He said: I saw your maternal uncle Ibn Abbas kiss the Stone then prostrate upon it, saying: I saw Umar kiss it and prostrate upon it, saying: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) kiss it and prostrate upon it. And this hadith - we do not know it narrated from Umar except from this angle with this isnad."
"252","And from what Abu Hurayra narrated from Umar from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). 216 - Salama ibn Shabib narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, he said: Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah, from Abu Hurayra. 217 - And Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to me, from al-Numan ibn Rashid, from al-Zuhri, from Ubaydullah, from Abu Hurayra, from Umar, that he said to Abu Bakr: How do you fight the people when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I was commanded to fight the people until they say: There is no god but Allah. Abu Bakr said: Had they withheld from me a young goat of what they used to render to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), I would have fought them over it. Abu Bakr said: And this hadith was narrated from al-Zuhri, from Ubaydullah, from Abu Hurayra, by Mamar, al-Numan, Muhammad ibn Ishaq, Ibrahim ibn Sad and others, so we sufficed with Mamar. And Salih ibn Abi al-Akhdar said: from al-Zuhri, from Said and Abu Salama, from Abu Hurayra, from Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And there have been narrated in this chapter from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) hadiths from Abu Hurayra, Ibn Umar, Jabir, Anas and others concerning: had they withheld from me a young goat or a hobble."
"253","218 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Samad ibn Abd al-Warith narrated to us, he said: al-Harb ibn Shaddad narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salama, from Abu Hurayra, from Umar, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to command us to perform ghusl on Friday."
"254","And from what Anas ibn Malik narrated from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him. 219 - Hudba ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us, from Thabit, from Anas, he said: When Umar was stabbed, Hafsa wailed over him. So Umar said: Do you not know that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The one wailed over is punished. Abu Bakr said: And this hadith was narrated by Hammad ibn Salama from Thabit, from Anas, from Umar; and Jafar ibn Sulayman narrated it from Thabit, from Abu Rafi, from Umar. And we do not know anyone who narrated this wording from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except Umar."
"255","220 - Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray narrated to us, he said: Humayd narrated to us, from Anas, he said: Umar said: I agreed with my Lord in three: I said: O Messenger of Allah, had you taken the Maqam as a place of prayer - then: And take of the Maqam of Ibrahim a place of prayer, came down. And I said: Had you veiled the Mothers of the Believers, for the righteous and the wicked enter upon you - then the verse of hijab came down. He said: And it reached me something of the Mothers of the Believers, so I came to them, woman by woman, until I came to one of them, and she said: Before me is the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - does he not admonish his household. So Allah Almighty revealed: Perhaps his Lord, if he divorces you - to the end of the verse. And this hadith - we do not know it narrated except from Umar ibn al-Khattab; and Ali ibn Zayd also narrated it from Anas."
"256","221 - Ahmad ibn Abdullah ibn Ali ibn Suwayd ibn Manjuf narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Humayd and Ali ibn Zayd, from Anas, from Umar, with the like of it, or close to it."
"257","222 - Amr narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn al-Mughira narrated to us, from Thabit, from Anas, he said: We sighted the crescent, and I was a man of sharp sight, so I said: O Commander of the Faithful, do you not see it? He said: I hope to see it while upon my bed. Then he began narrating to us, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) kept showing us the death-places of the people on the night of Badr: This is the death-place of so-and-so tomorrow, if Allah wills; and this is the death-place of so-and-so tomorrow, if Allah wills. And none of them deviated from the death-place the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) named. And this hadith - Sulayman ibn al-Mughira worded its isnad well; and others besides Sulayman make it from Anas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And we do not recall anyone who narrated it from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except Umar."
"258","And from what Abu Said al-Khudri narrated from Umar from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). 223 - Yahya ibn Habib ibn Arabi narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn al-Mufaddal narrated to us, he said: Dawud, meaning Ibn Abi Hind, narrated to us, from Abu Nadra, from Abu Said, he said: The lizard was mentioned before Umar, so he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only found it distasteful and did not forbid it. And this hadith has been narrated from Umar from more than one angle, and this isnad is among the best of them in connection from Umar."
"259","224 - And Salm ibn Junada narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash mentioned from al-Amash, from Abu Salih, from Abu Said: that Umar said: Two men entered upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and asked him, so he ordered for them two dinars. They went out from him and met Umar, praised, spoke good and thanked what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had done with them. So I entered upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and informed him of what they said. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: But so-and-so - I gave him between ten and a hundred, and he did not say that. One of them asks me and departs with his asking to the Fire. Umar said: Why do you give us what is Fire? He said: They refuse but to ask me, and Allah refuses for me miserliness. Abu Bakr said: And this hadith has been narrated from Umar from various angles - Abu Bakr narrated it thus from al-Amash, from Abu Salih, from Abu Said, from Umar. And Jarir narrated it from al-Amash, from Atiyya, from Abu Said. And it has been narrated from Jabir, from Umar; and from Salman ibn Rabia, from Umar."
"260","And from what Abu Musa al-Ashari narrated from Umar from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). 225 - Muhammad ibn Abd al-Malik narrated to us, he said: Abu Awana narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from Abu Burda, from Abu Musa, he said: When Umar was struck, Suhayb came from his house and entered upon him, standing at him weeping. Umar said to him: Do you weep over me? He said: Yes, by Allah, over you I weep, O Commander of the Faithful. He said: By Allah, I knew that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever is wept over is punished. And this hadith was also narrated by al-Shaybani from Abu Burda, from Abu Musa, from Umar."
"261","226 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Jafar narrated to us, he said: Shuba narrated to us, from al-Hakam, from Umara ibn Umayr, from Ibrahim ibn Abi Musa, from his father: that he used to give fatwas to people concerning muta, so a man said to him: Slow down with some of your fatwas, for you do not know what the Commander of the Faithful innovated concerning the rites afterwards - until he met him and asked him. Umar said: I knew the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did it, and his companions; but I disliked that they remain married with them among the acacia trees, then go to Hajj with their heads dripping. And this hadith has been narrated from Abu Musa from another angle, and he narrated it with other than this wording; and we do not know Ibrahim ibn Abi Musa narrated from his father except this hadith."
"262","227 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna and Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, they said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Qays ibn Muslim, from Tariq ibn Shihab, from Abu Musa al-Ashari, he said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was at al-Batha. He said: With what did you enter ihram? He said: I entered ihram with what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered ihram with. He said: Did you drive sacrificial animals? I said: No. He said: Then circumambulate the House and al-Safa and al-Marwa, and exit ihram. So I circumambulated the House and al-Safa and al-Marwa, then came to a woman of my people and she combed my head. So I used to give fatwas to people in the rule of Abu Bakr and the rule of Umar. While I was speaking in Madina, a man said: Do you know what the Commander of the Faithful innovated concerning the rites? So I said: O people, whomever we gave a fatwa with something, let him wait - this Commander of the Faithful is coming to you, so follow him. When he came, I said: O Commander of the Faithful, what is this you innovated concerning the rites? He said: If we take by the Book of Allah, Allah Almighty said: And complete Hajj and Umra for Allah; and if we take by the sunna of our Prophet, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not exit ihram until he sacrificed the sacrificial animals."
"263","228 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Jafar narrated to us, he said: Shuba narrated to us, from Qays ibn Muslim, from Tariq ibn Shihab, from Abu Musa, from Umar, with the like of it. And this hadith - we do not know it narrated with an isnad better than this isnad; and a group narrated it from Qays ibn Muslim, from Tariq ibn Shihab, from Abu Musa."
"264","And from what Jabir ibn Abdullah narrated from Umar from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). 229 - Nasr ibn Ali and Amr ibn Ali narrated to us - their hadiths are close - Nasr said: Abu Ahmad informed me, and Amr said: Abu Ahmad narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If I live, if Allah wills, I shall surely expel the Jews from the Arabian Peninsula; and if I live, if Allah wills, I shall surely forbid naming Rabah, Najah, Aflah and Yasar."
"265","230 - And Abdullah ibn Shabib narrated it to us, he said: Ismail ibn Abi Uways narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ismail narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Ibrahim ibn Uqba narrated to us, from his uncle Musa ibn Uqba, from al-Zuhri, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, from Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said: Expel the Jews and Christians from the Arabian Peninsula."
"266","231 - Abu Bakr said: And this hadith was also narrated by Ibn Jurayj from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, from Umar. Amr ibn Ali narrated it to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us."
"267","232 - And Salama ibn Shabib narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ayan narrated to us, he said: Maqil narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, from Umar: that a man performed ablution for Zuhr prayer and left the spot of a fingernail upon his foot untouched by water. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw him and said: Return and perfect your ablution. So he returned, performed ablution and prayed. Abu Bakr said: And this hadith - we do not know anyone who narrated it with isnad from Umar except from this angle; and al-Amash narrated it from Abu Sufyan, from Umar, halted."
"268","233 - And Muhammad ibn Yahya al-Qutai narrated it to us, he said: Bishr ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahia narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, from Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The people of Madina shall surely leave it, then only few shall inhabit it; then they shall leave it and never inhabit it ever. Abu Bakr said: And this hadith - we do not know it narrated from Umar except from this angle; nor from other than Umar with this wording from a sound angle; and Ibn Lahia - people tolerated his hadith, like Ibn al-Mubarak, Ibn Wahb and others of the trustworthy."
"269","234 - And Zuhayr ibn Muhammad ibn Qumayr narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Abd al-Karim al-Sanani narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Aqil narrated to us, from his father, from Wahb ibn Munabbih: that he heard Jabir ibn Abdullah: that Umar ibn al-Khattab informed him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I shall surely expel the Jews and Christians from the Arabian Peninsula until I leave in it none but a Muslim. And we do not know Wahb ibn Munabbih narrated from Jabir, from Umar, except this hadith; and Wahb narrated from Jabir, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), sound hadiths."
"270","235 - And Nahar ibn Uthman narrated to us, he said: Mutamir ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Bishr, from al-Amash, from Abu Sufyan, from Jabir, from Umar, he said: Two men entered upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he ordered for them two dinars. They went out from him, met Umar, praised, spoke good and thanked what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had done with them. So I entered upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and informed him of what they said. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: But so-and-so - I gave him between ten and a hundred, and he did not say that. One of them asks me and departs with his asking to the Fire. They said: So why do you give us what is Fire? He said: They refuse but to ask me, and Allah refuses for me miserliness. And this hadith - we do not know anyone who narrated it from Jabir, from Umar, except Abdullah ibn Bishr, from al-Amash, from Abu Sufyan; and we do not know anyone who narrated it from Abdullah ibn Bishr except Mutamir ibn Sulayman."
"271","236 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna and Hilal ibn Yahya narrated to us, they said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Layth ibn Sad narrated to us, from Bukayr ibn Abdullah ibn al-Ashajj, from Abd al-Malik ibn Said, from Jabir, from Umar, he said: I felt desire and kissed while fasting, then came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: I did today a grave thing. He said: What is it? I said: I felt desire and kissed while fasting. He said: Have you seen had you rinsed from water? I said: Then it would not harm. He said: Then why? And this hadith - we do not know it narrated except from Umar from this angle."
"272","And from what al-Numan ibn Bashir narrated from Umar. 237 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Jafar narrated to us, he said: Shuba narrated to us, from Simak, he said: I heard al-Numan ibn Bashir delivering a sermon, saying: Umar said - mentioning what people attained of the world - he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) spending the day writhing, not finding lowly dates to fill his belly with. And this hadith - we do not know it narrated from Umar except from this angle; Shuba only said in it: from Simak, from al-Numan, from Umar. And others narrated it from al-Numan ibn Bashir, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And Shuba is more memorizing than others who narrated it from Simak."
"273","238 - al-Husayn ibn Mahdi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Razzaq informed me, he said: Israil informed me, from Simak, meaning Ibn Harb, from al-Numan ibn Bashir, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, concerning the saying of Allah blessed and exalted: And when the buried infant girl is asked. He said: Qays ibn Asim came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, I buried daughters of mine alive in the Jahiliyya. He said: Free a slave for each of them. He said: O Messenger of Allah, I am an owner of camels. He said: Then sacrifice for each of them a camel. And this hadith - we do not know it narrated from Umar except from this angle; and none narrated it with isnad from Umar except Abd al-Razzaq from Israil - although we heard it from none from Abd al-Razzaq except al-Husayn ibn Mahdi; and Abd al-Razzaq was opposed in its isnad from Israil."
"274","And from what al-Ashath ibn Qays narrated from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him. 239 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Abu Awana narrated to us, he said: Dawud al-Awdi narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman al-Musalli, from al-Ashath ibn Qays, he said: I was a guest of Umar; when it was part of the night, he stood to his wife beating her, so I intervened between them. When he returned to his bed and took his resting-place, he said: O Ashath, memorize from me something I heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: A man is not asked for what he beats his wife? And this hadith - we do not know it narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except from this angle; and Abd al-Rahman al-Musalli, in my view he is Abu Wabara; and Abd al-Rahman and his son narrated hadiths; and Abd al-Rahman - we do not know he narrated other than this hadith."
"275","And from what al-Bara ibn Azib narrated from Umar. 240 - Yusuf ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn al-Jahm narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Abi Qays narrated to us, from Ali ibn Abd al-Ala, from his father, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from al-Bara, he said: I was sitting with Umar when a rider came to him claiming he saw the crescent - the crescent of Shawwal - alone. So Umar said: O people, break fast. Then he stood, came to water, performed ablution from it and wiped over two muqs of his. Then he stood and prayed Maghrib. Then the rider stood and said: O Commander of the Faithful, by Allah, I ask none about this I saw other than you. He said: Yes; I saw one better than me do it, and the best of this nation - I saw Abu al-Qasim (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) do it as you saw me do. And this hadith - we do not know it narrated with this wording from Umar except from this angle; and others narrated this hadith from Abd al-Ala, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Umar, without mentioning al-Bara; and some of them did not narrate it with isnad from Umar."
"276","And from what Abd al-Rahman ibn Abza narrated from Umar. 241 - Ali ibn Nasr and Muhammad ibn Mamar narrated to us - the wording is Muhammad ibn Mamar's - they said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shuba narrated to us, from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid, from al-Shabi, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abza: that Umar pronounced four takbirs over Zaynab bint Jahsh, then sent to the wives of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Who shall enter her into her grave? They said: Whoever used to enter upon her in her life. Then Umar said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say: The quickest of you to join me is the longest of you in hand. So they used to stretch their hands; and that was only because she was a craftswoman aiding with what she made in the path of Allah. And this hadith has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from various angles; and we do not know anyone who narrated it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) more eminent than Umar; and others narrated it from Ismail, from al-Shabi, mursal; and Shuba narrated it with isnad, saying: from Ibn Abi Layla; and we do not know anyone who narrated it from Shuba except Wahb."
"277","242 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Abi Ayyub narrated to us, he said: Abu Aqil informed me: that his cousin, his father's brother's son, narrated to him: that Uqba ibn Amir narrated to him that he went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the expedition of Tabuk. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sat one day narrating to his companions, saying: Whoever performs ablution, perfecting ablution, then stands and prays two rakats - they expiate his sins, and he becomes as the day his mother bore him. Uqba said: Praise be to Allah who granted me to hear this hadith. Umar, while sitting, said: Do you marvel at this - the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said before this more than this; he said before you came. So I said to Umar: What did he say? He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever performs ablution, perfecting ablution, then raises his sight to the heaven and says: I bear witness that there is no god but Allah alone, He has no partner, and that Muhammad is His servant and His Messenger - eight doors of Paradise are opened for him; he enters from whichever he wishes."
"278","243 - And Bishr ibn Adam narrated it to us, he said: Zayd ibn al-Hubab narrated to us, he said: Muawiya ibn Salih narrated to us, from Rabia ibn Yazid, from Abu Idris, from Uqba ibn Amir, from Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with the like of it. Abu Bakr said: And this hadith has been narrated from Uqba from more than one angle, so we sufficed with those we mentioned."
"279","And from what Ibn al-Saidi narrated from Umar. 244 - Ibrahim ibn Said al-Jawhari narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyayna narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from al-Saib ibn Yazid, from Huwaytib ibn Abd al-Uzza, from Ibn al-Saidi, from Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whatever Allah brings you of this wealth without asking and without the soul aspiring, accept it."
"280","245 - Amr ibn Ali and Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, they said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to us, from Bukayr ibn Abdullah ibn al-Ashajj, from Busr ibn Said, from Ibn al-Saidi, he said: Umar, may Allah have mercy on him, employed me upon charity. When I finished, he ordered for me my wage. I said: I worked only for Allah, and my wage is upon Allah. He said: Take the wage of what you worked, for I worked in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he paid me. So I said the like of what you said. He said: When you are given something without asking for it, eat and give charity. And this hadith has been narrated from Umar from more than one angle; and we do not know Ibn al-Saidi narrated from Umar except this hadith. And in the hadith of al-Zuhri are four of the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), some of them narrating from some: al-Saib ibn Yazid, Huwaytib ibn Abd al-Uzza, Ibn al-Saidi and Umar. And four narrated it; and we do not know in a hadith four men of the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), some narrating from some, with a sound isnad, except in this hadith."
"281","And from what Fadala ibn Ubayd narrated from Umar. 246 - Bishr ibn Adam narrated to us, he said: Zayd ibn al-Hubab narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahia narrated to me, he said: Ata ibn Dinar narrated to me, he said: Abu Yazid al-Khawlani narrated to me, he said: Fadala ibn Ubayd narrated to me, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab narrated to me: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Martyrs are four: a man of good faith who met the enemy, was true to Allah and fought until killed - people shall raise their necks looking at him on the Day of Resurrection; and a believing man who met the enemy, as though struck by the thorns of talh from cowardice - a stray arrow struck him and killed him - he is in the second rank; and a man who mixed a righteous deed and another evil - met the enemy, was true to Allah and fought until killed - he is in the third rank; and a man who transgressed against himself - met the enemy, was true to Allah and fought until killed - he is in the fourth rank. Abu Bakr said: And this hadith - we do not know it narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with this wording except from Umar from this angle; and it has no isnad other than this isnad."
"282","And from what Amr ibn Hurayth narrated from Umar. 247 - Zuhayr ibn Muhammad and Ahmad ibn Ishaq narrated to us - the wording is Zuhayr's - they said: Khallad ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid, from Amr ibn Hurayth, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: That the belly of one of you be filled with pus is better for him than it be filled with poetry. And this hadith has been narrated by more than one from Ismail, from Amr ibn Hurayth, from Umar, halted. And we do not know anyone who narrated it with isnad except Khallad from Sufyan."
"283","And from what Kahmas al-Hilali narrated from Umar. 248 - Muhammad ibn Bashshar narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Yazid narrated to us - a Basran from whom a group narrated - he said: Muawiya ibn Qurra narrated to us, from Kahmas al-Hilali, he said: I heard Umar ibn al-Khattab say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The best of people is my generation of whom I am; then those who follow them; then there shall arise peoples among whom fatness spreads - they bear witness though not asked, and they have clamor in their markets. And we do not know Kahmas al-Hilali narrated with isnad from Umar except this hadith; and Kahmas narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) one hadith."
"284","And from what Abu al-Tufayl narrated from Umar ibn al-Khattab. 249 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Sad narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, he said: Abu al-Tufayl narrated to me: that Umar employed Nafi ibn Abd al-Harith over Makka, then met him at Usfan and said: Whom did you employ? Or he said: Whom did you leave over Makka? He said: I left over them Ibn Abza. He said: Who is Ibn Abza? He said: A freedman of ours. He said: And you leave over them a freedman? He said: O Commander of the Faithful, he is a reciter of the Book of Allah, knowledgeable of the obligations, a judge. So Umar said: Indeed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah blessed and exalted surely raises with this Book peoples and lowers with it others. And this hadith - we do not know it narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with this wording except from this angle with this isnad."
"285","And from what Abdullah ibn Sarjis narrated from Umar. 250 - Yahya ibn Habib ibn Arabi narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Asim al-Ahwal, from Abdullah ibn Sarjis, he said: I saw al-Usayli, meaning Umar, kissing the Stone and saying: Had I not seen the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) kiss you, I would not have kissed you. And this hadith - we do not know it narrated except from the hadith of Asim, from Abdullah ibn Sarjis, from Umar. And a group narrated it from Asim."
"286","And from what Aisha, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), narrated from Umar. 251 - Ibrahim ibn Said narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Uways narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwa, from his father, from Aisha, from Umar, he said: Abu Bakr was the most beloved of us to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And this hadith - we do not know it narrated from Aisha, from Umar, except from this angle."
"287","And from what Umm Atiyya narrated from Umar. 252 - Yahya ibn Muhammad ibn al-Sakan narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Kathir al-Anbari narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Said narrated to us, from Ismail ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Atiyya, from his grandmother Umm Atiyya, she said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to Madina, he commanded the women of the Ansar and they were gathered in a house. Then he sent Umar to them; Umar came, greeted us and said: I am the messenger of the Messenger of Allah to you. We said: Welcome to the messenger of the Messenger of Allah. He said: I pledge you that you shall not fornicate, nor steal, nor kill your children, nor bring a slander you fabricate between your hands and feet, nor disobey in good. Then he extended his hand from outside the house, and we extended our hands from inside the house. He said: O Allah, bear witness. And he commanded us to bring out in the two Eids the secluded and the menstruating, and we were forbidden from following funerals, and no Friday is upon us."
"288","And from what Abu Umama ibn Sahl ibn Hunayf narrated from Umar. 253 - Nasr ibn Ali narrated to us, he said: Abu Ahmad informed me. And Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Abu Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Ayyash ibn Abi Rabia, from Hakim ibn Hakim ibn Abbad ibn Hunayf, from Abu Umama ibn Sahl, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab wrote to Abu Ubayda ibn al-Jarrah: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah and His Messenger are the patron of one who has no patron, and the maternal uncle is the heir of one who has no heir. And this hadith - we do not know it narrated from Umar except from this angle with this isnad; and it has been narrated from other than Umar; and the best isnad narrated in that from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is this isnad from Umar."
"289","And from what Malik ibn Aws ibn al-Hadathan narrated from Umar. 254 - Ahmad ibn Aban al-Qurashi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyayna narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Malik ibn Aws ibn al-Hadathan, from Umar: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Gold for gold is usury except hand to hand; and wheat for wheat is usury except hand to hand. And this hadith was narrated by a group from al-Zuhri, from Malik ibn Aws ibn al-Hadathan, from Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And we do not know anyone who narrated it from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except Umar ibn al-Khattab with this isnad."
"290","255 - And Ahmad ibn Abda narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyayna narrated to us, from Amr and Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Malik ibn Aws ibn al-Hadathan, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, he said: The properties of Banu al-Nadir were of what Allah granted His Messenger of what the Muslims did not spur upon with horses nor camels; the Messenger of Allah used to withhold for his household a year's provision. And this hadith - we do not know anyone who narrated it from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except Umar ibn al-Khattab; and we do not know for it an isnad from Umar except this isnad."
"291","256 - And Ahmad ibn Thabit al-Jahdari narrated to us, he said: Safwan ibn Isa narrated to us, from Usamah ibn Zayd, from al-Zuhri, from Malik ibn Aws ibn al-Hadathan, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had three exclusive properties: Banu al-Nadir, Khaybar and Fadak. As for Banu al-Nadir, it was reserved for his emergencies, and as for Fadak, it was reserved for wayfarers, and as for Khaybar, the Messenger of Allah divided it into three parts: two parts he distributed among the people, and one part as maintenance for his family, and whatever remained beyond his family's maintenance he reserved or placed among the poor of the Muhajirun. And this hadith, we do not know anyone who narrated it from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, and none narrated it from him except Malik ibn Aws ibn al-Hadathan."
"292","And from what Alqamah ibn Waqqas al-Laythi narrated from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him: 257 - Abu al-Hasan Muhammad ibn Ayyub ibn Habib ibn Yahya al-Raqqi al-Samut informed us, he said: Ahmad ibn Amr ibn Abd al-Khaliq al-Bazzar narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abd al-Malik al-Qurashi narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said al-Ansari narrated to us, he said: I heard Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-Taymi narrating from Alqamah ibn Waqqas al-Laythi, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Actions are only by intentions, and every man shall have only what he intended. So whoever's migration was to Allah and His Messenger, his migration is to Allah and His Messenger, and whoever's migration was to a worldly gain he attains or a woman he marries, his migration is to what he migrated to. Abu Bakr said: And this hadith has been narrated from Yahya ibn Said by a large group, among them Amr ibn al-Harith, Malik ibn Anas, Sufyan al-Thawri, and Abd al-Wahhab among a large group, and we do not know this wording narrated except from Umar ibn al-Khattab, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with this chain. And Alqamah ibn Waqqas narrated another hadith narrated by Hammad ibn Salamah, from Yahya ibn Said, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim, from Alqamah, from Umar; the trustworthy among Hammad's companions do not raise it, and a man raised it, so we did not mention it since the trustworthy do not raise it."
"293","Asim ibn Umar from his father: 258 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Jahdam narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Jafar narrated to us, he said: Umarah ibn Ghaziyyah narrated to us, from Khubayb ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Yasaf, from Hafs ibn Asim, from his father, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When the mu'adhdhin says: Allah is Greatest, Allah is Greatest, and one of you says: Allah is Greatest, Allah is Greatest, then he says: I bear witness that there is no god but Allah, he says: I bear witness that there is no god but Allah, he says: I bear witness that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah, he says: I bear witness that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah, he says: Come to prayer, he says: There is no might nor power except by Allah, he says: Come to success, he says: There is no might nor power except by Allah, then he says: Allah is Greatest, Allah is Greatest, there is no god but Allah, he says: Allah is Greatest, Allah is Greatest, there is no god but Allah, from his heart, he will enter Paradise. And this hadith, we do not know it narrated from Umar except by this route with this chain."
"294","259 - Muhammad ibn al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Urwah narrated to us, from his father, from Asim ibn Umar, from his father."
"295","260 - And Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated it to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Dawud narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Asim ibn Umar ibn al-Khattab, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: When the night approaches from here and the day retreats from here and the sun has set, the fasting person has broken his fast. And this hadith, we do not know it narrated from Umar except by this route with this chain, and its chain is sound. And Ibn Abi Awfa narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) something similar, so we mentioned it from Umar due to the eminence of Umar and the soundness of its chain."
"296","Ubaydullah ibn Umar from his father: 261 - Mahmud ibn Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, from Isa ibn al-Mukhtar, from Ibn Abi Layla, and he is Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ikrimah ibn Khalid, from Abu Bakr ibn Ubaydullah ibn Umar, from his father, from Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: For the nose when its amputation is complete, the full blood-money; for the eye fifty, for the hand fifty, for the leg fifty, for the penetrating wound a third of a life, for the bone-displacing wound fifteen, for the bone-exposing wound five, for the tooth five, and for each finger among those, ten ten. Abu Bakr said: And this hadith, we do not know it narrated from Umar except by this route with this chain, and we do not know Ikrimah ibn Khalid narrated from Abu Bakr ibn Ubaydullah except this hadith."
"297","262 - Ibrahim ibn Said narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said al-Umawi narrated to us, from Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Asim ibn Ubaydullah, from his father, from Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I bear witness that there is no god but Allah and I bear witness that I am the Messenger of Allah, and I bear witness that no one says it from the truth of his heart except Allah shields him from the heat of the Fire. And this hadith, Jarir narrated it from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Asim ibn Ubaydullah, from his father, from Umar."
"298","263 - Muhammad ibn Abd al-Malik narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Abdullah narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Asim ibn Ubaydullah, from his father, or his uncle, from Umar ibn al-Khattab: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed ablution and wiped over the two leather socks."
"299","Aslam the freedman of Umar from Umar: 264 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Khalid ibn Athmah narrated to us, he said: Malik ibn Anas narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, he said: I heard Umar saying: We went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on one of his journeys, and when we were partway, I spoke to the Messenger of Allah and he was silent, then I spoke to the Messenger of Allah and he was silent, then I spoke to the Messenger of Allah and he was silent, so I moved my mount aside and said: You asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) three times, each time he does not speak to you; how deserving you are that Quran be revealed concerning you. I had barely waited when I heard a caller calling me, and he said to me: O son of al-Khattab, there was revealed upon me tonight a surah more beloved to me than everything the sun has risen upon, then he recited: Indeed We have granted you a clear victory."
"300","265 - And al-Fadl ibn Sahl narrated it to us, he said: Abu Nuh Abd al-Rahman ibn Ghazwan narrated to us, he said: Malik narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, from Umar, he said: We went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on one of his journeys, and when we were partway, and he mentioned something similar. And this hadith, we do not know it narrated from Umar except by this route, and we do not know anyone narrated it from Zayd ibn Aslam except Malik, and none narrated it from Malik except Muhammad ibn Khalid ibn Athmah and Abd al-Rahman ibn Ghazwan."
"301","266 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Malik ibn Anas narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, he said: I heard Umar ibn al-Khattab saying: I gave a horse in the path of Allah, then the one who had it wasted it, so I wanted to buy it, so I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about that, and he said: Do not buy it, even if he gives it to you for one dirham, for the one who returns in his gift is like the dog returning in its vomit. And this hadith has been narrated by more than one from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, from Umar. And none of them mentioned 'the one who returns in his gift is like the dog returning in its vomit' except Malik, and it has been narrated from Ibn Abbas and from Abu Hurayrah, so we mentioned it from Umar due to the eminence of Umar and the excellence of its chain."
"302","267 - Umar ibn al-Khattab al-Sijistani narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn al-Mundhir narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sadaqah al-Fadaki narrated to us, he said: Malik narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, from Umar, he said: A letter was written to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he said to Abdullah ibn Arqam: Answer these people. So Abdullah ibn Arqam took it and wrote it, then brought the letter and presented it to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: You have done well. That weighed in my soul until I was appointed, so I placed him over the treasury. And this hadith, we do not know anyone narrated it from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, from Umar, except Malik."
"303","268 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna and Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, they said: Abu Amir narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Sad narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, from Umar, that he said: Why now the trotting and the baring of the shoulders, when Allah has brought Islam and removed disbelief and its people, yet we do not abandon anything that was at the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And this hadith, we do not know it narrated except from Umar with this chain."
"304","269 - Ibrahim ibn Ziyad al-Saigh narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Numayr narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Sad narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, from Umar: that a man at the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was named Abdullah, nicknamed Himar, who used to make the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) laugh, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had flogged him for drink, then one day he was brought and he flogged him, so a man from the people said: O Allah curse him, how often he is brought. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not curse him, for he loves Allah and His Messenger. And this hadith, we do not know anyone narrated it from Hisham ibn Sad except Abdullah ibn Numayr, and al-Layth ibn Sad narrated it from Khalid ibn Yazid, from Said ibn Abi Hilal, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, from Umar also."
"305","270 - Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahim narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym al-Fadl ibn Dukayn narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Sad narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded us to give charity, and that coincided with wealth I had, so I said: Today I will outstrip Abu Bakr if I ever outstrip him a day, so I brought half my wealth. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What have you left for your family? I said: The like of it. And Abu Bakr came with all he had, so he said: What have you left for your family? He said: Allah and His Messenger. So I said: I will never race you in anything ever. And this hadith, we do not know anyone narrated it from Hisham ibn Sad, from Zayd, from his father, from Umar, except Abu Nuaym. And Hisham ibn Sad, Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi, al-Layth ibn Sad, Abdullah ibn Wahb, al-Walid ibn Muslim and a large group of scholars narrated from him, and we did not see anyone withhold from his hadith nor impute to it a defect requiring withholding from his hadith."
"306","271 - al-Fadl ibn Sahl narrated to us, he said: Abu Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Sad narrated to us, from Zayd, from his father, from Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to give me gifts, and I would say: If only you gave it to one more needy than me. So he would say: O Umar, whatever Allah gives you of this wealth without asking and without aspiring of the soul, eat and give in charity. He said: And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent something to me and I returned it, so when I came to him he said: What carried you to return what I sent to you? I said: O Messenger of Allah, you have said: It is better for you not to ask people anything. He said: That is only about asking people; whatever comes to you without asking is provision Allah has provided you. And this hadith, similar wording has been narrated from Umar by more than one route, and we do not know anyone narrated this hadith from Zayd, from his father, from Umar, except Hisham ibn Sad."
"307","272 - Muhammad ibn Uthman al-Thaqafi narrated to us, he said: Umayyah ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Sad narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, that Umar ibn al-Khattab said to a freedman of his called Hunayy: O Hunayy, admit the owner of the small herd and the small flock of sheep, and leave me alone from the sheep of Ibn Affan and Ibn Awf, for if their livestock perish they return to wealth, and if the owner of the small flock and small herd perishes he comes saying: O Umar, O Umar. And water and pasture are easier upon me; they are their wealth over which they fought in the Jahiliyyah and upon which they embraced Islam in Islam."
"308","273 - Yahya ibn Qatan al-Ubulli narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Hunayni narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Sad narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and asked him, so he said: I have nothing to give you, but borrow until something comes to us and we give you. So Umar said: Allah has not burdened you with this; you gave what you had, and when you have nothing, you are not burdened. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) disliked Umar's remark until it was seen in his face, so the man said: O Messenger of Allah, may my father and mother be for you, give and do not fear diminishment from the Lord of the Throne. He said: So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) smiled and said: With this I have been commanded. Abu Bakr said: This hadith, we do not know it narrated from Umar except by this route with this chain, and we do not know anyone narrated it from Hisham ibn Sad except Ishaq ibn Ibrahim, and he was not a memorizer."
"309","274 - Salamah ibn Shabib narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ayan narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Zayd ibn Aslam narrated to us, from his father, from his grandfather, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Every lineage and cause will be cut off on the Day of Resurrection except my lineage and my cause. And this hadith has been narrated by more than one from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Umar as mursal, and we do not know anyone who said: from Zayd, from his father, except Abdullah ibn Zayd alone."
"310","275 - Muhammad ibn Sahl ibn Askar and al-Husayn ibn Mahdi narrated to us, and the wording is al-Husayn ibn Mahdi's, they said: Abd al-Razzaq informed us, he said: Mamar informed us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, from Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Season with olive oil and anoint with it, for it comes from a blessed tree. And this hadith, we do not know it narrated from Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), except by this route, and none narrated it from Zayd except Mamar and Ziyad ibn Sad. And more than one narrated it from Abd al-Razzaq, from Mamar, from Zayd, from his father, and I know it only from Umar. And others narrated it without doubt. And this wording has been narrated from Abu Usayd and from Abu Hurayrah, and both their chains are not established."
"311","276 - Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from Malik, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, he said: Umar said: Were it not that people become one class, no village conquered would be left except I would divide it as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) divided the spoils of Khaybar."
"312","277 - And Muhammad ibn Miskin narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Jafar ibn Abi Kathir narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, he said: Umar said to the Stone: I kiss you and I know you are a stone, and were it not that I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) kissing you I would not kiss you."
"313","278 - And Ahmad ibn Sinan narrated it to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Warqa informed us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, from Umar, similar. And this hadith has been narrated by various routes from Umar."
"314","279 - al-Hasan ibn al-Sabbah and Muhammad ibn Rizqullah narrated to us, they said: Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Usamah ibn Zayd, from his father, from his grandfather, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Would you like me to inform you of the beginning of my Islam? He said: We said: Yes. He said: I was the harshest of people against the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). While I was on a severely hot day on one of the roads of Mecca, a man of Quraysh saw me and said: Where are you going, O son of al-Khattab? I said: I want this man. He said: O son of al-Khattab, this matter has entered your house while you say this. I said: What is that? He said: Your sister has gone to him. He said: So I returned angry until I knocked on her door. And when one who had nothing embraced Islam, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would join one or two men to a man to spend on him. He said: And he had joined two of his companions to my sister's husband. He said: So I knocked on the door, and it was said to me: Who is this? I said: I am Umar ibn al-Khattab. And they were reading a book in their hands, so when they heard my voice they stood and hid in a place and left the book. So when my sister opened the door for me I said: O enemy of yourself, have you become a Sabian? He said: And I raised something and struck her head with it. So the woman wept and said to me: O son of al-Khattab, do what you were going to do, for I have embraced Islam. So I went and sat on the bed, and there was a sheet in the middle of the doorway, so I said: What is this sheet here? She said to me: Leave us alone, O son of al-Khattab, for you do not wash from janabah nor purify yourself, and none touches this except the purified. I kept at her until she gave it to me, and in it was: In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate. So when I read 'the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate' I remembered from where it is derived, then I returned to myself and read in the sheet: Whatever is in the heavens and earth glorifies Allah, and He is the Exalted, the Wise. So whenever I passed a name of Allah's names I remembered Allah, so I threw the sheet from my hand. He said: Then I returned to myself and read in it: Whatever is in the heavens and earth glorifies Allah, and He is the Exalted, the Wise, until I reached: Believe in Allah and His Messenger and spend from what He made you stewards of. He said: I said: I bear witness that there is no god but Allah, and I bear witness that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah. So the people came out hastening and uttered takbir in gladness at that. Then they said to me: Rejoice, O son of al-Khattab, for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) supplicated on Monday and said: O Allah, strengthen the religion by the more beloved of these two men to You, either Umar ibn al-Khattab or Abu Jahl ibn Hisham, and I hope the supplication of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is for you. So I said: Guide me to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), where is he? So when they recognized sincerity from me they guided me to him in the house he was in. So I came until I knocked on the door, and he said: Who is this? And I said: Umar ibn al-Khattab, and they knew my harshness against the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and did not know of my Islam, so none of them dared to open for me until the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to them: Open for him; if Allah intends good for him He will guide him. He said: So the door was opened for me, and two men seized my arms until I drew near the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to them: Release him. So they released me. So I sat before him, and he took the folds of my shirt then said: Embrace Islam, O son of al-Khattab; O Allah, guide him. So I said: I bear witness that there is no god but Allah and that you are the Messenger of Allah. He said: So the Muslims uttered a takbir heard in the roads of Mecca. He said: And they had been seventy before that, and when a man embraced Islam and people knew of him they would beat him and he would beat them. He said: So I came to a man and knocked on his door, and he said: Who is this? I said: Umar ibn al-Khattab. So he came out to me and I said to him: Do you know that I have become a Sabian? He said: Did you do it? I said: Yes. He said: Do not do it. He said: And he entered the house and shut the door against me. He said: So I went to another man of Quraysh and called him, so he came out and I said to him: Do you know that I have become a Sabian? So he said: Did you do it? I said: Yes. He said: Do not do it, and he entered the house and shut the door against me. So I said: This is nothing. He said: And there I was, neither beaten nor anything said to me. So the man said: Would you like your Islam to be known? He said: I said: Yes. He said: When people sit in al-Hijr, come to so-and-so and say to him between you and him: Do you know that I have become a Sabian, for he seldom keeps anything secret. So I came to him while people had gathered in al-Hijr, and I said to him between me and him: Do you know that I have become a Sabian? He said: So he said: Did you do it? He said: I said: Yes. He said: So he called at the top of his voice: Lo, Umar has become a Sabian. He said: So those people rushed at me and kept beating me and I beating them until my maternal uncle came. It was said to him: Umar has become a Sabian. So he stood at al-Hijr and called at the top of his voice: Lo, I have granted protection to my nephew, let none touch him. He said: So they left me. So I could not wish to see a Muslim beaten but I saw him. So I said: This is nothing; people are beaten while I am not beaten and nothing is said to me. So when people sat in al-Hijr I came to my uncle and said: Listen, your protection is returned upon you. He said: Do not do it. He said: But I refused. So I kept being beaten and beating until Allah made Islam prevail. And this hadith, we do not know anyone narrated it from Usamah ibn Zayd, from his father, from his grandfather, from Umar, except Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Hunayni. And we do not know narrated concerning Umar's Islam a chain better than this chain, though al-Hunayni, we have mentioned that he left Medina and was restrained and his hadith became confused."
"315","280 - al-Fadl ibn Sahl al-Karkhi and Ahmad ibn al-Walid narrated to us, they said: Muhammad ibn al-Hasan al-Makhzumi narrated to us, he said: Usamah ibn Zayd ibn Aslam narrated to me, from his father, from his grandfather, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Cool the prayer when heat is intense, for intense heat is from the exhalation of Hell. And Hell said: Part of me has eaten part of me, so it sought permission from Allah for two breaths, and He permitted it. So intense heat is from the exhalation of Hell, and intense cold is from its zamharir. And this hadith, we do not know it narrated from Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), except by this route. And Muhammad ibn al-Hasan narrated it from Usamah, from his father, from his grandfather. And Muhammad ibn al-Hasan is rejected in hadith, though his hadith has been tolerated."
"316","281 - Abdullah ibn Shabib narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Muhammad al-Farwi narrated to us, he said: Usamah ibn Zayd ibn Aslam narrated to us, from his father, from Aslam the freedman of Umar, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood at Mecca offering himself to the Arab tribes tribe by tribe during the season, finding none answering what he called to, until this clan of the Ansar came to him, when Allah made them fortunate and drove honor to them, so they sheltered and helped. So may Allah reward them well on behalf of their Prophet. By Allah, we have not fulfilled to them as we covenanted with them; we said to them: We are the rulers and you are the ministers. And if I remain until the head of the year, no worker of mine shall remain except an Ansari. And this hadith, we do not know it narrated from Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), except by this route, and its chain is fair."
"317","282 - Ibrahim ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Khidash ibn Ajlan narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Zayd ibn Aslam narrated to us, from his father, from his grandfather, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, he said: I entered upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and there was a black boy massaging his back, so I asked him and he said: The she-camel bolted with me. And this hadith is not narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except from Umar, from him. And none narrated it from Umar except Aslam. And Hisham ibn Sad and Abdullah ibn Zayd narrated it from Zayd."
"318","283 - Abdullah ibn Shabib narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Muhammad al-Farwi narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Zayd ibn Aslam narrated to us, from his father, from his grandfather, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Islam will prevail until horses wade seas, and until merchants travel across the sea. Then there will appear a people reciting the Quran saying: Who recites better than us? Who is more learned than us? Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: And is there any good in those? They said: Allah and His Messenger know best. He said: Those are the fuel of the Fire; those are from you, from this nation."
"319","284 - Muhammad ibn Isa and Abdullah ibn Shabib narrated to us, they said: Ishaq ibn Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Zayd ibn Aslam narrated to us, from his father, from his grandfather, he said: I heard Umar ibn al-Khattab saying: We had waited long for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to come to us, and the Ansar used to go out to the back of al-Harrah and sit until the day rose high, then when the day rose and the sun grew hot they returned to their homes. Umar said: And we were waiting for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when a man of the Jews gestured upon one of their fortresses and shouted at the top of his voice: O company of Arabs, this is your companion whom you await. Umar said: And I heard the commotion among Banu Amr ibn Awf, so I went out of the door, and there were the Muslims wearing arms. So I set out with the people at noon, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took to the right until he alighted among Banu Amr ibn Awf."
"320","285 - Muhammad ibn Umar ibn Ali al-Muqaddami narrated to us, he said: Umayyah ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Sad narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, from Umar, he said: I saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) exacting retribution upon himself. And this hadith, we do not know anyone narrated it from Hisham ibn Sad except Umayyah ibn Khalid, and we heard it only from Muhammad ibn Umar. And it has been narrated from Umar by another route not established. And it is narrated from al-Fadl ibn Abbas concerning retribution, and it is not established."
"321","286 - Zuhayr ibn Muhammad ibn Qumayr narrated to us, he said: Husayn ibn Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Abu Mashar narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, and from Umar ibn Abdullah the freedman of Ghufrah, they said: Wealth from Bahrain came to Abu Bakr, so he said: Whoever has a promise from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), let him come and take it. He said: So Jabir ibn Abdullah came and said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) promised me, saying: When wealth from Bahrain comes to me I will give you like this and like this and like this three times, both handfuls. He said: Take with your hands. So he took with his hands and found it five hundred. He said: Return to it, then he gave him the like of it. Then he distributed the rest among the people, and each one got ten dirhams. The next year more wealth came, so he distributed among them and each man got twenty dirhams, and surplus remained. So he said to the people: O people, surplus remains of this wealth, and you have servants attending to you and working for you; if you wish we allocate to them. So he allocated to them five dirhams five dirhams. They said: O caliph of the Messenger of Allah, if only you preferred the Muhajirun. He said: Their reward is upon Allah; these are livelihoods; equality in them is better than preference. When Abu Bakr died, may Allah be pleased with him, Umar was made caliph, may Allah be pleased with him, and Allah granted him conquests and more wealth came. So he said: Abu Bakr had a view on this wealth and I have another view; I will not make one who fought the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) like one who fought with him. So he preferred the Muhajirun and Ansar. So he assigned to those of them who witnessed Badr five thousand five thousand, and whoever embraced Islam before the people of Badr he assigned four thousand four thousand. And he assigned to the wives of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) twelve thousand, to each woman, except Safiyyah and Juwayriyah he assigned to each six thousand six thousand, but they refused to take it. So he said: I assigned to them due to migration. They said: You did not assign due to migration; you assigned due to their place with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and we have the like of their place. So he saw that and made them equal. And he assigned to al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib twelve thousand for his kinship to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And he assigned to Usamah ibn Zayd four thousand, and to al-Hasan and al-Husayn five thousand five thousand, joining them to their father for their kinship to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And he assigned to Abdullah ibn Umar three thousand. So he said: O father, you assigned to Usamah ibn Zayd four thousand and to me three thousand? Neither his father had merit his father lacked over you, nor he had merit he lacked over me. So he said: His father was more beloved to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) than your father, and he was more beloved to the Messenger of Allah than you. And he assigned to the sons of the Muhajirun and Ansar among those who witnessed Badr two thousand two thousand. Then Umar ibn Abi Salamah passed by him, so he said: Add a thousand for him, boy. So Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Jahsh said: Why do you add for him over us? His father had no merit our fathers lacked. He said: I assigned to him two thousand for Abu Salamah and added a thousand for Umm Salamah; if you have a mother like Umm Salamah I will add a thousand for you. And he assigned to the people of Mecca eight hundred. And he assigned to Uthman ibn Abdullah ibn Uthman, and he is the nephew of Talhah ibn Ubaydullah, meaning Uthman ibn Abdullah, eight hundred. And he assigned to the son of al-Nadr ibn Anas two thousand dirhams. So Talhah ibn Ubaydullah said to him: The son of Uthman like him came to you and you assigned eight hundred, and a boy of the Ansar came and you assigned among two thousand. So he said: I met this one's father on Uhud; he asked me about the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so I said: I see him killed. So he drew his sword and bared his forearm and said: If the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) has been killed, Allah is Living and does not die. So he fought until killed, while this one herds sheep; do you want me to make them equal? So Umar acted upon this all his life until in the last year in which he performed Hajj, some people said: If the Commander of the Believers dies we will appoint so-and-so, meaning Talhah ibn Ubaydullah, and they said: The pledge of Abu Bakr was a slip. So he wanted to speak in the middle of the days of Tashriq at Mina, so Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf said to him: O Commander of the Believers, this gathering is dominated by the rabble who cannot bear your speech; delay until you come to the land of migration where your companions and the abode of faith and the Muhajirun and Ansar are, then speak your speech so your speech is borne. He said: So he hastened until he came to Medina, then went out on Friday, praised Allah and extolled Him, then said: It has reached me the saying of one of you: If Umar dies or if the Commander of the Believers dies we will appoint so-and-so and pledge him, and the rule of Abu Bakr was a slip. Yes, by Allah, it was a slip. Whence do we have the like of Abu Bakr to stretch our necks to as we stretched to Abu Bakr. And Abu Bakr saw a view and I saw a view; Abu Bakr saw to divide equally and I saw to prefer. If I live to this year I shall return to Abu Bakr's view, for his view is better than mine. I have seen a dream, and I see it only as nearness of my term: I saw as if a red rooster pecked me three pecks, and Asma interpreted it saying: A non-Arab slave will kill you. If I perish, your affair is to these six with whom the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died while pleased with them: Uthman ibn Affan, Ali ibn Abi Talib, Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam, Talhah ibn Ubaydullah, and Sad ibn Malik. And if I live I shall make a covenant you will not perish by. Lo, stoning: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stoned and we stoned after him. Were it not that you say Umar wrote what is not in Allah's Book I would have written it. We read in Allah's Book: The old man and old woman if they commit adultery, stone them outright as exemplary punishment from Allah, and Allah is Mighty, Wise. Then I looked at the paternal aunt and brother's daughter and did not make them heirs. If I live I shall open for you a path you recognize, and if I perish Allah is my Caliph and you choose your view. I have registered the diwan and founded the garrison cities. I fear for you only two men: a man who interprets the Quran on other than its interpretation and fights over it, and a man who sees himself more deserving of rule than his fellow and fights over it. He spoke this speech on Friday and died, may Allah be pleased with him, on Wednesday. And this hadith, similar wording has been narrated from Umar concerning his killing by various routes, and we do not know it narrated from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, in this completeness except from the hadith of Abu Mashar, from Zayd, from his father."
"322","287 - Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Shabbuwayh and Muhammad ibn Miskin narrated to us, they said: Said ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Mutarrif Abu Ghassan narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, from Umar ibn al-Khattab: that he said: Captives were brought to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and there was a woman among the captives milking her breast; whenever she found a boy among the captives she took him, clasped him to her belly and nursed him. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to us: Do you see this woman casting her child into the Fire? We said: No, by Allah, while she can avoid casting him. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah is more merciful to His servant than this woman to her child. He said: And it reached me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was on one of his expeditions; while they marched they took a bird's chick, then one of its parents came until it fell into the hands of the one who took the chick. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do you marvel at this bird: its chick was taken, so it came until it fell into their hands. By Allah, Allah is more merciful to His creation than this bird to its chick. And this hadith, we do not know it narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except by Umar, and we know no path from Umar except this path, and none narrated it from Zayd except Muhammad ibn Mutarrif."
"323","288 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abi Adi and Abu Amir narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Abi Humayd, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"324","289 - And Muhammad ibn Marzuq narrated it to us, he said: al-Minhal ibn Bahr narrated to us, he said: Hisham al-Dastuwai narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, from Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said: Inform me of the greatest of creation in station with Allah on the Day of Resurrection? They said: The angels. He said: And what prevents them, with their nearness to their Lord; rather others. They said: The prophets. He said: And what prevents them, while revelation descends upon them; rather others. They said: Then inform us, O Messenger of Allah. He said: A people coming after you believing in me though they never saw me, and finding hanging sheets they believe in them; those are the greatest of creation in station and those are the greatest of creation in faith with Allah on the Day of Resurrection. And this hadith, we do not know it narrated from Umar except by this route. And the hadith of al-Minhal ibn Bahr, from Hisham al-Dastuwai, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, from Umar, the memorizers, the trustworthy, only narrate it from Hisham, from Yahya, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Umar as mursal. And this hadith is only known from the hadith of Muhammad ibn Abi Humayd. And Muhammad is a man of Medina, not strong; a group of trustworthy narrated from him and tolerated his hadith. He narrated this hadith from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, from Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And he also narrated another not corroborated upon."
"325","290 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abi Adi and Abu Amir narrated to us, they said: Muhammad ibn Abi Humayd narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, from Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Shall I not inform you of the best of your rulers and the worst of them? They said: Yes. He said: The best of your rulers are those whom you love and who love you, and the worst of your rulers are those whom you hate and who hate you."
"326","291 - Ibrahim ibn Said narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Uways narrated to us, he said: Zayd ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Zayd narrated to us, from his father, from his grandfather, from Aslam, he said: Umar said: Whom did you accompany on this journey of yours? I said: A people of Bakr ibn Wail. He said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Your Bakri brother, so do not trust him. And this hadith, we do not know it narrated from Umar except by this route, and in it are two men whose hadith is weak: one Zayd ibn Abd al-Rahman, the other Abd al-Rahman ibn Zayd, and he is very rejected in hadith."
"327","292 - Abu Kurayb narrated to us, he said: Rishdin ibn Sad narrated to us, he said: al-Dahhak ibn Shurahbil narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, from Umar: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed ablution once once. And this hadith is mistaken, and I reckon its mistake came from al-Dahhak ibn Shurahbil; Rishdin ibn Sad and Abdullah ibn Lahiah narrated it from him, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, from Umar. And the correct is what the trustworthy narrated from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Ibn Abbas."
"328","293 - al-Jarrah ibn Makhlad narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn Anbasah narrated to us, from Ali ibn Hashim ibn al-Barid, from Muhammad ibn Ubaydullah ibn Abi Rafi, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, from Umar, he said: I saw al-Hasan and al-Husayn, may Allah's mercy be upon them, upon the shoulders of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so I said: What an excellent steed beneath you. He said: And what excellent riders they are. And none narrated it from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except Umar ibn al-Khattab with this chain. And Muhammad ibn Ubaydullah ibn Abi Rafi, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, from Umar, was not corroborated upon."
"329","And from what Said ibn al-Musayyib narrated from Umar: 294 - Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, Yazid ibn Zuray narrated to us, he said: Husayn the teacher narrated to us, from Amr ibn Shuayb, from Said ibn al-Musayyib: that two brothers disputed, and each swore not to speak to his brother. So they came to Umar, and he said to one of them: Expiate your oath and speak to your brother. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no vow in disobedience nor in severing kinship."
"330","295 - Ibrahim ibn Hani narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Bilal narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Bashir narrated to us, from Matar, from Amr ibn Shuayb, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, from Umar: that a man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: My father wants to take my wealth. He said: You and your wealth belong to your father. And this hadith, we do not know it narrated from Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), except by this route. And others than Matar narrated it from Amr ibn Shuayb, from his father, from his grandfather."
"331","296 - Ibrahim ibn Abdullah ibn al-Junayd narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abdullah ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Lahiah narrated to us, from Bukayr ibn Abdullah, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, from Umar, he said: We campaigned with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in Ramadan, the conquest campaign and the Badr campaign, and we broke fast in both. And this hadith, we do not know it narrated from Umar except by this route with this chain."
"332","297 - Muhammad ibn Ali ibn al-Hasan ibn Shaqiq narrated to us, he said: al-Nadr ibn Shumayl narrated to us, he said: Abu Qurrah narrated to us, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever recites in a night: So whoever hopes for the meeting of his Lord, let him do righteous work and associate none in his Lord's worship, there shall be for him light from Aden Abyan to Mecca, its filling angels. And this hadith, we do not know it narrated from Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), except by this route with this chain."
"333","298 - Ibrahim ibn Hani al-Naysaburi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Ghaffar ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahiah narrated to us, from Amr ibn Shuayb, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, from Umar, he said: The first woman to seek khul in Islam was Habibah bint Sahl; she was under Thabit ibn Qays ibn Shammas. So she came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, neither I nor Thabit. So he said to her: Will you return to him what you took from him? She said: Yes. And he had married her for a garden of palms. So Thabit said: Does that please, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Yes. And he assigned her neither maintenance nor lodging. And this hadith with this wording, we do not know it narrated from Umar except with this chain. And it has been narrated from Ibn Abbas and others concerning Thabit ibn Qays and his wife's khul with differing wordings."
"334","299 - Ibrahim ibn Hani narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Sallam al-Attar narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Sabrah narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Said, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, he said: Sabigh al-Tamimi came to Umar ibn al-Khattab and said: O Commander of the Believers, inform me about: By the scattering winds. He said: They are the winds, and were it not that I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying it I would not say it. He said: Then inform me about: By the carriers of burden. He said: They are the clouds, and were it not that I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying it I would not say it. He said: Then inform me about: By the distributors of command. He said: They are the angels, and were it not that I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying it I would not say it. He said: Then inform me about: By the runners smoothly. He said: They are the ships, and were it not that I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying it I would not say it. He said: Then he ordered him struck a hundred and placed him in a house. When he healed he called him and struck him another hundred and carried him upon a saddle and wrote to Abu Musa al-Ashari: Prevent people from sitting with him. So he remained so until he came to Abu Musa and swore to him with emphasized oaths he found nothing in himself of what he used to find. So he wrote concerning that to Umar, so Umar wrote: I reckon him truthful, so leave him to sit with people. And this hadith, we do not know it narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by any route except this route. And it came only from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Sabrah, I reckon, for Abu Bakr is weak in hadith and Said ibn Sallam was not of the hadith folk. And I only mentioned this hadith since I did not memorize it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except by this route, so I mentioned it and clarified its defect."
"335","And from what Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd al-Qari narrated from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him: 300 - Salamah ibn Shabib narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, he said: Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from al-Miswar, and Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd al-Qari, from Umar: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The Quran was revealed upon seven letters. And this hadith, its chain is fair. And we do not know it narrated from Umar except by this route. And this wording has been narrated from Ubayy, Hudhayfah, Abu Hurayrah and others, so we mentioned it from Umar due to Umar's eminence and its chain's fairness."
"336","301 - Zuhayr ibn Muhammad ibn Qumayr and al-Husayn ibn Mahdi narrated to us, they said: Abd al-Razzaq informed us, he said: Yunus ibn Sulaym informed us, from Abu Bakr meaning Yunus ibn Yazid, he said: al-Zuhri narrated to us, from Urwah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd al-Qari, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, he said: When revelation descended upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), a humming like the humming of bees was heard at his face. So it descended upon him and we fell silent until it lifted from him. Then he faced the qiblah and raised his hands, then said: O Allah, increase us and do not decrease us, honor us and do not disgrace us, give us and do not deprive us, favor us and do not disfavor against us, and be pleased with us. And this hadith, we do not know it narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with this wording except from Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with this chain."
"337","302 - Muhammad ibn Miskin narrated to us, he said: Said ibn al-Hakam narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yunus ibn Yazid informed us, from Ibn Shihab, he said: al-Saib ibn Yazid and Ubaydullah meaning Ibn Abdullah narrated to me, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd al-Qari, he said: I heard Umar ibn al-Khattab saying, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever sleeps past his portion of the night then recites it between the Fajr prayer and the Zuhr prayer, it is written for him as if he recited it in the night. And this hadith, we do not know it narrated with this wording except from Umar, and its chain is sound."
"338","And from what Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr ibn Harithah al-Ansari narrated from Umar: 303 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Abi al-Wazir narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Ishaq ibn al-Mustawrid, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr ibn Harithah al-Ansari: that Umar used to come to Quba mosque on Monday and Thursday. One day he came and found none of the people in it, so he said: Why do I see none of the people in this mosque? He said: By Him in Whose Hand is my soul, I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and Abu Bakr and some of his companions while we carried its stones upon our bellies, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) himself founded it with his hand, with Jibril, peace be upon him, directing him to the Kabah. And this hadith, we do not know it narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except by this route with this chain."
"339","And from what Abdullah ibn Suraqah narrated from Umar: 304 - Salih ibn Muadh Abu Bishr narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Muhammad narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abdullah ibn al-Hadi, from al-Walid ibn Abi al-Walid, from Uthman ibn Abdullah ibn Suraqah, from his father, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever shades a warrior or shades the head of a warrior, the doubt is from Abu Bakr, Allah will shade him on the Day of Resurrection. And whoever builds a mosque for Allah, Allah will build for him a house in Paradise. And this hadith, we do not know it narrated from Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), except by this route with this chain. And some narrated it saying: from Yazid ibn al-Hadi, from Uthman ibn Abdullah ibn Suraqah, from Umar, without saying: from his father."
"340","And from what al-Ahnaf ibn Qays narrated from Umar: 305 - Muhammad ibn Abd al-Malik al-Qurashi narrated to us, he said: Daylam ibn Ghazwan narrated to us, he said: Maymun al-Kurdi narrated to us, from Abu Uthman al-Nahdi, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) warned us against every eloquent hypocrite."
"341","306 - I heard Abu Ghassan Rawh ibn Hatim mentioning from Sulayman ibn Harb, from Hammad ibn Zayd, from Abu Suwayd ibn al-Mughirah, from al-Hasan, from al-Ahnaf, from Umar, similar. And this hadith, we do not know it narrated from Umar except from the hadith of al-Ahnaf and Abu Uthman connected. And Suwayd ibn al-Mughirah is an eminent man of Basra."
"342","And from what Abu Uthman al-Nahdi, whose name is Abd al-Rahman ibn Mull, narrated from Umar: 307 - Abu Kurayb narrated to us, he said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, from Asim, from Abu Uthman, from Umar ibn al-Khattab: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade silk except the measure of two fingers. And this hadith has been narrated from Umar by more than one route."
"343","308 - Muhammad ibn Marzuq ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Imran al-Sadi Abu Hafs narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn al-Hasan, judge of Basra, narrated to us, he said: Said al-Jurayri narrated to us, from Abu Uthman al-Nahdi, he said: I heard Umar ibn al-Khattab saying, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When two Muslim men meet and one greets the other, the more beloved of them to Allah is the better of them in cheer to his companion. And when they shake hands, a hundred mercies descend upon them: ninety for the initiator and ten for the other. And this hadith, we do not know it narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except by this route with this chain, and Umar ibn Imran was not corroborated upon this hadith."
"344","And from what Abu Rafi narrated from Umar: 309 - Muhammad ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Dawud ibn Shabib narrated to us, from Hammad ibn Salamah, from Ali ibn Zayd, from Abu Rafi, from Umar ibn al-Khattab."
"345","310 - And Muhammad ibn Mamar narrated to us, he said: Affan narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Ali ibn Zayd, from Abu Rafi, from Umar: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was at al-Hajun when the idolaters repelled him, so he said: O Allah, show me a sign today; I shall not mind who belies me after it. So he came and it was said to him: Call a tree. So he called a tree, and it came plowing the earth until it reached him and greeted him. Then he commanded it and it returned. Dawud said: to its planting place, and Affan said: to its place. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I shall not mind who belies me after it among my people. And this hadith, we do not know it narrated from Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), except with this chain."
"346","And from what Abu Sinan al-Duali narrated from Umar: 311 - Ibrahim ibn Ziyad al-Saigh narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn Musa narrated to us, from Ibn Lahiah, from Abu al-Aswad: he heard Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Labibah, from Abu Sinan al-Duali: that he entered upon Umar while with him were men of the first Muhajirun. So Umar said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Worldliness does not enter upon a people except Allah casts enmity and hatred among them until the Day of Resurrection. And this hadith, we do not know it narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except by this route with this chain."
"347","And from what Abu al-Aswad al-Dayli narrated from Umar: 312 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Fadl, as I know, narrated to us, he said: Dawud ibn Abi al-Furat narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Buraydah, from Abu al-Aswad al-Dili, from Umar: that a funeral passed by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and they praised it well, so he said: It is due. And another funeral passed by him and they praised it evilly, so he said: It is due. You are Allah's witnesses on earth, or: The believers are Allah's witnesses, meaning on earth. And we do not know this wording narrated from Umar except by this route. And it has been narrated from others than Umar, and Abu al-Aswad narrated from Umar only this hadith."
"348","And from what Said ibn al-Asi narrated from Umar: 313 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Bukayr narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Umar al-Qurashi narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Amr ibn Said narrated to me, from his father: that he heard his father on the day of al-Marj claiming he heard Umar ibn al-Khattab saying: Were it not that I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Allah the Blessed and Exalted will defend this religion by Christians of Rabiah on the bank of the Euphrates, I would not leave a Bedouin except I killed him or he submits. And this hadith, we do not know it narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except from Umar, from him, with this chain."
"349","And from what Madan ibn Abi Talhah narrated from Umar: 314 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Abi Abdullah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Salim ibn Abi al-Jad, from Madan: that Umar ibn al-Khattab addressed on Friday, mentioning the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and mentioning Abu Bakr, then said: I saw a rooster peck me three pecks, and I see it only as the nearness of my term. And some people order me to appoint a successor, yet Allah the Blessed and Exalted would not waste His religion nor His caliphate by which He sent His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). If my affair hastens, the caliphate is consultation among these six with whom the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died while pleased with them. And I know people will impugn this affair; I struck them with this hand of mine for Islam. If they do, those are Allah's enemies, the disbelievers, the strayers. Then I shall not leave after me anything more important to me than the kalalah. And I did not review the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in anything as I reviewed him on the kalalah, nor was he stern to me in anything as he was stern to me in it, until he poked my chest with his finger and said: O Umar, does not suffice you the summer verse at the end of Surat al-Nisa? And if I live I shall judge in it a judgment by which he who reads the Quran and he who does not read the Quran shall judge. O Allah, I call You to witness over the governors of the garrison cities, for I only sent them to teach people their Prophet's sunnah, divide their fay among them, and refer to me what is problematic of their affair. Then you, O people, eat two trees I see only as foul: this onion and garlic. And I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he found their smell from a man in the mosque, order him removed to al-Baqi. So whoever eats them, let him kill them by cooking."
"350","315 - And Ismail ibn Abi al-Harith narrated it to us, he said: Shabbabah narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Salim, from Madan, from Umar, similar. And we do not know Madan narrated from Umar except this hadith, and its chain is sound. And it has been narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on dislike of garlic and onion by various routes; Maqil ibn Yasar, Jabir ibn Abdullah, Abu Hurayrah, Anas ibn Malik and al-Mughirah ibn Shubah narrated that, so we mentioned it from Umar alone."
"351","And from what Ibn al-Simt narrated from Umar: 316 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Khumayr, from Habib ibn Ubayd, from Jubayr ibn Nufayr, from Ibn al-Simt: that he came to a land called Dumin of Hims, about a dozen miles out, and prayed two rakahs. So I said to him: You pray two rakahs? So he said: I saw Umar praying at Dhu al-Hulayfah two rakahs, and he said: I do as I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) doing. And this hadith, we do not know it narrated from Umar except by this route with this chain. And it is narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: A few is between three to nine."
"352","And from what Ibn Abd Kulal narrated from Umar: 317 - Muhammad ibn Miskin narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Bakr narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, from Rashid ibn Sad, from Ibn Abd Kulal, he said: I heard Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Allah will raise from a city in Sham called Hims seventy thousand with no reckoning upon them, between the olive, the wall, and the red barath. And this hadith, we do not know it narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except by this route with this chain. And Ibn Abd Kulal is not known for transmission."
"353","And from what Masruq ibn al-Ajda narrated from Umar: 318 - Muhammad ibn al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Abu Usamah narrated to us, he said: Junayd ibn Abi Wahrah narrated to us, from Mujalid, from al-Shabi, from Masruq. 319 - And al-Fadl ibn Sahl narrated it to us, he said: Abu al-Nadr narrated to us, he said: Abu Aqil al-Thaqafi narrated to us, he said: Mujalid narrated to us, from al-Shabi, from Masruq, he said: I met Umar ibn al-Khattab, so he said: What is your name? So I said: Masruq ibn al-Ajda. So he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: al-Ajda is a devil. You are Masruq ibn Abd al-Rahman. And this hadith, we do not know anyone narrated it from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except Umar, and we know no path from Umar except this path."
"354","320 - Muhammad ibn Mansur al-Tusi, and he was of the best of people, narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Ibrahim ibn Sad narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to me from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Muhammad ibn Said, from Mujalid ibn Said, from al-Shabi, from Masruq. 321 - And he narrated it to us another time from Yaqub, from his father, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Mujalid, from al-Shabi, from Masruq, and he did not insert between Ibn Ishaq and Mujalid anyone. Masruq said: Umar addressed us and said: Do not be excessive in women's dowries, for were that an honor in this world or piety with Allah, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would have been most deserving of it among you. He did not give any of his women more than twelve uqiyyah. And this hadith has been narrated from Umar by more than one route. And we do not know it narrated from Masruq, from Umar, except by this route with this chain."
"355","322 - Muhammad ibn Hashim, nephew of Abd al-Wahid ibn Ghiyath, narrated to us, he said: Sahl ibn Bakkar narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Jabir, from al-Hasan ibn Masruq, from his father, from Umar: that he mentioned the kalalah and said: I did not review the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in anything as I reviewed him on the kalalah, nor was he stern in anything as he was stern to me in it, until he poked my chest with his finger and said: Does not suffice you the summer verse at the end of Surat al-Nisa. And we do not know anyone narrated from al-Hasan ibn Masruq except Jabir, and we only heard this hadith from the hadith of Abu Awanah from Jabir."
"356","And from what Amr ibn Maymun al-Awdi narrated from Umar: 323 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna and Muhammad ibn Bashshar narrated to us, they said: Muhammad ibn Jafar narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Amr ibn Maymun, he said: Umar prayed the dawn at Jam and said: The idolaters would not depart until the sun rose, saying: Shine, Thabir. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) opposed them and departed before sunrise. And this hadith, we know no chain from Umar except this chain. And it has been narrated from Ibn Abbas and others, and Umar is the highest who narrated it from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"357","324 - Abd al-Ala ibn Zayd al-Attar narrated to us, he said: Khallad ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Abi Ishaq narrated to us, from his father, from Amr ibn Maymun, from Umar: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to seek refuge from five: O Allah, I seek refuge in You from miserliness and cowardice, I seek refuge in You from evil old age, I seek refuge in You from the trial of the breast, and I seek refuge in You from the punishment of the grave. And this hadith has been narrated by others than Yunus, from Abu Ishaq, from Amr ibn Maymun, from Abdullah."
"358","And from what Abdullah ibn Khalifah narrated from Umar: 325 - al-Fadl ibn Sahl narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Bukayr narrated to us, he said: Israil narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abdullah ibn Khalifah, from Umar: that a woman came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: Supplicate Allah to admit me to Paradise. So he magnified the Lord, Blessed and Exalted, and said: His Kursi encompasses the heavens and earth, and it creaks like the creaking of a new saddle when ridden, due to His weight. Abu Bakr said: And this hadith, we do not know it narrated with this wording from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except from Umar, from him. And al-Thawri narrated this hadith from Abu Ishaq, from Abdullah ibn Khalifah, from Umar as mawquf. And Abdullah ibn Khalifah narrated no other hadith as musnad, and none narrated it from him except Israil. And none narrated from Abdullah ibn Khalifah except Abu Ishaq. And similar has been narrated from Jubayr ibn Mutim with other than its wording."
"359","Alqamah ibn Qays from Umar: 326 - al-Qasim ibn Bishr ibn Maruf narrated to us, he said: Musab ibn al-Miqdam narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, from Umar: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed a man praying and supplicating, so he said: Ask, you shall be given. And there he was Abdullah ibn Masud."
"360","327 - And Ali ibn al-Mundhir narrated it to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Fudayl narrated to us, he said: al-Amash narrated to us, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, and al-Amash, from Khaythamah, from Qays ibn Marwan, from Umar: that he passed with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) one night while a man recited, so he listened to his recitation. When he sat supplicating he said: Ask, you shall be given."
"361","And al-Hasan ibn Ubaydullah narrated this hadith from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, from Qays or Ibn Qays, from a man of Jufa, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). 328 - Muhammad ibn Abd al-Malik al-Qurashi narrated it to us, from Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad, from al-Hasan ibn Ubaydullah."
"362","And from what Zayd ibn Wahb narrated from Umar ibn al-Khattab: 329 - Ammar ibn Khalid al-Wasiti narrated to us, he said: al-Qasim ibn Malik al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: al-Amash narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Wahb, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, that he said: When you are three on a journey, appoint one of you over you; that is a commander the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) appointed. And this hadith has been narrated by more than one from al-Amash, from Zayd ibn Wahb, from Umar as mawquf. And we do not know anyone raised it except al-Qasim ibn Malik, from al-Amash."
"363","330 - Salamah ibn Shabib narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Abi Hakim narrated to us, from Yasin the oil-seller, from al-Amash, from Zayd ibn Wahb, from Umar, he said: The Adha prayer is two rakahs, the Fitr prayer is two rakahs, the Friday prayer is two rakahs, complete not shortened upon the tongue of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And this hadith, Ibn Abi Layla narrated it from Umar. And Zubayd al-Ayami narrated it; Shubah, al-Thawri and Muhammad ibn Talhah narrated it from Zubayd, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Umar. And Yazid ibn Ziyad ibn Abi al-Jad narrated it from Zubayd, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Kab ibn Ujrah, from Umar. And we do not know it narrated from al-Amash, from Zayd ibn Wahb, from Umar, except from the hadith of Yasin, from al-Amash."
"364","331 - al-Hasan ibn Qazaah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Habib narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Zubayd, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, he said: Umar said: The travel prayer is two rakahs, the Friday prayer is two rakahs, the Fitr prayer is two rakahs, the Adha prayer is two rakahs, complete not shortened upon the tongue of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And this hadith, Yazid ibn Ziyad ibn Abi al-Jad narrated it from Zubayd, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Kab ibn Ujrah, from Umar. And Shubah and al-Thawri did not mention Kab ibn Ujrah, and they are memorizers, while Yazid ibn Ziyad is not a memorizer. And Shubah and al-Thawri narrated it, meaning the hadith of al-Hasan ibn Qazaah."
"365","And from what Qays ibn Abi Hazim narrated from Umar: 332 - Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahim, companion of al-Sabiri, Abu Yahya known as Saiqah, narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Salam ibn Harb narrated to us, from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim: that al-Zubayr sought Umar's permission for jihad, so he said: Sit, for you have fought with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And this is narrated from Umar by another route narrated by Fudayl ibn Marzuq, from Atiyyah, from Ibn Umar, from Umar. And this chain narrated by Ismail, from Qays, is better in chain, though the hadith of Fudayl, from Atiyyah, is higher since from Ibn Umar, from Umar. And Ismail ibn Abi Khalid and Qays need no mention due to their fame. And Abd al-Salam ibn Harb, eminent scholars narrated from him."
"366","333 - Dawud ibn Sulayman Abu Sulayman the tutor narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Jarir narrated to us, from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, from Umar: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to them with silk in one hand and gold in the other, and said: These two are forbidden for the males of my nation, lawful for its females. And this hadith, we do not know anyone narrated it from Ismail, from Qays, from Umar, except Amr ibn Jarir. And Amr is weak in hadith, though his hadith has been tolerated and narrated from him. And this wording has been narrated from others than Umar, and we know no established hadith in that with the transmitters."
"367","And from what Amr ibn Shurahbil narrated from Umar: 334 - al-Fadl ibn Sahl narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Israil narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Amr ibn Shurahbil, from Umar ibn al-Khattab: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered a caller who called: No drunkard shall approach prayer. And this hadith, we do not know it narrated from Umar except by this route, nor is it narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with this wording except from Umar."
"368","335 - Abu Kurayb narrated to us, he said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid, from al-Shabi, and Qays ibn Abi Hazim, he said: Adi ibn Hatim came to Umar while he distributed to people, so he said: O Commander of the Believers, do you not know me? He said: Yes, with the best knowledge I know you: you embraced Islam when they disbelieved, gave when they withheld, fulfilled when they betrayed, and came forward when they turned back. And this hadith has been narrated by more than one from Ismail, from al-Shabi. And we do not know anyone narrated it from Ismail, from Qays and al-Shabi, except Abu Muawiyah, and we heard it only from Abu Kurayb."
"369","336 - Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Hudbah ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from al-Mughirah, from al-Shabi, from Adi ibn Hatim, he said: I came to Umar ibn al-Khattab while he distributed to people. So I came to him from his right and he turned from me, then I came from his left and he turned from me, then I came from before him and said: O Commander of the Believers, do you not know me? He said: Yes, may Allah greet you with the best knowledge: I know you embraced Islam when they disbelieved, gave when they withheld, fulfilled when they betrayed, and came forward when they turned back. And the first charity that whitened the face of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was your people's charity when you brought it carrying to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: So I said: Since you know me, I do not mind. Abu Bakr said: The meaning of his saying 'you embraced Islam when they disbelieved' is that his people apostatized and he did not apostatize; 'you fulfilled when they betrayed,' you fulfilled what was upon you of zakat; 'you gave when they withheld,' when your people withheld zakat you said to them: It is upon me in my wealth."
"370","And from what Hanzalah ibn Nuaym narrated from Umar: 337 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Hasan al-Anazi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghadirah al-Anazi narrated to us, from his uncle al-Ghadban ibn Hanzalah, from his father Hanzalah ibn Nuaym al-Anazi, he said: I heard Umar ibn al-Khattab, mentioning Anazah, saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: A clan wronged, aided. And this hadith, we do not know it narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except from Umar, and we know no path from Umar except this path."
"371","338 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Jarir ibn Hazim, from Ishaq ibn Suwayd, from Ibn Hujayrah, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Beware of sitting in the roadways; if you must do it, give the road its right. It was said: And what is its right? He said: Lowering the gaze and returning greetings, I reckon he said: and guiding the lost. And this hadith, we do not know anyone raised it except Jarir ibn Hazim, from Ishaq ibn Suwayd, and none narrated it from Jarir as musnad except Ibn al-Mubarak. And Hammad ibn Zayd narrated this hadith from Ishaq ibn Suwayd as mursal."
"372","And from what Ibn al-Had narrated from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him: 339 - Muhammad ibn Said ibn Yazid ibn Ibrahim al-Tustari narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn al-Yaman narrated to us, he said: Zamah narrated to us, from Salamah ibn Wahram, from Tawus, from Ibn al-Hadi, from Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah is not shy of the truth; do not come to women in their backs. And this hadith, we do not know it narrated from Umar except by this route with this chain."
"373","And from what Abu Tamim al-Jayshani narrated from Umar: 340 - Bishr ibn Adam narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Haywah narrated to us, from Ibn Hubayrah, from Bakr ibn Amr, from Abu Tamim al-Jayshani, from Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If you relied upon Allah as He deserves, He would provide for you as He provides the birds: they go out hungry and return full. And this hadith, we do not know anyone narrated it from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except Umar ibn al-Khattab with this chain. And I reckon Bakr ibn Amr did not hear from Abu Tamim."
"374","And from what Suwayd ibn Ghafalah narrated from Umar: 341 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Abd al-Ala, from Suwayd ibn Ghafalah, he said: I saw Umar kissing the Stone and saying: I kiss you knowing you are a stone harming nor benefiting, but I saw Abu al-Qasim (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fond of you. And this wording, we do not know it narrated from Umar except from the hadith of Suwayd ibn Ghafalah, from Umar."
"375","And from what Usayr ibn Jabir narrated from Umar: 342 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Muadh ibn Hisham narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to me, from Qatadah, from Zurarah ibn Awfa, from Usayr ibn Jabir, he said: When the reinforcements, the reinforcements of Yemen, came to Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, he would ask them: Is among you Uways ibn Amir? Until he came upon Uways and said: Are you Uways ibn Amir? He said: Yes. He said: From Murad, from Qaran? He said: Yes. He said: Did you have leprosy and healed except a dirham-sized spot? He said: Yes. He said: Do you have a mother? He said: Yes. He said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Uways ibn Amir will come to you with the reinforcements of Yemen, from Murad, from Qaran; he had leprosy and healed except a dirham-sized spot. He has a mother dutiful to her; if he swears upon Allah, He fulfills him. If you can have him seek forgiveness for you, do so. So seek forgiveness for me. So he sought forgiveness for him. So Umar said to him: Where do you intend? He said: Kufa. He said: Shall I not write for you to its governor? He said: To be among the obscure of people is more beloved to me. He said: The next year a man of their nobles performed Hajj and met Umar, so he asked him about Uways. So he said: I left him shabby of house, little of goods. He said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Uways ibn Amir will come to you with the reinforcements of Yemen, from Murad then from Qaran; he had leprosy and healed except a dirham-sized spot. He has a mother dutiful to her; if he swears upon Allah, He fulfills him. If you can have him seek forgiveness for you, do so. So he came to Uways and said: Seek forgiveness for me. He said: You are fresh from a righteous journey, so he sought forgiveness for him. He said: Did you meet Umar? He said: Yes. So he sought forgiveness for him. So people noticed him and he went on his way. Usayr said: And I clothed him a cloak, and whenever a man saw him he said: Whence does Uways have this cloak. And we do not know Usayr ibn Jabir raised from Umar except this hadith. Abu Bakr said: The hadith of Usayr is rejected, though its chain outwardly is fair, it has a defect."
"376","Musnad Uthman ibn Affan, may Allah be pleased with him: What Zayd ibn Thabit narrated from Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him: 343 - Abu al-Abbas Ahmad ibn al-Hasan ibn Ishaq al-Razi informed us, he said: Ahmad ibn Amr ibn Abd al-Khaliq al-Bazzar narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna and Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, they said: Uthman ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: Fulayh ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Said ibn al-Harith, from Kharijah ibn Zayd ibn Thabit, from his father, from Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him: that he performed ablution three times three times, and said: Thus I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) perform ablution. And this hadith, its chain is fair. And we do not know Zayd ibn Thabit narrated from Uthman a musnad hadith except this hadith, nor has it a chain from Zayd ibn Thabit except this chain."
"377","Abdullah ibn Abbas: 344 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna and Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, they said: Yahya ibn Said narrated to us, he said: Awf narrated to us, from Yazid al-Farisi, he said: Ibn Abbas narrated to us, he said: I said to Uthman: What carried you to turn to al-Anfal, though of the Mathani, and to Baraah, though of the Miin, so you joined them and did not write between them the line 'In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate,' placing them in one place; what carried you to that? So Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would have numerous surahs descend upon him; when a verse descended upon him he would say: Place it in the surah in which such and such is mentioned. And al-Anfal was of the first revealed at Medina and Baraah of the last revealed, and their story resembled its story, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not clarify to us in that anything. Hence we joined them and did not write between them the line 'In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate.' And this hadith, we do not know it narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except by this route. And we do not know anyone narrated it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except Uthman, and Ibn Abbas narrated from Uthman only this hadith."
"378","Ibn Umar: 345 - Muhammad ibn Mamar narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Abi Arubah narrated to us, from Yala ibn Hakim, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Uthman, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"379","346 - And Ibrahim ibn Ziyad al-Saigh narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Sulayman al-Razi narrated to us, he said: al-Mughirah ibn Muslim narrated to us, from Matar al-Warraq, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Uthman, and the wording is Yala ibn Hakim's, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said: The blood of a Muslim man is not lawful except by one of three: adultery after chastity, disbelief after Islam, or deliberate murder of a soul, so he shall be killed for it. And this hadith, we do not know anyone narrated it from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Uthman, except Matar and Yala. And it has been narrated from Uthman by other than this route."
"380","347 - Ibrahim ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abu Jafar al-Razi narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Uthman: that he looked upon them and said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in a dream, saying: O Uthman, you shall break fast with us tonight. So he woke fasting and was killed that day."
"381","348 - Abdullah ibn Shabib narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Urwah narrated to me, he said: Abdullah ibn Umar narrated to me, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, that Uthman said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) left me behind from Badr and struck a share for me. And Uthman said concerning the Ridwan pledge: So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) struck with his right upon his left for me, and the left of the Messenger of Allah is better than my right."
"382","Abdullah ibn Jafar: 349 - Ahmad ibn Thabit al-Jahdari narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir Abd al-Malik ibn Amr narrated to us, from Ishaq ibn Yahya ibn Talhah, from Muawiyah ibn Abdullah ibn Jafar, from his father, from Uthman: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed ablution three times three times. And this hadith from Abdullah, from Uthman, has been narrated by another route."
"383","Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, may Allah be pleased with him: 350 - Muhammad ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Ansari narrated to us, he said: Kahmas ibn al-Hasan narrated to us, from Musab ibn Thabit, I reckon from Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, he said: Uthman addressed the people and said: I narrate to you a hadith; nothing prevented me from narrating it to you except miserliness with you. I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever guards a night behind the vulnerability of the Muslims is better than a thousand nights its night prayed and its day fasted. And this hadith, we do not know it narrated with this wording from Uthman except with this chain. And more than one narrated it from Kahmas, from Musab ibn Thabit, from Uthman. And Jafar ibn Sulayman and Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Ansari said: from Kahmas, from Musab, from Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, from Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him."
"384","Zayd ibn Khalid al-Juhani, may Allah be pleased with him: 351 - Muhammad ibn Uthman ibn Karamah narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Shayban narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Zayd ibn Khalid al-Juhani: that he asked Uthman about a man who has intercourse without ejaculation. So he said: Upon him is only ablution. Uthman said: I bear witness I heard that from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And this hadith, we do not know it narrated from Uthman except with this chain. And more than one narrated it from Yahya, so we sufficed with Shayban, from Yahya."
"385","Abu Hurayrah from Uthman, may Allah be pleased with them: 352 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Abu Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Mawhab narrated to us, he said: My uncle narrated to me, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: Uthman went out for Hajj and saw Abdullah ibn Jafar wearing saffron-dyed clothes, so Uthman said to him: Why wear saffron when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade saffron? So Ali said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not forbid you nor him from saffron; he forbade only me. And this hadith, we do not know it narrated from Uthman except by this route."
"386","Salamah ibn al-Akwa, may Allah be pleased with him: 353 - Muhammad ibn Uthman narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Ubaydah narrated to us, from Iyas ibn Salamah, from his father: that Uthman wrapped his lower garment to mid-calf and said: Thus is the lower garment of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And this hadith, we know none narrated it higher than Uthman on the lower garment of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), though narrated by various routes from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and narrated from Abu Bakr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), disconnected."
"387","Tariq ibn Shihab, may Allah be pleased with him: 354 - Abdah ibn Abdullah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Bishr al-Abdi narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Abdullah ibn al-Aswad narrated to us, he said: Husayn ibn Umar narrated to us, from Makhariq, from Tariq, from Uthman, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever deceives the Arabs shall not enter my intercession, nor shall my love reach him. And this hadith, we do not know it narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except from Uthman, from him, with this chain. And we do not know anyone corroborated Abdullah ibn Abdullah ibn al-Aswad upon this hadith, nor Husayn ibn Umar was anyone corroborated upon this narration."
"388","Said ibn al-Asi, may Allah be pleased with him: 355 - Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahim and Ibrahim ibn Ziyad al-Saigh narrated to us, they said: Yaqub ibn Ibrahim ibn Sad narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to me from Salih ibn Kaysan, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Yahya ibn Said ibn al-As narrated to me that Said narrated to him: that Uthman and Aishah, may Allah be pleased with them, informed him: that Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, sought permission upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while reclining on his bed wearing Aishah's cloak. So he permitted Abu Bakr while so, and he fulfilled his need then left. Then Umar sought permission while in that state, and he fulfilled his need then left. Then Uthman sought permission, so he sat and said to Aishah: Gather your clothes upon you. He said: So I fulfilled my need then left. Aishah said: I did not see you alarmed for Abu Bakr and Umar as you were alarmed for Uthman. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Uthman is a shy man, and I feared if I permitted him while in that state he would not convey to me his need. And this hadith, we do not know it narrated from Uthman, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), except by this route. And others than Uthman narrated it, and this chain is the best in chain narrated in that and most connected."
"389","Aban ibn Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him: 356 - al-Abbas ibn Jafar, Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahim and Ibrahim ibn Ziyad narrated to us, they said: Yaqub ibn Ibrahim ibn Sad narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abdullah, nephew of al-Zuhri, narrated to us, from his uncle Ibn Shihab, from Salih ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Farwah: that Amir ibn Sad ibn Abi Waqqas informed him, from Aban ibn Uthman, from Uthman: that he informed him he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Consider if one of them had a flowing river between his house and his workplace, bathing in it five times daily, would any of his dirt remain? They said: No. He said: So are the five prayers. And this hadith, we do not know it narrated from Uthman, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), except by this route with this chain. And this hadith is the highest hadith in this chapter from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"390","357 - Ahmad ibn Aban al-Qurashi narrated to us, he said: Anas ibn Iyad narrated to us, he said: Abu Mawdud narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Kab, from Aban ibn Uthman, from Uthman, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever says morning and evening: In the name of Allah with Whose name nothing harms in earth nor heaven, nothing shall harm him. And if he says it in the morning, nothing harms him until evening; if he says it in the evening, nothing harms him until morning. And this hadith, we do not know anyone narrates it from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with this wording except Uthman. And more than one narrated it from Abu Mawdud, from a man, from Aban. And Anas ibn Iyad joined it and named the man, saying: He is Muhammad ibn Kab."
"391","358 - Ibrahim ibn Abdullah al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Abd al-Malik ibn Waqid narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Abu Abd al-Rahim, from Zayd, from Ibn Aqil, from Aban, from Uthman: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to Zamzam and said: Draw water; were it not you would be overpowered over it, I would draw. And this hadith, we do not know it narrated from Uthman, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), except by this route. And it has been narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by more than one route from others than Uthman."
"392","359 - Bishr ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Maslamah narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Umayyah narrated to us, from Imran ibn Musa ibn Mannah, from Aban, from Uthman: that he saw a funeral approaching; when he saw it he stood for it. Then he informed that he saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) doing it. And this hadith, we do not know it narrated from Uthman, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), except by this route. And it has been narrated from others than Uthman."
"393","360 - Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahim narrated to us, he said: Malik ibn Ismail narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Salam narrated to us, from Ishaq ibn Abdullah, from Muhammad ibn Abi Umamah, from Aban, from Uthman: that he saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) eat bread and meat and pray without ablution. And this hadith, in it only Ishaq ibn Abdullah, and the rest of its chains are fair."
"394","361 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said narrated to us, from Malik ibn Anas, from Nafi, from Nubayh ibn Wahb, from Aban, from Uthman, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"395","362 - And Muhammad ibn Marzuq ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Ala ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Said, meaning Ibn Abi Arubah, narrated to us, from Matar and Yala ibn Hakim, from Nafi, from Nubayh ibn Wahb, from Aban ibn Uthman, from Uthman: that the Prophet, may Allah's blessings upon him."
"396","363 - And Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahhab narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Nubayh ibn Wahb, from Aban, from Uthman, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"397","364 - And Muammal ibn Hisham narrated it to us, he said: Ismail ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Nubayh ibn Wahb, from Aban, from Uthman, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"398","365 - And Muhammad ibn Mirdas al-Ansari narrated it to us, he said: Umar ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Abi Sulayman narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Nafi, from Nubayh ibn Wahb, from Aban, from Uthman, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"399","366 - And Muhammad ibn Miskin narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhib narrated to us, from Nafi, from Nubayh ibn Wahb, from Aban, from Uthman, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The muhrim shall not marry nor give in marriage."
"400","367 - al-Hasan ibn Yahya al-Aruzzi narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Idris narrated to us, he said: Fulayh ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Nafi narrated to us, he said: I heard Nubayh ibn Wahb narrating, he said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz sent to Aban: I wish to marry, so attend the marriage. He said: And that was at Mecca, while he was in ihram. So Aban sent to him: I heard Uthman saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: The muhrim shall not marry nor give in marriage."
"401","368 - Ahmad said: Ishaq said: Fulayh said: And Abd al-Ala and Abd al-Jabbar, sons of Nubayh ibn Wahb, narrated to me, from their father, from Aban, from Uthman, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similar."
"402","369 - Ahmad ibn Abdah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Ayyub ibn Musa, from Nubayh ibn Wahb, from Aban ibn Uthman, from Uthman, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"403","370 - And Muhammad ibn Miskin narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Yusuf narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Ayyub ibn Musa, from Nubayh ibn Wahb, from Aban, from Uthman, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"404","371 - And Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated it to us, he said: Abd al-Samad narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, from Ayyub ibn Musa, from Nubayh ibn Wahb, from Uthman, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: When the muhrim complains of his eye, he shall poultice it with aloes. And this hadith, we do not know anyone narrated it from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except Uthman."
"405","372 - And Abd al-Wahid ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, he said: Anbasah ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from Allaq ibn Abi Muslim, from Abd al-Malik ibn Allaf, from Aban, from Uthman, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The first to intercede on the Day of Resurrection are the prophets, then the martyrs, then the muadhdhins. Abu Bakr said: And this Anbasah is weak in hadith, and Abd al-Malik ibn Allaf, we do not know anyone narrated from him except Anbasah."
"406","Amr ibn Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him: 373 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Musa al-Qurashi narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to me, from his uncle, from Rabiah ibn Abi Abd al-Rahman, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, from Amr ibn Uthman, he said: My father said to me: O my son, if you hold any of people's affairs, honor Quraysh; for I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever intends humiliation of Quraysh, Allah humiliates him. And this hadith, we do not know it narrated from Uthman, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), except by this route with this chain."
"407","Aslam from Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him: 374 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: al-Qasim ibn al-Hakam narrated to us, he said: Abu Ubadah al-Zuraqi narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, he said: I attended Uthman on the day he was besieged while people were at the funeral place; if a pebble were thrown it would fall only upon a man's head. He said: So I saw Uthman look from the small door facing Jibril's station, peace be upon him, and say: Is Talhah among you? They were silent. He said: Is Talhah among you? They were silent. He said: Is Talhah among you? They were silent three times. So Uthman said: I did not think a group hearing my call three last times would not answer me. So he said: I adjure you by Allah, O Talhah, do you know that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was at such and such place, with you and me with him, none but you and me, and he said to you: O Talhah, every prophet has a companion of his nation in Paradise, and this Uthman is my companion in Paradise? He said: O Allah, yes. So he turned from him. And this hadith, Uthman and Talhah ibn Ubaydullah narrated it. And we do not know Aslam narrated from Uthman other than this hadith."
"408","Ibn Abza: 375 - Muhammad ibn Musa al-Qattan al-Wasiti narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Aban narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Abdullah narrated to us, from Jafar ibn Abi al-Mughirah, from Ibn Abza, from Uthman, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: A ram of Quraysh called Abdullah shall be buried at Mecca, upon him like half people's sins. Abu Bakr said: And I think it is only from Yaqub, from Hafs ibn Humayd, from Ibn Abza, and I fear he erred."
"409","Said ibn al-Musayyib: 376 - Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahim, companion of al-Sabiri, narrated to us, he said: Mualla ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Shuayb ibn Ruzayq narrated to us, from Ata al-Khurasani, from Said ibn al-Musayyib: that Uthman sat in a place and ate bread and meat, then stood to prayer, saying: I ate what the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ate, sat in the Prophet's sitting place, and did as the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did. And this hadith, we do not know anyone narrated it from Said ibn al-Musayyib, from Uthman, except Ata al-Khurasani, nor anyone narrated it from Ata except Shuayb ibn Ruzayq."
"410","377 - Abdullah ibn Shabib narrated to us, he said: al-Walid ibn Ata al-Agharr narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, he said: Rabiah ibn Abi Abd al-Rahman narrated to me, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, he said: Uthman performed ablution: rinsed thrice, sniffed thrice, washed his face thrice, his right hand thrice, his left hand thrice, wiped his head, washed his feet thrice, then said: I saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) perform ablution thus. And this hadith, we do not know anyone narrated it from Rabiah, from Said, from Uthman, except Abdullah ibn Abd al-Aziz, nor anyone narrated it from Abdullah ibn Abd al-Aziz except al-Walid ibn Ata, and we heard it only from Abdullah ibn Shabib."
"411","378 - al-Jarrah ibn Makhlad narrated to us, he said: Huyayy Muhammad ibn Hatim narrated to me, he said: Yahya ibn al-Yaman narrated to us, from Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, from Uthman: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed ablution three times three times. And this hadith, we heard it only from al-Jarrah, from Huyayy, and he was of the best of people."
"412","379 - Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahim narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahiah narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Wardan narrated to us, he said: I heard Said ibn al-Musayyib, he said: I heard Uthman saying upon the pulpit: I used to buy dates, measuring in my vessels, then carry them to the market, saying therein such and such, taking its profit and leaving them with it. That reached the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he said: When you buy, measure, and when you sell, measure out. And this hadith, we do not know it narrated from Uthman except by this route with this chain."
"413","380 - Yusuf ibn Musa al-Qattan al-Wasiti narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Makhlad narrated to us, he said: Sallam Abu al-Mundhir narrated to us, from Ali ibn Zayd, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, he said: Uthman raised his voice upon Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, so Abd al-Rahman said to him: Why raise your voice upon me, when I witnessed Badr and you did not, pledged the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and you did not pledge, and fled on Uhud and I did not flee. So Uthman said: As for your saying: I witnessed Badr and you did not, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) left me behind over his daughter, striking a share for me and giving me my reward. And as for your saying: I pledged the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and you did not pledge, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent me to some idolaters, and you know that; when I was detained he struck with his right upon his left, saying: This is for Uthman ibn Affan. So the left of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is better than my right. And as for your saying: You fled on Uhud and I did not flee, Allah the Blessed and Exalted said: Those of you who turned back on the day the two hosts met, Satan only caused them to slip for some they earned, and Allah has pardoned them. So why reproach me for a sin Allah has pardoned me? And this hadith, we do not know it narrated from Said ibn al-Musayyib, from Uthman, except by this route, nor anyone narrated it from Ali ibn Zayd except Sallam Abu al-Mundhir."
"414","Abu Umamah ibn Sahl, may Allah be pleased with him: 381 - Muhammad ibn Abd al-Malik al-Qurashi narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Said, from Abu Umamah ibn Sahl, from Uthman, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The blood of a Muslim man is not lawful except by one of three: a man who commits adultery after chastity, disbelieves after Islam, or deliberately kills a soul. And this hadith, thus a group narrated it from Hammad ibn Zayd with this chain, except Muhammad ibn Isa ibn al-Tabba narrated it from Hammad, from Yahya, from Abu Umamah and Abdullah ibn Amir ibn Rabiah, from Uthman, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"415","Malik ibn Abi Amir: 382 - Amr ibn Malik narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Makhramah, meaning Ibn Bukayr, narrated to us, from his father, he said: Sulayman ibn Yasar informed me: that Malik ibn Abi Amir narrated to him, from Uthman, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Do not sell the dinar for two dinars nor the dirham for two dirhams. And this hadith, Abu Suhayl ibn Malik narrated it from his father, from Uthman; Asim ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated it, and Asim is not strong. And we do not know it narrated from Uthman except from the hadith of Malik ibn Abi Amir from him."
"416","Amir ibn Sad: 383 - Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahim narrated to us, he said: Surayj ibn al-Numan narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Zinad narrated to us, from his father, from Amir ibn Sad, from Uthman, he said: I heard him saying: Nothing prevents me from narrating from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except that I am the most retentive of his companions from him; but I bear witness I heard him saying: Whoever says upon me what I did not say, let him take his seat in the Fire. And this hadith, Amir ibn Sad and Mahmud ibn Labid narrated it from Uthman."
"417","Mahmud ibn Labid from Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him: 384 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr al-Hanafi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Hamid ibn Jafar narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to me, from Mahmud ibn Labid, from Uthman, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever lies upon me deliberately, let him take his seat in the Fire. And we do not know Mahmud ibn Labid heard from Uthman, though he was early."
"418","385 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Hamid ibn Jafar narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to me, from Mahmud ibn Labid, that Uthman said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever builds a mosque for Allah, Allah builds for him its like in Paradise. And we do not know Mahmud ibn Labid raised from Uthman except these two hadiths, nor know for them a path except this path."
"419","Abu Uthman al-Nahdi: 386 - Sadaqah ibn al-Fadl narrated to us, he said: Salim ibn Nuh narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Amir narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Abu Uthman: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade silk except the measure of two fingers. Thus Umar ibn Amir said, from Qatadah, from Abu Uthman, from Uthman. And others than Umar narrated it from Qatadah, from Abu Uthman, from Umar. And we do not know anyone corroborated Umar ibn Amir upon this narration from Uthman."
"420","387 - Abdullah ibn al-Sabbah narrated to us, he said: al-Hajjaj ibn Nusayr narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from al-Awwam ibn Murajim, from Abu Uthman al-Nahdi, from Uthman, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The hornless shall be retaliated for from the horned, meaning on the Day of Resurrection. And this hadith, we do not know it narrated from Uthman except by this route, and none narrated this hadith with this chain except al-Hajjaj, from Shubah."
"421","Ibn Abd al-Shariq al-Khathami: 388 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Muadh ibn Hani narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Ubayd ibn Umayr narrated to us, he said: I heard Abu Muawiyah narrating from Ibn Abd al-Shariq al-Khathami, he said: I heard Uthman saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whose feet become dusty in the path of Allah, or: no man's feet become dusty in the path of Allah, except Allah forbids him the Fire. So I never saw more walkers than that day. And this hadith, we do not know it narrated from Uthman except by this route. And this Abu Muawiyah from whom Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Ubayd narrated, I heard none naming him, nor naming Ibn Abd al-Shariq."
"422","Abu Said, freedman of Abu Usayd: 389 - Ahmad ibn al-Miqdam narrated to us, he said: al-Mutamir ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: I heard my father narrating from Abu Nadrah, from Abu Said, freedman of Abu Usayd, he said: It reached Uthman that the delegation of Egypt approached, so he met them in a village of his outside Medina, disliking they enter upon him, or as he said. When they knew his place they came to him and said: Call for the Mushaf. So he called, meaning it, and said: Open. So he read until he reached this verse: Say: Have you seen what Allah sent down to you of provision, making some unlawful and lawful; say: Did Allah permit you or do you forge against Allah. So they said: Did Allah's preserve permit you or do you forge against Allah? So he said: Proceed; it descended concerning such and such. As for the preserve, Umar preserved it for charity camels; when I ruled I did what he did, adding nothing over what he added. He said: And I see him only saying: And I that day was such years old. Then he said: They asked him about things; he kept saying: Proceed; it descended concerning such and such. Then they asked him about things he recognized with no way out for him, so he said: I seek Allah's forgiveness. Then he said: What do you want? They said: We want the people of Medina not to take stipends, for this wealth is for those who fought for it and these elders of Muhammad's companions (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: So he agreed and they agreed. He said: And they took upon him, and I see they wrote a document upon him, and he took upon them not to split the staff nor part the congregation. He said: So he agreed and they agreed, and they came with him to Medina. So he ascended the pulpit, praised Allah and extolled Him, then said: By Allah, I never saw a delegation better than this delegation. Lo, whoever has crops let him join his crops, whoever has udders let him milk them. Lo, you have no wealth with us; this wealth is only for those who fought for it and these elders of Muhammad's companions (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: So people angered and said: This is Banu Umayyah's deceit. And the delegation returned pleased. When partway, a rider exposed himself to them, then parted, then returned insulting them. So they seized him and said to him: What is your affair? You have an affair. So he said: I am the Commander of the Believers' messenger to his governor in Egypt. So they searched him and found with him a letter in Uthman's name bearing his seal: to crucify them, strike their necks, or cut their hands and feet. He said: So they returned and said: He broke the covenant, and Allah made his blood lawful. So they came to Medina and came to Ali, saying: Do you not see Allah's enemy wrote concerning us such and such; stand with us to him. He said: By Allah, I shall not stand with you. They said: Then why did you write to us? He said: By Allah, I never wrote to you any letter. So some looked to some, then some said: Is it for this you fight or for this you anger. And Ali went out and alighted at a village outside Medina. So they came to Uthman and said: Did you write concerning us such and such? He said: They are only two: you establish two witnesses, or Allah's oath: I neither wrote, dictated, nor knew. And you know a letter may be written in a man's name, and a seal engraved upon a seal. He said: So they besieged him. One day he looked upon them and said: Peace upon you. He said: And I heard none return upon him except a man return within himself. So he said: I adjure you by Allah, do you know I bought Ruma well from my wealth, sweetening with it, making my rope as any Muslim's rope? It was said: Yes. He said: Then why prevent me drinking its water until forced upon sea water. He said: I adjure you by Allah, do you know I bought such and such from my wealth and added it to the mosque? They said: Yes. He said: Do you know anyone prevented praying in it before me? Then he mentioned things the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him, and I see he mentioned his writing the Mufassal with his hand. He said: So prohibition spread, and it was said: Gently with the Commander of the Believers. And this hadith, we do not know anyone narrated it except al-Mutamir ibn Sulayman from his father."
"423","al-Ahnaf ibn Qays from Uthman: 390 - Muhammad ibn Yazid ibn al-Rawwas narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Idris narrated to us, he said: Husayn ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from Amr ibn Jawan, he said: al-Ahnaf said. 391 - And Yaqub ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: al-Mutamir ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from his father, from Husayn, meaning Ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Amr ibn Jawan, he said: al-Ahnaf ibn Qays said: We set out as pilgrims and entered Medina; while we set down our mounts a comer came to us saying: People panicked in the mosque. He said: So I and my companion went, and there were people gathered mid-mosque around men. So I threaded through them, and there were Ali, al-Zubayr, Talhah and Sad. It was only moments until Uthman came walking in the mosque wearing two yellow sheets, or a yellow sheet, raised upon his head. So I said to my companion: Stay until I see what brought him. When he drew near them it was said: This is Ibn Affan. So he said: Is Ali here? They said: Yes. He said: Is Talhah here? They said: Yes. He said: Is al-Zubayr here? They said: Yes. He said: Is Sad here? They said: Yes. He said: I adjure you by Allah besides Whom there is no god, do you know the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever buys the mirbad of so-and-so, Allah forgives him. So I bought it for twenty or twenty-five thousand, then came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: I bought it. So he said: Place it in the mosque, or said: Place it in the Muslims' mosque, and its reward is yours. So they said: Yes. He said: I adjure you by Allah besides Whom there is no god, do you know the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever buys Ruma well, Allah forgives him. So I bought it for such and such, then came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: I bought it for such and such. He said: So he said: Make it a waterhouse for Muslims, and its reward is yours. So they said: O Allah, yes. So he said: I adjure you by Allah besides Whom there is no god, do you know the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) looked in the people's faces on the day of the hardship army, saying: Whoever equips these, Allah forgives him. So I equipped them until they lacked neither bridle nor hobble. They said: Yes. He said: O Allah, bear witness, O Allah, bear witness, thrice. Then he left. And this hadith, we do not know anyone narrated it from al-Ahnaf except Ibn Jawan, and they differed in his name. And we do not know anyone narrated from Ibn Jawan except Husayn ibn Abd al-Rahman."
"424","Ibn Farrukh: 392 - Muammal ibn Hisham narrated to us, he said: Ibn Ulayyah narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Ubayd narrated to us, from Ata ibn Farrukh, from Uthman, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: May Allah forgive a man easy when selling, easy when buying, easy when paying, easy when demanding. And Ata ibn Farrukh is a man of Basra; Yunus ibn Ubayd and Ali ibn Zayd narrated from him. And we do not know he heard from Uthman."
"425","Abu Wail: 393 - Yusuf ibn Musa al-Qattan narrated to us, he said: Waki ibn al-Jarrah narrated to us, he said: Israil narrated to us, from Amir ibn Shaqiq, from Abu Wail, he said: I saw Uthman perform ablution: washing his hands thrice, his face thrice, rinsing thrice and sniffing thrice, washing his arms thrice thrice, wiping his head and his ears outer and inner, interlacing his toes, interlacing his beard thrice, then washing his face before his hands. Then he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) do as you saw me do. And this hadith, we do not know it narrated from Uthman except by this route with this chain."
"426","394 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Thabit ibn Thawban narrated to us, from Abdah ibn Abi Lubabah, from Shaqiq ibn Salamah, he said: I saw Ali and Uthman perform ablution three times three times, saying: Thus we saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) perform ablution."
"427","395 - Ibrahim ibn al-Mustamirr narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Asim narrated to us, he said: al-Mutamir ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from his father, from Asim, from Abu Wail, he said: al-Walid ibn Uqbah met Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf and said: Why do you not come to the Commander of the Believers nor attend him. So Abd al-Rahman said to him: Convey to him from me that I was not absent from Badr, nor fled on the day of Uhud's two eyes. So it reached Uthman, and he said: As for his saying: I was not absent from Badr, I stayed behind over the daughter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he struck a share for me; whoever the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) strikes a share for is as if he witnessed it. And as for his saying: I did not flee on the day of Uhud's two eyes, Allah the Mighty exalted has pardoned all who fled, so why reproach me for a sin Allah pardoned. And this hadith has been narrated by more than one from Abu Wail, from the hadith of Asim and from the hadith of Mansur. And we mentioned it from al-Taymi, from Asim, since of fair outlet, and sufficed with it."
"428","Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami: 396 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna and Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, they said: Yahya ibn Said narrated to us, he said: Shubah and Sufyan narrated to us, from Alqamah ibn Marthad, from Sad ibn Ubaydah, from Abu Abd al-Rahman, from Uthman, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), one of them said: The best of you, and the other said: The most excellent of you is whoever learns the Quran and teaches it. And this hadith, we do not know it narrated from Uthman except by this route. And more than one narrated it from Alqamah ibn Marthad, from Abu Abd al-Rahman, from Uthman, except Yahya ibn Said joined Shubah and al-Thawri in this hadith, narrating it from Alqamah, from Sad, from Abu Abd al-Rahman, from Uthman. And Sufyan's companions narrate it from Alqamah, from Abu Abd al-Rahman. And it is Shubah who said: from Sad. And I heard Amr ibn Ali saying: I said to Yahya: al-Thawri narrates it from Alqamah, from Abu Abd al-Rahman. So he said: I heard it from Shubah, from Alqamah, from Sad, then heard it from al-Thawri, and did not doubt he said as Shubah said, or it was with me as Shubah narrated. And a group narrated it from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); Ali ibn Abi Talib, Sad ibn Abi Waqqas, Abdullah ibn Masud narrated it, and their chains have defects. So we mentioned Uthman's hadith for his eminence, its chain's excellence, and sufficed with it over others."
"429","397 - Ahmad ibn Uthman ibn Hakim narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Qays narrated to us, from Alqamah ibn Marthad, from Sad ibn Ubaydah, from Abu Abd al-Rahman, from Uthman, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similar."
"430","398 - Muhammad ibn Miskin narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Zayd, meaning Ibn Abi Unaysah, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu Abd al-Rahman, from Uthman: that he adjured them, saying: Do you not know the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was upon Hira with me, Abu Bakr, and mentioning some of his companions, when it shook. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Be still; upon you is only a prophet, a truthful, or a martyr. I reckon he said: So they said: Yes."
"431","399 - Abdullah ibn al-Harith al-Marwazi narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Uthman narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to me, from Shubah, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu Abd al-Rahman, from Uthman, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similar. And this hadith, we do not know it narrated from Uthman except by this route we mentioned, unless Yunus ibn Abi Ishaq differed in its chain, narrating it from his father, from Abu Salamah. And we memorized it only from the hadith of Abu Ishaq, from Abu Abd al-Rahman. And none narrated it from Shubah except Uthman ibn Jabalah."
"432","Alqamah ibn Qays: 400 - Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Ubayd narrated to us, from Abu Mashar, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, he said: We came with Abdullah upon Uthman, so Uthman said to Abdullah: What remains of you for women? So Abdullah said: Come near, O Alqamah. And I was a youth, so I drew near. So Uthman said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to bachelor youths and said: Whoever of you has means, let him marry; for it lowers the gaze and guards the private part. And whoever not, upon him is fasting, for it is a suppressant for him. Thus Yunus narrated it from Abu Mashar, and Yazid ibn Zuray and Ismail ibn Ulayyah narrated it from Yunus. And this hadith, al-Amash only narrated it from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, from Abdullah ibn Masud, and it is the correct. And Mansur narrated it from Ibrahim, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, from Abdullah. And Yunus's hadith is mistaken; the correct is only Ibn Masud's hadith, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"433","Abbad ibn Zahir Abu Ruwa: 401 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Jafar narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, he said: Simak ibn Harb narrated to us, he said: I heard Abbad ibn Zahir Abu Ruwa saying: I heard Uthman saying: We accompanied the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in travel and residence; he would visit Muslim sick and attend their funerals, or said: follow their funerals. And this hadith, we do not know it narrated from Uthman except with this chain. And we do not know Abbad ibn Zahir narrated other than this hadith, nor anyone narrated from him other than Simak."
"434","Abu Sahlah from Uthman: 402 - Abu Kurayb and Yahya ibn Dawud al-Wasiti narrated to us, they said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Abi Khalid narrated to us, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, from Abu Sahlah: that Uthman said on the Day of the House: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) covenanted to me a covenant, and I restrain myself upon it. And we do not know Abu Sahlah narrated except this hadith, nor anyone narrated from him other than Qays ibn Abi Hazim."
"435","Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Amrah: 403 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Abu Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu Sahl, and he is Uthman ibn Hakim, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Amrah, from Uthman, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever prays Isha in congregation is as standing half a night, and whoever prays Isha and Fajr in congregation is as standing a night. And this hadith, we do not know it narrated except from Uthman, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"436","Abdullah ibn Shaqiq 404 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Jafar narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Abdullah ibn Shaqiq, he said: Uthman used to forbid tamattu (combining Hajj and Umrah), while Ali used to enjoin it. Uthman said something to Ali about that, so Ali said: You certainly know that we performed tamattu with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: Yes, but we were in a state of fear. And we do not know of Abdullah ibn Shaqiq narrating anything with an isnad from Uthman except this hadith."
"437","Abu Salih the freedman of Uthman, whose name is al-Harith 405 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Abi Ayyub narrated to us, he said: Abu Aqil narrated to me that he heard al-Harith, the freedman of Uthman, saying: Uthman sat on the sitting places and we sat with him. When the muadhdhin came to him, he called for water about a mudd in amount and performed wudu, then said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performing wudu like this wudu of mine, then he said: Whoever performs wudu like my wudu, then stands to pray Zuhr, he is forgiven for what is between it and Asr prayer; whoever prays Asr is forgiven for what is between it and Maghrib prayer; whoever prays Maghrib is forgiven for what is between it and Isha prayer; whoever prays Isha is forgiven for what is between it and Fajr prayer - or he said Subh prayer - then if he stands and performs wudu, then prays, he is forgiven for what is between it and Zuhr. And these are the good deeds that remove evil deeds. They said: These are the good deeds, so what are the enduring good deeds? Uthman said: They are: La ilaha illa Allah, and Subhan Allah wa bihamdihi, and Allahu Akbar, and al-hamdu lillah, and la hawla wa la quwwata illa billah. And we do not know this hadith with its wording narrated from Uthman except through this route."
"438","406 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to us, he said: Abu Aqil Zuhrah ibn Mabad narrated to us, from Abu Salih the freedman of Uthman, he said: I heard Uthman saying on the minbar: I had concealed from you a hadith I heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then it occurred to me to narrate it to you so that each person may choose for himself what seems fit to him. I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Guarding for one day in the path of Allah is better than a thousand days in other dwellings. And these two hadiths which Abu Salih the freedman of Uthman narrated, we know no route for them except these two routes."
"439","Abu Ubayd the freedman of Abd al-Rahman ibn Azhar 407 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhib narrated to us, from Said ibn Khalid, from Abu Ubayd the freedman of Abd al-Rahman ibn Azhar, he said: I saw Ali ibn Abi Talib and Uthman praying on the day of Fitr and Adha, and they mentioned - or they said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade fasting these two days - meaning the day of Fitr and the day of Adha."
"440","Rabah 408 - And Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated it to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: I heard Muhammad ibn Abi Yaqub narrating from al-Hasan ibn Sad, from Rabah, he said: My family married me to a slave woman of theirs, and she gave birth to a black boy like me. Then a Roman slave called Yuhannas got her pregnant, so she gave birth to another like a small lizard. I said: What is this? She said: He belongs to Yuhannas. So I came to Uthman and mentioned that to him. He sent for her and said: I judge between you by the judgment of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged that the child belongs to the bed, and for the adulterer is the stone alone. Then she became pregnant and gave birth for me to a boy like me; two black and one red, and they were attributed to me. And we do not know this hadith narrated from Uthman except through this route."
"441","Ibn Darah from Uthman 409 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Safwan ibn Isa narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: I entered upon Ibn Darah in his house and he heard me rinsing my mouth, so he said: O Muhammad! I said: At your service. He said: Shall I not inform you about the wudu of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? I said: Yes. He said: I saw Uthman ibn Affan at the sitting places; he called for wudu water, rinsed his mouth three times, sniffed water three times, washed his face three times, wiped his head and washed his forearms, and washed his feet, then said: Whoever loves to look at the wudu of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), let him look at this wudu of mine. And we do not know anyone narrating from Ibn Darah except Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Maryam."
"442","Khufaf ibn Arabah 410 - Muhammad ibn Abd al-Malik al-Wasiti narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Isa ibn Tariq - and he was unobjectionable - narrated to us, from Isa ibn Yunus, he said: Mujalid narrated to us, from al-Shabi, from Khufaf ibn Arabah, from Uthman, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Faith is Yemeni, and the support of faith is in Qahtan, and harshness is in the children of Adnan. Himyar is the head of the Arabs and their fang, Madhhij is their crown and protection, al-Azd is their shoulder and skull, Hamdan is their withers and peak. O Allah, honor the Ansar, through whom Allah established the religion, who sheltered me and helped me and protected me; they are my companions in this world and my party in the Hereafter, and the first of my ummah to enter Paradise. And we do not know this hadith narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except through this route with this isnad, and we do not know Khufaf narrating with an isnad except this hadith."
"443","Amr ibn Said ibn al-As 411 - al-Hasan ibn Yahya al-Aruzzi narrated to us, he said: Hisham Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Ishaq - and he is Ibn Said ibn Amr ibn Said - narrated to us, from his father, he said: My father narrated to me, he said: We were sitting with Uthman, and he called for water to perform wudu, and said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: There is no person whom an obligatory prayer reaches, and he perfects its wudu and its humility and its bowing, except that it is expiation for his previous sins - I think he said: As long as it is not a major sin, and that is for all time. And we do not know of Amr ibn Said narrating from Uthman except this hadith."
"444","Kathir ibn al-Salt from Uthman 412 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: al-Mughirah ibn Salamah narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb narrated to us, from Musa ibn Uqbah, he said: Abu Alqamah the freedman of Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf narrated to me, from Kathir ibn al-Salt, he said: Uthman dozed off on the day he was killed, then woke up, then said: Were it not that you would say Uthman wished a wish, I would narrate to you. He said: We said: Narrate to us, for we are not upon what people say. He said: I saw tonight the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this dream of mine, and he said: You will be present with us on Friday."
"445","413 - al-Hasan ibn al-Sabbah al-Bazzar narrated to us, he said: Khalaf ibn Tamim narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Ibrahim ibn al-Muhajir narrated to us, he said: I heard Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, he said: Kathir ibn al-Salt said: I entered upon Uthman while he was besieged, and he said: O Kathir, I do not see myself except killed this very day. He said: I said: May Allah grant you victory over your enemy. He said: Then he repeated it to me, so I said to him: Was anything said to you about it? He said: No, but I stayed awake this night, and when it was near dawn I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and Abu Bakr, and Umar, and the Prophet of Allah said: O Uthman, do not detain us, for we are waiting for you. So he was killed that very day. He said: I said: Is the one speaking to Uthman Kathir? He said: Yes. And we do not know of Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr narrating from Kathir from Uthman except this hadith."
"446","Humran the freedman of Uthman 414 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Hurayth ibn al-Saib narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn Abi al-Hasan al-Basri narrated to us, he said: Humran ibn Aban narrated to me, from Uthman: That the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Three things after which the son of Adam has no surplus: coarse bread - this food - and a garment that covers him, and a house that shelters him; and whatever is beyond that, the son of Adam has no surplus in it. So I said to Humran: What kept you back from this? He said: Worldly life held me back. And we do not know this hadith narrated from Uthman except with this isnad, and al-Hasan did not narrate with an isnad from Imran from Uthman except this hadith."
"447","Abu Bishr al-Walid from Humran 415 - Muhammad ibn Abd al-Malik al-Qurashi narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn al-Mufaddal narrated to us, he said: Khalid al-Hadhdha narrated to us, from al-Walid Abu Bishr, from Humran ibn Aban, from Uthman, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever dies while knowing that Allah is true will enter Paradise. And we do not know this hadith narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except through this route with this isnad."
"448","Jami ibn Shaddad from Humran 416 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Jafar narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Jami ibn Shaddad, he said: I heard Humran ibn Aban narrating in the mosque that he heard Uthman narrating from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: Whoever completes wudu as Allah commanded him, then the prescribed prayers are expiations for what is between them. And Jami ibn Shaddad did not narrate from Humran except this hadith."
"449","417 - And Ali ibn Shuayb al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: Abu Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Misar narrated to us, from Jami ibn Shaddad, from Humran, from Uthman, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with the like of it."
"450","Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman from Humran 418 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Wardan narrated to us, he said: Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to me, he said: Humran narrated to me, he said: I saw Uthman perform wudu: he washed his hands three times, washed his face three times, washed his forearms three times, wiped his head three times, and washed his feet three times, then said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) perform wudu like this, and he said: Whoever performs wudu with less than this, it suffices him. And we do not know of Abu Salamah narrating from Humran except this hadith."
"451","Muslim ibn Yasar from Humran 419 - Ahmad ibn Abdah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray narrated to us, he said: Said narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Muslim ibn Yasar, from Humran: That Uthman - may Allah be pleased with him - called for wudu water."
"452","420 - And Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Adi narrated to us, from Said, from Qatadah, from Muslim ibn Yasar, from Humran, from Uthman - may Allah be pleased with him - that he called for wudu water, so he rinsed his mouth and sniffed water and washed his face three times, and his forearms three times three times, and wiped his head and the back of his feet, then smiled and said: Will you not ask me what made me smile? We said: What made you smile, O Commander of the Faithful? He said: I smiled because the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called for wudu water near this place, and the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed wudu as I performed wudu, then smiled as I smiled, then said: Will you not ask me what made me smile? We said: What made you smile, O Prophet of Allah? He said: It made me smile that when a slave performs wudu and washes his face, Allah removes from him every sin committed by his face; when he washes his forearms it is likewise; when he wipes his head it is likewise; when he purifies his feet it is likewise."
"453","421 - And Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated it to us: Muadh ibn Hisham narrated to us, my father narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Humran, from Uthman, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - and Muadh ibn Hisham did not say: From my father, from Qatadah, from Muslim ibn Yasar."
"454","Muhammad ibn Kab al-Qurazi from Humran 422 - Muhammad ibn Said ibn Yazid ibn Ibrahim al-Tustari narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Makhlad narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Hazim narrated to us, he said: I heard Muhammad ibn Kab saying: Humran narrated to me, he said: Uthman called for wudu water while wanting to go out to prayer on a cold night, so I brought him water and he washed his face and hands. I said: It suffices you; you have perfected wudu, and the night is severely cold. He said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: No slave perfects wudu except that Allah forgives him his past and future sins. And we do not know of Muhammad ibn Kab narrating with an isnad from Humran except this hadith."
"455","Urwah ibn al-Zubayr from Humran 423 - Ubayd ibn Ismail al-Habbari, from the offspring of Habbar ibn al-Aswad, narrated to us, he said: Abu Usamah narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Humran, from Uthman: That he was brought water and performed wudu: he rinsed his mouth and sniffed water and washed his face three times, and his hands three times three times, and wiped his head until he completed the wudu, and said: Thus I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performing wudu."
"456","424 - And al-Hasan ibn Yahya narrated it to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Abd al-Majid narrated to us; 425 - and Ahmad ibn Amr ibn Ubaydah narrated it to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us, from Abu al-Zinad, from his father, from Urwah, from Humran, from Uthman, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with the like of it. And this hadith was narrated from Urwah by Hisham, and a group narrated it from Hisham, one of them Shubah, so we sufficed with whom we mentioned from Hisham. And Abu al-Aswad narrated it from Urwah, from Humran, from Uthman, and Ibn Luhayah narrated it from him. And Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr narrated it from Urwah, from Humran, from Uthman."
"457","426 - And al-Hasan ibn Muhammad al-Zafarani narrated it to us with it, he said: Yahya ibn Abbad narrated to us, he said: Fulayh ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr, from Urwah, from Humran, from Uthman, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - and he mentioned that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed wudu three times three times."
"458","Musa ibn Talhah from Humran 427 - Muhammad ibn Abd al-Malik narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from Musa ibn Talhah, from Humran, he said: Prayer was announced one day while I was with Uthman, and Uthman - may Allah be pleased with him - said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: There is no Muslim who performs wudu and perfects wudu, then goes to a prescribed prayer, except that it is expiation for what is between it and the next one. And we do not know of Abd al-Malik narrating from Musa ibn Talhah, from Humran, from Uthman except this hadith. And Asim ibn Bahdalah narrated from al-Musayyib ibn Rafi, from Musa ibn Talhah, from Humran, from Uthman, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) something resembling this speech, and it is:"
"459","428 - What Khalid ibn Yusuf narrated to us with it, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Asim, from al-Musayyib, from Musa ibn Talhah, from Humran, from Uthman, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And Hammad ibn Salamah narrated this hadith from Asim ibn Bahdalah but did not connect it as Abu Awanah connected it."
"460","Ata ibn Yazid from Humran 429 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Mamar narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Ata ibn Yazid al-Laythi, from Humran: That Uthman performed wudu: he rinsed his mouth and sniffed water and washed his face three times, and his hands three times, and wiped his head, and washed his feet three times, and said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) perform wudu as I performed wudu, then he said: Whoever performs wudu like this wudu of mine and prays two rakahs, not talking to himself in them, is forgiven his past sins."
"461","430 - And Ahmad ibn Mansur ibn Sayyar narrated to us: Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Ata ibn Yazid, from Humran, from Uthman, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with the like of it."
"462","431 - And Ibrahim ibn Ziyad al-Saigh narrated to us: Yaqub ibn Ibrahim ibn Sad narrated to us, my father narrated to us, from Salih, from al-Zuhri, from Ata ibn Yazid, from Humran, from Uthman; he said: And Urwah narrated it to me, from Humran, from Uthman. And we do not know of Ata ibn Yazid narrating from Humran, from Uthman except this hadith."
"463","Zayd ibn Aslam from Humran 432 - Ahmad ibn Aban narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Humran, he said: I brought Uthman his wudu water, and he said: People narrate from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) hadiths, I do not know what they are. I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) perform wudu like this wudu of mine, then he said: Whoever performs wudu like this wudu of mine is forgiven his past sins, and his prayer and his walking to the mosque are supererogatory. And this hadith with this wording, we know no one narrates it except Uthman. And this route is among the best of what is narrated from Uthman on that."
"464","Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir from Humran 433 - al-Hasan ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Idris narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Hakim narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir narrated to us, he said: Humran narrated to me, he said: I heard Uthman saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever performs wudu and perfects wudu, sins exit his body until they exit from under his nails. And we do not know of Uthman ibn Hakim narrating from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Humran, from Uthman except this hadith, and none narrated it with an isnad from Uthman ibn Hakim except Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad."
"465","434 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us, he said: Ayyub ibn Sayyar narrated to us, from Ibn al-Munkadir, from Humran: That Uthman called for an earthen vessel of water at the funeral place, and rinsed his mouth and sniffed water three times, and washed his face three times, and washed his hands three times three times, and wiped his head and his ears, their outer and inner, once, and washed his feet three times, then said: I saw your Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) do this. And we do not know of Ibn al-Munkadir narrating from Humran, from Uthman except these two hadiths."
"466","Mabad al-Juhani from Humran 435 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Musawir narrated to us, he said: Awf narrated to us, from Mabad al-Juhani, he said: Humran narrated to me, he said: I was with Uthman, and he called for wudu water and performed wudu. When he finished he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed wudu as I performed wudu. When he finished he smiled and said: Do you know why I smiled? He said: We said: Allah and His Messenger know best. He said: When a Muslim slave performs wudu and completes his wudu, then enters his prayer and completes his prayer, he exits from his prayer as he exits from his mothers belly. And this hadith is not narrated with this wording except from Uthman with this isnad."
"467","Muadh ibn Abd al-Rahman from Humran 436 - Muhammad ibn Miskin ibn Numaylah narrated to us, he said: Adam ibn Abi Iyas narrated to us, he said: Shayban ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim, he said: Muadh ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to me: That Humran informed him that he heard Uthman saying: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) perform wudu and perfect wudu, then he said: Whoever performs wudu like this wudu of mine - or like my wudu - then comes to the mosque and bows two rakahs is forgiven his past sins. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not be deceived."
"468","437 - Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahim narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Muhammad narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Abi Habib narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Abi Salamah, and Nafi ibn Jubayr, from Muadh ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Taymi, from Humran, from Uthman: That he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever performs wudu and perfects wudu, then walks to a prescribed prayer, is forgiven. And we do not know of Muadh narrating from Humran except these two hadiths. And the hadith of Muhammad ibn Ibrahim from Muadh was narrated by Hisham from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim, from Abu Wail, from Humran, without including Muadh between them. And al-Awzai narrated this hadith from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim also, except that they narrated it from Muhammad: some narrated it from Abu Wail, from Humran, and some narrated it from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim, from a man, from Humran."
"469","Mujahid from Humran from Uthman 438 - al-Hasan ibn al-Sabbah al-Bazzar narrated to us: Khalaf ibn Tamim narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Ibrahim ibn Muhajir narrated to us, he said: I heard my father mentioning it from Mujahid, from Humran ibn Aban, he said: I saw Uthman - may Allah be pleased with him - call for water and perform wudu, then he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever purifies himself and perfects purification, then prays, is expiated for his past. Then he turned to his companions and said: I adjure you by Allah, did you hear - I think he said - three or four times. They said: Yes, we heard. And we do not know of Mujahid narrating from Humran, from Uthman except this hadith. And one other than Ismail ibn Ibrahim narrated it from Ibrahim ibn Muhajir, from Ikrimah ibn Khalid. And we know no one who said: From Ibrahim ibn Muhajir, from Mujahid, from Humran except Khalaf from Ismail."
"470","Abd al-Malik ibn Ubayd from Humran 439 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Muadh ibn Muadh narrated to us, he said: Imran ibn Hudayr narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Ubayd, from Humran, from Uthman."
"471","440 - And Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Sawwaf narrated it to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Humran narrated to us, he said: Imran narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Ubayd, from Humran, from Uthman, raised: Whoever knows that prayer upon him is a prescribed right, or a right and obligatory, enters Paradise. And this hadith raised, we know it was narrated only from Uthman."
"472","Abd al-Karim from Humran 441 - Muhammad ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Raja narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz al-Majishun narrated to us, from Abd al-Karim, from Humran, he said: Uthman performed wudu: he washed his face three times, and his hands three times, and wiped his head, and washed his feet three times three times, then said: I performed wudu as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed wudu, then he was brought meat with a bone and picked it, and said: I ate as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ate."
"473","al-Jurayri from Humran 442 - al-Mundhir ibn al-Walid al-Jarudi narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: Shaddad ibn Said narrated to us, from al-Jurayri, from Humran, from Uthman: That he called for wudu water and washed his palms three times, and rinsed his mouth three times, and sniffed water three times, and washed his face three times, and washed his right hand three times and his left hand three times, and wiped his head and his ears, then washed the right foot three times and washed the left three times, then said: Thus I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) perform wudu."
"474","Abu Alqamah the freedman of Ibn Abbas 443 - Muhammad ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Bakr al-Bursani narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Abi Ziyad al-Qaddah narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Ubayd ibn Umayr informed me, from Abu Alqamah the freedman of Ibn Abbas, from Uthman: That he called for wudu water with people from among the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) present, and poured with his right hand upon the left and washed them three times, and rinsed his mouth three times, and sniffed water three times, and washed his face three times, and washed his hands to the elbows three times, then wiped his head, and washed his feet and cleaned them thoroughly, then said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) perform wudu like this wudu, and he said: Perform wudu as you saw me perform wudu, then he said: Whoever performs wudu and perfects his wudu, then prays two rakahs, becomes free of his sins like the day his mother bore him."
"475","Hani the freedman of Uthman 444 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Idris narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Yusuf al-Sanani narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Buhayr narrated to us, he said: Hani the freedman of Uthman narrated to us: Uthman, when he stood over a grave, wept until he wet his beard. It was said to him: Paradise and Hell are mentioned in your presence and you do not weep, yet you weep from this? He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The grave is the first of the dwellings of the Hereafter; if one is saved from it, what comes after it is easier than it, and if one is not saved from it, what comes after it is harder than it."
"476","445 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Idris narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Buhayr narrated to me, he said: Hani the freedman of Uthman narrated to me, from Uthman, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood over the grave of a man while he was being buried; when they finished, he said: Seek forgiveness for your brother and ask Allah for him firmness, for he is being questioned now. And these two hadiths are not narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except from the hadith of Uthman, and we know no isnad for this except this isnad."
"477","Abdullah ibn Rashid the freedman of Uthman 446 - Muhammad ibn Mamar narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahid ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Rashid the freedman of Uthman, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah has one hundred and seventeen paths; whoever meets Him with one of those traits enters Paradise. And we do not know this hadith narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except through this route. Abd al-Wahid ibn Zayd is not strong, and we know no one narrating from Abdullah ibn Rashid except Abd al-Wahid."
"478","Wahb ibn Umayr 447 - Abdullah ibn Yusuf al-Thaqafi narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ata ibn Abi Maymunah narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to me, from Wahb ibn Umayr, he said: I heard Uthman saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade a woman shaving her head. And we do not know Wahb ibn Umayr narrating except this hadith, and we know no one narrating from him except Ata ibn Abi Maymunah. And Rawh is not strong."
"479","Abu Thawr al-Fahmi 448 - Bishr ibn Adam narrated to us, he said: Zayd ibn al-Hubab informed us, he said: Ibn Luhayah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Amr al-Maafiri narrated to me, he said: I heard Abu Thawr al-Fahmi saying: Abd al-Rahman ibn Udays al-Balawi came - and he was among those who pledged under the tree - and ascended the minbar, praised Allah and extolled Him, and mentioned Uthman. Abu Thawr said: I entered upon Uthman, and he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married me his daughter, then his daughter; then I pledged allegiance to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with this - meaning the right hand - and I never touched my private part with it, nor stayed behind, nor wished, nor drank wine in Jahiliyyah nor in Islam. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Who will buy this narrow street and add it to the mosque, and for him is a house in Paradise? So I bought it and added it to the mosque. End of the hadith of Uthman ibn Affan - may Allah be pleased with him - and it is the end of the fifth part, and praise be to Allah abundantly as He deserves."
"480","Musnad of Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him; What Ibn Masud narrated from Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him 449 - Ibn Mufarrij narrated to us by ijazah, he said: Muhammad ibn Ayyub narrated to us by ijazah, he said: Ahmad ibn Amr ibn Abd al-Khaliq al-Bazzar narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Said al-Jawhari narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said al-Umawi narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Asim, from Zirr, from Abdullah ibn Masud, he said: Ali ibn Abi Talib - may Allah be pleased with him - said to us that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commands you to recite as you were taught. And this hadith was narrated by more than one from Asim, from Zirr, from Abdullah ibn Masud, and the highest who narrated it from Asim is al-Amash. And we know no one narrating it from al-Amash except Yahya ibn Said al-Umawi."
"481","Talhah ibn Ubaydullah from Ali 450 - Muhammad ibn Umar al-Kindi narrated to us, he said: Hani ibn Said narrated to us, he said: al-Hajjaj ibn Artah narrated to us, from Uthman ibn Abdullah ibn Mawhab, from Musa ibn Talhah, from his father, he said: Wealth was brought to Umar and he divided it among the Muslims, and a surplus remained. He consulted about it, and they said to him: If only you left it for a calamity if there is one. He said: While Ali did not speak. He said: What is wrong with you, O Abu al-Hasan, that you do not speak? He said: The people have already informed you. Umar said: You shall speak. He said: Allah has already completed the division of this wealth, and he reminded him of the hadith of the wealth of Bahrain when it came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and night came between him and dividing it, so he prayed the prayers in the mosque; I saw that on the face of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) until he finished it. He said: Without doubt you shall divide it. So Ali divided it. Talhah said: I received from it eight hundred dirhams."
"482","Said ibn Jubayr from Ibn Abbas from Ali 451 - Muhammad ibn Said al-Attar narrated to us, he said: Ubaydah ibn Humayd narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, from Ali, he said: I was a man who emitted much madhiy, so I was shy to ask the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so I ordered al-Miqdad and he asked him, and he said: In it is wudu. And we do not know anyone narrating this hadith from al-Amash except Ubaydah."
"483","Sulayman ibn Yasar from Ibn Abbas from Ali 452 - Umar ibn al-Khattab al-Sijistani narrated to us, he said: Asbagh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, from Makhramah ibn Bukayr, from his father, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, from Ibn Abbas, from Ali, he said: I was a man who emitted much madhiy, so I was shy to ask the Messenger of Allah, so I ordered a man and he asked him while I was present, and he said: In it is wudu. And we do not know of Sulayman ibn Yasar narrating from Ibn Abbas from Ali except this hadith, and it has no isnad except this isnad."
"484","Ibn Abi Mulaykah from Ibn Abbas from Ali 453 - Muhammad ibn Umar ibn Khallad al-Bahili narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn al-Sari narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Said ibn Abi Husayn narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Mulaykah, from Ibn Abbas, from Ali: That he entered upon Umar while he was covered, and said: I hope Allah gathers you with your two companions, for I used to hear the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: I went, I and Abu Bakr and Umar; I did, I and Abu Bakr and Umar. And we do not know this hadith narrated from Ali with this wording except with this isnad, and Umar ibn Said ibn al-Mubarak, and Isa ibn Yunus, and Abu Usamah and others narrated it from Umar ibn Said."
"485","Abdullah ibn al-Harith from Ibn Abbas from Ali 454 - Mahmud ibn Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to me, he said: Isa ibn al-Mukhtar narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Layla, from Abd al-Karim, from Abdullah ibn al-Harith, from Ibn Abbas, from Ali: That the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted the meat of game for one in ihram. And we do not know anyone narrating this hadith except Abd al-Karim, from Abdullah ibn al-Harith, from Ibn Abbas, from Ali. And Muhammad ibn Abi Layla narrated it from him; Imran ibn Abi Layla and Isa ibn al-Mukhtar narrated it from Muhammad."
"486","455 - Mahmud ibn Bakr narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to me, from Isa ibn al-Mukhtar, from Muhammad ibn Abi Layla, from Abd al-Karim, from Abdullah ibn al-Harith, from Ibn Abbas, from Ali, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade me to recite while bowing or prostrating. And we know no one narrating this hadith also except Ibn Abi Layla, from Abd al-Karim, from Abdullah ibn al-Harith."
"487","456 - ... from the son-in-law of Salamah ibn al-Fadl, from Salamah ibn al-Fadl, from Ibn Ishaq, from Abd al-Ghaffar ibn al-Qasim, from al-Minhal ibn Amr, from Abdullah ibn al-Harith, from Ibn Abbas, from Ali, he said: When: And warn your nearest kindred was revealed, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Ali, prepare a leg of sheep with a sa of food and gather Banu Hashim for me - and they were that day forty men or forty less a man. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called for the food and placed it among them, and they ate until they were full, though among them was one who eats a young camel with its seasoning; then he took the cup and they drank from it until satisfied - meaning of milk. Some of them said: We have seen nothing like magic; they think it was Abu Lahab who said it. He said: O Ali, prepare a leg of sheep with a sa of food and prepare a cup of milk. He said: I did, and they ate as they ate on the first day and drank as they drank the first time, and there remained as remained the first time. He said: We have not seen today anything of magic. He said: O Ali, prepare a leg of sheep with a sa of food and prepare a cup of milk. He said: I did. He said: O Ali, gather Banu Hashim for me. So I gathered them and they ate and drank, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) hastened to address them and said: Which of you will settle my debt? He said: He was silent and the people were silent. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) repeated the speech, and I said: I, O Messenger of Allah. He said: You, O Ali? You, O Ali? And we do not know this hadith narrated with this isnad connected from Ibn Abbas from Ali except from the hadith of Salamah from Ibn Ishaq, and we know no one narrating of Abdullah ibn al-Harith from Ibn Abbas from Ali except these hadiths we mentioned."
"488","Abdullah ibn Hunayn from Ibn Abbas from Ali 457 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said al-Qattan narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ajlan, from Ibrahim ibn Abdullah ibn Hunayn, from his father, from Ibn Abbas, from Ali, he said: My beloved forbade me three things - I do not say: He forbade people - wearing a gold ring, wearing qassi and mufaddamah garments, and reciting while bowing or prostrating."
"489","458 - And Ahmad ibn al-Faraj al-Himsi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ismail ibn Abi Fudayk narrated to us, he said: al-Dahhak ibn Uthman narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Abdullah ibn Hunayn, from his father, from Ibn Abbas, from Ali, he said: My beloved (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade me wearing a gold ring, wearing qassi garments, and reciting while bowing or prostrating."
"490","459 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir Abd al-Malik ibn Amr narrated to us, he said: Dawud ibn Qays narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Abdullah ibn Hunayn, from his father, from Ibn Abbas, from Ali, he said: My beloved (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade me - I do not say: He forbade people - wearing gold rings, wearing qassi and mufaddamah, and reciting while bowing or prostrating. And these hadiths of Ibn Hunayn were raised from Ibn Abbas from Ali by Muhammad ibn Ajlan, al-Dahhak, Dawud ibn Qays; and others narrate them from Ibrahim ibn Abdullah ibn Hunayn, from his father, from Ali, without mentioning Ibn Abbas."
"491","460 - Abdullah ibn Said narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Salam ibn Harb narrated to us, from Ishaq ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Farwah, from Ibrahim ibn Abdullah ibn Hunayn, from his father, from Ibn Abbas, from Ali: That the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If your lower garment is small or tight, wrap it around you, and if it is wide, then enfold yourself in it - meaning in prayer. And this Ishaq ibn Abdullah is not strong, and we know no one narrating this speech from Ibn Abbas from Ali except in this route with this isnad."
"492","Ikrimah from Ibn Abbas from Ali 461 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna and Bishr ibn Adam narrated to us, they said: Hisham ibn Abd al-Malik narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Ali said: When I proposed to Fatimah, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: What do you have? I said: I have nothing. He said: Where is your Hutamiyyah armor? He said: So I brought it to him, and he married me to Fatimah. And we do not know anyone narrating this hadith with this isnad except Hammad ibn Salamah, for he narrated it from Ayyub, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, from Ali."
"493","462 - And Said ibn Abi Arubah narrated it from Ayyub, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas: That the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Ali. Thus Abda ibn Sulayman narrated it from Said. Harun ibn Ishaq narrated to us, he said: Abda ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Said."
"494","Ubaydullah al-Khawlani from Ibn Abbas from Ali 463 - Muhammad ibn Ismail ibn Samurah al-Ahmasi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad al-Muharibi narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Muhammad ibn Talhah ibn Yazid ibn Rukanah, from Ubaydullah al-Khawlani, from Ibn Abbas."
"495","464 - And Muammal ibn Hisham narrated it to us, he said: Ismail ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Talhah ibn Yazid ibn Rukanah, from Ubaydullah al-Khawlani, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Ali ibn Abi Talib - may Allah be pleased with him - entered after pouring water; he called for wudu water, so we brought him a vessel, and he took about a mudd. When it was placed before him, he said: O Ibn Abbas, shall I not perform wudu as I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) perform wudu? He said: I said: Yes. He said: He tilted the vessel onto his hand and washed it three times, then rinsed his mouth and sniffed water and blew it out, and took a handful of water with both hands together and struck his face with it, then the second like that, then the third like that, then inserted his thumbs into what faces of his ears, then took a palmful of water with his right hand and poured it on his forehead, then let it flow over his face, then washed his right hand to the elbows and the left likewise, then wiped his head and the backs of his ears, then took with his hands a handful of water and struck his feet with it, then the second like that, then the third like that. And this hadith with these wordings, we know it narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only with this isnad. And we know no one narrating from Ubaydullah al-Khawlani except Muhammad ibn Talhah."
"496","Abu Jahmah from Ibn Abbas from Ali 465 - Yahya ibn al-Fadl narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir Abd al-Malik ibn Amr narrated to us, he said: Adi ibn al-Fadl narrated to us, from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Jahmah, from his father, from Ibn Abbas, from Ali: That the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, as far as I know: Put Quraysh forward and do not go before them; were it not that Quraysh would become arrogant, I would have informed them of what they have with Allah, Mighty and Majestic. And something like this speech was narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from more than one route, and we know it narrated from Ibn Abbas from Ali only through this route with this isnad. And Ibn al-Fadl is not a memorizer, and Abu Bakr ibn Abi Jahmah and his father - we know them narrating only this hadith."
"497","Abdullah ibn Umar from Ali 466 - al-Hasan ibn Ayyub al-Madaini narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Mualla al-Jazari informed us, from Maymun ibn Mihran, from Ibn Umar, he said: I heard Ali ibn Abi Talib - may Allah be pleased with him - saying to Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: You are trustworthy in heaven, trustworthy on earth. And we do not know this hadith narrated with this wording except with this isnad from this route. And Abu al-Mualla, his name is Furat ibn al-Saib."
"498","Abdullah ibn Jafar from Ali 467 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Habib al-Shahidi narrated to us, he said: Abda ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Abdullah ibn Jafar; 468 - and Salamah ibn Shabib narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, from Abdullah ibn Jafar, from Ali ibn Abi Talib - may Allah be pleased with him - that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The best of its women is Maryam daughter of Imran, and the best of its women is Khadijah daughter of Khuwaylid. And this hadith was narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from various routes; this is the best isnad narrated on that and the highest. And Ibn Jurayj added a man in the isnad: Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr from Abdullah ibn Jafar."
"499","469 - Ahmad ibn Aban narrated to us, he said: Anas ibn Iyad narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ajlan narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Kab, from Abdullah ibn Shaddad, from Abdullah ibn Jafar, from Ali, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) taught me, when distress befalls me, to say: There is no god but Allah, the Forbearing, the Generous; glory be to Allah; blessed is Allah, Lord of the Mighty Throne; and praise be to Allah, Lord of the worlds. And this hadith is narrated from Abdullah ibn Jafar from Ali from various routes, and this is the best isnad narrated on that."
"500","470 - Abbad ibn Yaqub narrated to us, he said: Husayn ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ismail ibn Abdullah ibn Jafar, from his father, from Ali, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded me to wash him from his well, the well of Ghars. And we do not know this hadith narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except through this route with this isnad."
"501","471 - Ubaydullah ibn Sad ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: My uncle Yaqub ibn Ibrahim ibn Sad narrated to me, he said: My father narrated to me, from Ibn Ishaq, he said: Aban ibn Salih narrated to me, from Muhammad ibn Kab al-Qurazi, from Abdullah ibn Shaddad, from Abdullah ibn Jafar, from Ali, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) taught me words to say at distress when it befalls me, so memorize them, and I memorized them. He said: Say: There is no god but Allah, the Forbearing, the Generous; blessed is Allah, Lord of the Mighty Throne; and praise be to Allah, Lord of the worlds. He said: When a matter distresses you."
"502","472 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Usamah ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Kab, from Abdullah ibn Shaddad, from Abdullah ibn Jafar, from Ali, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"503","Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr from Ali 473 - I found in my book from Muhammad ibn Abi Mansur al-Tusi, he said: Yaqub ibn Ibrahim ibn Said narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to me, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Yahya ibn Abbad, from his father, from Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, he said: I witnessed Uthman and Ali; Uthman used to forbid Umrah and combining it with Hajj. He said: While Ali entered ihram for both together. He said: They met, and Uthman said to him: You intend nothing but opposing me. He said: I do not intend to oppose you, but I will not abandon anything I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) doing for the saying of any person. And this hadith is narrated from Ali from various routes, and this is the best isnad narrated from Ali on that and the highest. And we know no one with an isnad of Ibn al-Zubayr from Ali except this hadith."
"504","Jubayr ibn Mutim from Ali 474 - Ahmad ibn Sinan and Muhammad ibn Musa al-Qattan narrated to us, they said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Sharik informed us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from Nafi ibn Jubayr, from his father, from Ali ibn Abi Talib - may Allah be pleased with him - he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was neither tall nor short, large of head and beard, coarse of hands and feet, his face tinged with redness; when he walked he leaned forward as though descending a slope. I never saw before him nor after him anyone like him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And this hadith is narrated from Ali from more than one route, and it is narrated from Ali with this isnad. And this is the best isnad narrated from Ali, and the most connected, and we know no one narrating of Jubayr ibn Mutim from Ali except this hadith."
"505","Abu Musa al-Ashari from Ali 475 - Ibrahim ibn Yusuf al-Sayrafi narrated to us, he said: Abu Yahya al-Taymi narrated to us, he said: Asim ibn Kulayb narrated to us, from Abu Burdah, from Abu Musa al-Ashari, from Ali, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Ask Allah for guidance and uprightness; and recall by guidance your guiding to the road, and by uprightness your straightening the arrow. And this hadith is narrated from Ali from more than one route, and this isnad is connected from Ali. A group narrated from Asim ibn Kulayb, from Abu Burdah, from Ali. And we know no one who said: From Abu Burdah, from his father, from Ali, except Abu Yahya al-Taymi."
"506","Abu Hurayrah from Ali 476 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna and al-Fadl ibn Sahl narrated to us, they said: Abu Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Mawhab narrated to us, from his uncle, from Abu Hurayrah, from Ali, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade me wearing saffron-dyed garments. And this hadith - Ali ibn Abi Talib only mentioned that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) singled him out with the prohibition apart from others."
"507","Abu Rafi from Ali 477 - Sulayman ibn Sayf al-Harrani narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Bazi narrated to us, from Ibn Ishaq, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Yasar narrated to me. 478 - And Ibrahim ibn Said al-Jawhari, al-Fadl ibn Sahl, and Ahmad ibn Mansur narrated to us, they said: Yaqub ibn Ibrahim ibn Sad narrated to us, from his father, from Ibn Ishaq, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Yasar narrated to me, from Ubaydullah ibn Abi Rafi, from his father, from Ali ibn Abi Talib - may Allah be pleased with him - that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Were it not that I would impose hardship on my ummah, I would have commanded them to use the siwak at every prayer, and I would have delayed the last Isha to a third of the night; for when the first third of the night passes, Allah, Blessed and Exalted, descends to the lowest heaven and remains there until dawn rises, saying: Is there no asker that he be given? Is there no caller that he be answered? Is there no intercessor-seeker that he be interceded for? Is there no repentant seeker of forgiveness that he be forgiven? And the wording is the wording of Said ibn Bazi. And this hadith was narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from various routes; we know it narrated from Ali from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only through this route with this isnad."
"508","479 - Yusuf ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Ismail narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith, from Zayd ibn Ali, from Ubaydullah ibn Abi Rafi, from Abu Rafi, from Ali: That the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood on the evening of Arafah with Usamah behind him, and said: This is the standing place, and all of Arafah is a standing place. And al-Thawri and al-Mughirah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith narrated this hadith from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith, from Zayd ibn Ali, from his father, from Ubaydullah ibn Abi Rafi, from Ali, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); and Ibrahim ibn Ismail differed from them in this isnad, saying: From Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith, from Zayd ibn Ali, from Ubaydullah ibn Abi Rafi, from his father, from Ali ibn Abi Talib - may Allah be pleased with him - and the correct is the hadith of al-Thawri and al-Mughirah."
"509","Abu Said al-Khudri from Ali 480 - Abdullah ibn Ayyub narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Yazid al-Sudai narrated to us, from Sadan al-Juhani, from Atiyyah al-Awfi, from Abu Said al-Khudri, he said: I asked Ali ibn Abi Talib and said: O Abu al-Hasan, which is better: walking behind the funeral or before it? He said: O Abu Said, and one like you asks about this? I said: Who asks about this but one like me? I saw Abu Bakr and Umar walking before it. He said: May Allah have mercy on them and forgive them; by Allah, they heard as we heard, but they were lenient and loved ease. O Abu Said, when you walk behind your Muslim brother, be silent and reflect upon yourself as though you have become like him. Your brother used to contend with you over the world; he exited it despoiled, stripped, with nothing but what he sent forth of righteous deeds. When you reach the grave and people sit, do not sit, but stand at the edge of his grave. When he is lowered into his pit, say: In the name of Allah and in the path of Allah, and upon the religion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). O Allah, Your slave has descended to You, and You are the best to descend upon. He left the world behind his back, so make what he has come to better than what he left behind, for You said: And what is with Allah is better for the righteous. Then throw upon him three handfuls. And this hadith enters the musnad of Ali because of what he said: By Allah, they heard as we heard, but they made ease. And we know no one narrating of Atiyyah from Abu Said from Ali except this hadith."
"510","481 - Ahmad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Ismail narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from al-Jurayri, from Abu Nadrah, from Abu Said, he said: Ali said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A people will emerge reciting the Quran, and it will not pass their throats; they will pass through religion as an arrow passes through the target. And this hadith is only known from Abu Said from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and its end becomes from Ali. And Hammad ibn Salamah narrated from Abu Harun from Abu Said, and from al-Jurayri from Abu Nadrah from Abu Said."
"511","What Abu Juhayfah narrated from Ali 482 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Mukharrimi narrated to us, he said: al-Hajjaj ibn Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Abi Ishaq narrated to us, from his father, from Abu Juhayfah, from Ali, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever receives a prescribed punishment in this world and is punished with it, Allah, Mighty and Majestic, is more just than to double the punishment upon His slave; and whoever commits a sin in this world and Allah conceals it for him and pardons him, Allah, Mighty and Majestic, is more generous than to return to something He already pardoned. And we know no one narrating this hadith from Yunus ibn Abi Ishaq, from his father, from Abu Juhayfah, from Ali, except al-Hajjaj."
"512","483 - And al-Hasan ibn Khalaf narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Yusuf narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik, from Abu Hamzah al-Thumali, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu Juhayfah, from Ali, that he said: I narrate to you a hadith which every Muslim - or the Muslims - must retain. We said: Yes. So he narrated it to us at the beginning of the day and we forgot it by the end of the day. We came to him and said to him: The hadith you narrated to us that the Muslims must retain - we forgot it, so repeat it to us. He said: There is no Muslim slave who commits a sin and Allah seizes him for it in this world and punishes him with it, except that Allah, Mighty and Majestic, is more generous than to return to his punishment on the Day of Resurrection; and there is no Muslim who commits a sin and Allah forgives him for it in this world, except that Allah is more generous than to return to his punishment on the Day of Resurrection for what He pardoned. Then he recited this verse: And whatever misfortune befalls you is because of what your hands earned, and He pardons much."
"513","484 - Yusuf ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Hakam ibn Bashir ibn Salman narrated to us, he said: I heard him mentioning it from Khallad al-Saffar, from al-Hakam al-Nasri, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu Juhayfah, from Ali, he said: Two phrases I memorized from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and I love that you memorize them from me: That the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The screen between you and the jinn is to say: Bismillah. And we do not know this hadith narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except through this route, and it was narrated from Anas from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: The screen between the eyes of the jinn and the private parts of the children of Adam."
"514","485 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna ibn Ubayd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Jafar narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Awn ibn Abi Juhayfah, from his father, he said: Ali said: When I narrate to you from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a hadith, falling from the sky is dearer to me than saying what he did not say; but when I do not say to you: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, then I am at war, and war is deception."
"515","486 - Khalaf ibn Khalifah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Ismail, from al-Shabi, from Abu Juhayfah, he said: I said to Ali ibn Abi Talib: Did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entrust to you anything he did not entrust to people? He said: No, except what is in this sheet; in it is: freeing the captive, and no Muslim is killed for a disbeliever; the blood of Muslims is equal. And Mutarrif narrated this hadith from al-Shabi, from Abu Juhayfah, from Ali."
"516","Amr ibn Hurayth from Ali 487 - Ibrahim ibn Said al-Jawhari narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn al-Numan al-Jufi Abu Ishaq narrated to us, he said: I heard al-Rabi ibn Said al-Jufi, he said: al-Walid ibn Sari, the freedman of Amr ibn Hurayth, narrated to us, he said: We went out with the Commander of the Faithful Ali ibn Abi Talib - may Allah be pleased with him - on a feast day, and some of his companions asked him: O Commander of the Faithful, what do you say about prayer on Eid day before the imam and after him? He said: He did not reply to them anything. Then another people came and asked him as those before them had asked, and he did not reply to them. When we reached the prayer, he led people in prayer, magnifying seven and five times, then addressed people, then descended and mounted. They said: O Commander of the Faithful, these are people praying. He said: What could I do? You asked me about the sunnah: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not pray before it nor after it. Whoever wishes may do, and whoever wishes may leave. Do you think I would prevent people who pray, and be like one who prevents a slave from praying? And we do not know this hadith narrated from Amr ibn Hurayth except through this route with this isnad, and we know it narrated from Ali only through this connected route."
"517","488 - Abu Kurayb narrated to us, he said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, he said: Harun ibn Salman narrated to us, from Amr ibn Hurayth, from Ali, he said: The best of this ummah after its Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is Abu Bakr, and after Abu Bakr is Umar; and if I wished to name the third I would name him."
"518","Jabir ibn Abdullah from Ali 489 - Abbad ibn Yaqub narrated to us, he said: Hatim ibn Ismail narrated to us, he said: Jafar ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from his father, from Jabir ibn Abdullah: That the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) remained nine years without performing Hajj, then it was announced in the tenth that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would perform Hajj; and he mentioned the Hajj of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) until he reached in the narration to: Ali came from Yemen with sacrificial camels for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and found Fatimah wearing dyed clothes and having applied kohl, so Ali disapproved of that to her, and she said: My father ordered me with that. Ali entered upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: Ali used to say: I went to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) complaining about Fatimah for what she did, disapproving of her, and informed him of what she said. He said: She spoke truthfully, she spoke truthfully. Then he said: With what did you enter ihram when you undertook Hajj? He said: I said: O Allah, I enter ihram with what Your Messenger entered ihram. He said: I have the sacrificial animal with me. And this hadith from this point enters the hadith of Jabir ibn Abdullah from Ali."
"519","490 - Ishaq ibn Ziyad al-Ayli narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to me, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Jabir, he said: Ali said: I was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and Abu Bakr and Umar came, and he said: These two are the masters of the elderly of Paradise among the first and the last, except the prophets and messengers. Do not inform them, O Ali. And we do not know this hadith narrated from Jabir from Ali except through this route with this isnad."
"520","Abu al-Tufayl from Ali 491 - Yusuf ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Marwan ibn Muawiyah narrated to us, he said: Mansur ibn Hayyan narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Tufayl narrated to us, he said: I was with Ali when a man came to him and said: O Commander of the Faithful, what did the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) confide to you? He became angry and said: He did not confide to me anything he concealed from people except that he narrated to me four phrases. He said: What are they? He said: Allah curses whoever curses his parents, and Allah curses whoever alters the landmarks of the earth - Marwan said: Whoever takes from anothers land. And this hadith with this wording, we know it narrated from Ali only through this route with this isnad."
"521","492 - Yusuf ibn Musa al-Qattan and Muhammad ibn Uthman ibn Karamah narrated to us - and the wording is Yusufs - they said: Ubaydullah ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Fitr narrated to us, from Abu al-Tufayl, he said: I heard Ali adjuring people in al-Rahbah: I adjure by Allah every Muslim who heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying on the day of Ghadir Khumm what the Imam said. Some people stood and testified that we saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) take Alis hand, saying: Am I not more entitled to the Muslims than themselves? They said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Whoever I am his mawla - O Allah, befriend whoever befriends him and oppose whoever opposes him. And this hadith was narrated from Ali from more than one route, and Fitr narrated it from Abu al-Tufayl from Ali, and Maruf ibn Kharrabudh narrated it."
"522","493 - Yusuf ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Fitr narrated to us, from al-Qasim ibn Abi Bazzah, from Abu al-Tufayl, from Ali: That the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If only one day remains of the world, Allah will send a man from my household who will fill it with justice as it was filled with injustice. And we do not know this hadith narrated from Ali with this wording with an isnad better than this isnad."
"523","494 - Yusuf ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Fitr narrated to us, from al-Qasim ibn Abi Bazzah, from Abu al-Tufayl, he said: We said to Ali: Did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) leave among you a book besides the Quran? He said: No, except this sheet in the hilt of his sword; in it is: Allah curses whoever alters - or a word like it - the landmarks of the earth, meaning: taking something from the road."
"524","495 - Umar ibn Muhammad ibn al-Hasan al-Asadi narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ubaydullah narrated to us, from al-Qasim ibn Abi Bazzah, from Abu al-Tufayl, from Ali, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah curses whoever curses his parents, Allah curses whoever slaughters for other than Allah, and Allah curses whoever alters the landmarks of the earth. And we know no one narrating it from al-Qasim ibn Abi Bazzah, from Abu al-Tufayl, from Ali, except Muhammad ibn Ubaydullah. And we heard it only from Umar ibn Muhammad ibn al-Hasan al-Asadi."
"525","Abu Layla from Ali 496 - Yusuf ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Layla narrated to us, from al-Hakam, and al-Minhal, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from his father, he said: I said to Ali - and he used to converse with him at night - People disapprove of you going out in the heat in heavy stuffed garments, and in winter in two light sheets. Ali said: Were you not with us? I said: Yes. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called Abu Bakr and tied a banner for him, then sent him; he marched with people and was defeated until he reached and returned. Then he called Umar and tied a banner for him, and he marched then returned defeated with people. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I shall give the banner to a man who loves Allah and His Messenger, and whom Allah and His Messenger love; Allah will grant him victory; he is not a fleer. He sent to me and called me, and I came to him while I had ophthalmia and could see nothing. He spat in my eyes and said: O Allah, suffice him the pain of heat and cold. So neither heat nor cold harmed me after that."
"526","Abd al-Rahman ibn Abza from Ali 497 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Abu Farwah - and he is not the one who narrates from Ibn Abi Layla - from Zaidah al-Hamdani, from Ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abza, from his father, from Ali: That Abu Bakr and Umar were in a funeral walking before it, while Ali walked behind it. I said to Ali, and he said: They both indeed knew that walking behind it is better, but they are lenient and make ease for people. And we know no one narrating of Ibn Abza from Ali except this hadith."
"527","Abu Umamah al-Bahili from Ali 498 - Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahim narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn al-Hasan ibn Shaqiq narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Ayyub, from Ubaydullah ibn Zahr, from Ali ibn Yazid, from al-Qasim, from Abu Umamah, from Ali, he said: I used to come to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and seek permission; if he was in prayer he cleared his throat, and if he was not in prayer he permitted me. And this hadith is narrated from Ali through this route, and from the hadith of Abdullah ibn Nujayy from Ali. And this isnad and the other isnad narrated on that are not strong. And this isnad is better connected, because it is from a Companion from Ali, even though Ubaydullah ibn Zahr, Ali ibn Yazid, and al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Rahman are in it."
"528","al-Hasan ibn Ali from Ali 499 - Yusuf ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Mihran ibn Abi Umar narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn al-Mutawakkil narrated to us, he said: Kathir al-Nawwa narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn al-Hasan ibn Ali, from his father, from his grandfather, from Ali, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There will be a people at the end of time called al-Rafidah; they reject Islam. And we know no isnad for this hadith from al-Hasan except this isnad."
"529","al-Husayn ibn Ali from Ali 500 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna and Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, they said: Muhammad ibn Abi Adi narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, he said: Aban ibn Salih narrated to me, from Ikrimah, he said: I stood with al-Husayn ibn Ali at Muzdalifah, and I kept hearing him saying: Labbayk, Labbayk, until he stoned the jamrah. I said: O Abu Abdullah, what is this talbiyah? He said: I heard Ali ibn Abi Talib - may Allah be pleased with him - reciting talbiyah until he reached the jamrah, and he narrated to me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited talbiyah until he reached it. And this hadith is of good isnad, and we know it narrated from Ali only through this route."
"530","501 - Abdullah ibn Shabib narrated to us, he said: Ayyub ibn Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Uways narrated to me, from Sulayman ibn Bilal, from Jafar ibn Muhammad, from his father, from Ali ibn al-Husayn, from his father, from Ali, he said: We performed tamattu of Umrah to Hajj with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And this speech was narrated from Ali, and this isnad is the best isnad narrated from Ali on that."
"531","502 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim al-Dahhak ibn Makhlad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, he said: Ibn Shihab informed me, from Ali ibn Husayn, from his father, from Ali ibn Abi Talib - may Allah be pleased with him - he said: I obtained a she-camel on the day of Badr, and he - may Allah be pleased with him - gave me ... he said: I obtained a she-camel, and made it kneel at the door of a man from the Ansar, wanting to load idhkhir upon them to sell it, with me a goldsmith man from Banu Qaynuqa, wanting to seek his help for the wedding feast of Fatimah, while Hamzah ibn Abd al-Muttalib was in the house drinking, and a singing girl sang to him: O Abu Hamzah, with the fat camels. He said: He rushed to them with the sword, slit their flanks, and took from their livers. He said: I said to Ibn Shihab: And from the hump - he had cut off their humps. He said: I looked at a matter that frightened me. He said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with Zayd ibn Harithah, and I went out with him until he came upon Hamzah. He said: He became angry at him. He said: Hamzah raised his gaze and said: Are you but the slaves of my father? He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) retreated from him. And we know no one narrating this hadith except al-Zuhri from Ali ibn Husayn from his father from his grandfather, and more than one narrated it from al-Zuhri, among them Yunus ibn Yazid and Ibn Jurayj."
"532","503 - Umar ibn al-Khattab al-Sijistani narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Yaman narrated to us, he said: Shuayb ibn Abi Hamzah narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, he said: Ali ibn Husayn narrated to me that al-Husayn ibn Ali informed him that Ali informed him: That the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) knocked on his and Fatimahs door at night and said: Will you not pray? I said: O Prophet of Allah, our souls are in Allahs hand; when He wishes to send them He sends them. He departed when he heard that and did not return anything to me, then I heard him as he turned away saying: And man is most contentious of things."
"533","504 - Ubaydullah ibn Sad narrated to us, from Yaqub ibn Ibrahim ibn Sad, from his father, from Ibn Ishaq, he said: Hakim ibn Hakim ibn Abbad ibn Hunayf narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, from Ali ibn al-Husayn, from his father, from Ali ibn Abi Talib - may Allah be pleased with him - he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon Fatimah after praying the dawn prayer, while she had prayed and lain down; then he mentioned the like of the hadith of Shuayb ibn Abi Hamzah from al-Zuhri."
"534","505 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Bazi narrated to us, he said: al-Sakan ibn Harun al-Bahili narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn Jafar ibn al-Hasan ibn Ali narrated to me, he said: Abdullah ibn al-Hasan ibn al-Hasan narrated to me, from his mother Fatimah daughter of al-Husayn, from her father, from Ali ibn Abi Talib - may Allah be pleased with him -: That the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The devils have despaired of being worshipped in this land of mine - meaning Madinah - and in the Arabian Peninsula, but by incitement among them. And we do not know this hadith narrated from Ali ibn Abi Talib - may Allah be pleased with him - from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except through this route with this isnad."
"535","506 - Hatim ibn al-Layth narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Abu Maymunah narrated to us, from Isa al-Madani, from Ali ibn Husayn, from his father, from Ali ibn Abi Talib - may Allah be pleased with him - he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took my hand and said: Musa asked his Lord to purify his mosque through Harun, and I asked my Lord to purify my mosque through you and your offspring. Then he sent to Abu Bakr: Close your door. He said: We belong to Allah and to Him return, then said: Hearing and obedience, and closed his door. Then he sent to Umar, then sent to al-Abbas likewise. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It is not I who closed your doors and opened Alis door, but Allah opened Alis door and closed your doors. And we do not know this hadith narrated with this wording from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except with this isnad. And in it are two defects: one is that Abu Maymunah is an unknown man; none is known to narrate from him except Ubaydullah ibn Musa. And Isa al-Mulai - we know him narrating only this hadith. We only wrote this hadith because we did not memorize it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so we mentioned it and clarified its defect."
"536","507 - Muhammad ibn al-Husayn al-Qaysi narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Arqam narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Abdullah ibn Hasan ibn Hasan narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Ali ibn al-Husayn, from his father, from his grandfather, from Ali ibn Abi Talib - may Allah be pleased with him - he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) led us in dawn prayer; when he finished his prayer, a man called to him: When is the Hour? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) rebuked him and scolded him, saying: Be silent - until when morning brightened he raised his glance to the sky and said: Blessed is its Raiser and Regulator. Then he cast his gaze to the earth and said: Blessed is its Spreader and Creator. Then he said: Where is the asker about the Hour? The man knelt on his knees and said: It is I, may my father and mother be sacrificed for you, who asked you. He said: That is at the injustice of rulers, belief in stars, denial of decree, when leadership is taken as booty, charity as a fine, and obscenity as excess; at that, your people perish. And we do not know this hadith narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with this wording except through this route with this isnad. And Yunus ibn Arqam was truthful; people of knowledge narrated from him and accepted his hadith, though he had intense Shiism."
"537","508 - Muhammad ibn Uthman ibn Makhlad al-Wasiti narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, from Ziyad ibn al-Mundhir, from Muhammad ibn Ali ibn al-Husayn, from his father, from his grandfather, from Ali, he said: When Allah wanted to teach His Messenger the adhan, Jibril came to him - peace be upon them both - on a mount called al-Buraq. He went to ride it but it became difficult, so Jibril said to it: Be still, for by Allah no slave more noble to Allah than Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) has ridden you. He said: He rode it until he reached the veil next to al-Rahman, Blessed and Exalted. He said: While he was thus, an angel came out of the veil. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Jibril, who is this? He said: By the One who sent you with the truth, I am the nearest of creation in place, and I have not seen this angel since I was created before this hour of mine. The angel said: Allahu Akbar, Allahu Akbar. He said: It was said to him from behind the veil: My slave spoke truth; I am Greater, I am Greater. Then the angel said: I bear witness there is no god but Allah. He said: It was said to him from behind the veil: My slave spoke truth; I - there is no god but I. He said: The angel said: I bear witness Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah. He said: It was said from behind the veil: My slave spoke truth; I sent Muhammad. The angel said: Come to prayer, come to success, prayer has commenced. Then the angel said: Allahu Akbar, Allahu Akbar. He said: It was said from behind the veil: My slave spoke truth; I am Greater, I am Greater. Then he said: There is no god but Allah. He said: It was said from behind the veil: My slave spoke truth; there is no god but I. He said: Then the angel took Muhammads (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) hand and put him forward, and the people of heaven aspired - among them Adam and Nuh. Abu Jafar Muhammad ibn Ali said: That day Allah completed for Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) honor over the people of heavens and earth. And we do not know this hadith narrated with this wording from Ali except with this isnad. And Ziyad ibn al-Mundhir had Shiism in him, and Marwan ibn Muawiyah and others narrated from him."
"538","509 - Hatim ibn al-Layth al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Abi Uways narrated to us, he said: Isa ibn Jafar ibn Ibrahim al-Talibi narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Umar ibn Ali ibn al-Husayn narrated to me, he said: My father narrated to me, from my grandfather Ali ibn Abi Talib - may Allah be pleased with him - he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not make my grave a festival, nor your houses graves; send blessings upon me and greet me, for your prayer reaches me. And we do not know this hadith narrated from Ali except through this route with this isnad. And with this isnad righteous hadiths were narrated containing objectionable things, so we mentioned this hadith because it is not objectionable: Do not make my grave a festival nor your houses graves. It was narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from other than this route."
"539","510 - Muhammad ibn Mamar narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, he said: Shaybah - meaning Ibn Muhammad - informed me that Muhammad ibn Ali informed him that al-Husayn ibn Ali informed him, from Ali, that he performed wudu three times three times, drank the remainder of his wudu, washed his hands before inserting them into the vessel, and said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) doing that."
"540","Abu Said ibn al-Mualla from Ali 511 - Abd al-Samad ibn Sulayman al-Muqri narrated to us, he said: Abu Nubatah Yunus ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Salamah ibn Wardan narrated to us, from Abu Said ibn al-Mualla, from Ali ibn Abi Talib - may Allah be pleased with him - and Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: What is between my grave and my minbar - or he said: My house and my minbar - is a garden from the gardens of Paradise."
"541","Umarah ibn Ruwaybah from Ali 512 - Salamah ibn Shabib narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn al-Wazir narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Jabir narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from Umarah ibn Ruwaybah, from Ali, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: People follow Quraysh; their righteous follow their righteous, and their wicked follow their wicked. And we do not know anyone narrating this hadith from Ali except Umarah ibn Ruwaybah, and Umarah narrated from Ali only this hadith, and none narrated it from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr except Muhammad ibn Jabir. And Umarah ibn Ruwaybah is a man from among the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); hadiths were narrated from him."
"542","Tariq ibn Shihab from Ali 513 - Ibrahim ibn Said narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, from Sharik, from Mukhariq, from Tariq ibn Shihab, he said: I saw Ali saying on the minbar: We have no book we read to you except the Book of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and this sheet, a sheet hanging in his sword; and he mentioned that in it are the obligations of charity which he took - meaning - in the lifetime of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And we know no one narrating of Tariq ibn Shihab from Ali except this hadith. And Tariq is a man who saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). End of the sixth part, and praise be to Allah abundantly as He deserves."
"543","And from what Marwan ibn al-Hakam narrated from Ali ibn Abi Talib 514 - Abu Umar Ahmad ibn Muhammad informed us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Yahya ibn Mufarrij informed us, he said: Abu al-Hasan Muhammad ibn Ayyub ibn Habib al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn Amr ibn Abd al-Khaliq al-Bazzar narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Jafar narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from al-Hakam ibn Utaybah, from Ali ibn Husayn, from Marwan ibn al-Hakam, he said: I witnessed Uthman and Ali - may Allah be pleased with them both - between Makkah and Madinah, while Uthman forbade tamattu and combining them - meaning Hajj and Umrah. When Ali - may Allah be pleased with him - saw that, he entered ihram for both together and said: Labbayk, Hajj and Umrah together. Uthman said: Do you see me forbidding people while you do it? He said: I would not abandon the sunnah of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for the saying of any person."
"544","515 - Ahmad narrated to us, he said: And Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated it to us, he said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us. 516 - And Yusuf ibn Musa narrated it to us, he said: Jarir narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Muslim, from Ali ibn Husayn, from Marwan ibn al-Hakam, from Ali - may Allah be pleased with him - from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"545","517 - And Ishaq ibn Shahin narrated it to us, he said: Khalid ibn Abdullah narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Ali ibn Husayn, from Marwan ibn al-Hakam, from Ali - may Allah be pleased with him - from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with the like of it."
"546","And from what Malik ibn Aws ibn al-Hadathan narrated from Ali 518 - Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Aban narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ibn Shihab, from Malik ibn Aws ibn al-Hadathan: That Umar - may Allah be pleased with him - adjured people, among them Talhah, al-Zubayr, and Abd al-Rahman, and said to Ali and al-Abbas - may Allah be pleased with them both: Do you both know that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: We are not inherited; what we leave is charity? They said: Yes, and Ali ibn Abi Talib - may Allah be pleased with him - said: Yes. And we know no one narrating of Malik ibn Aws from Ali - may Allah be pleased with him - except this hadith."
"547","And from what Said ibn al-Musayyib narrated from Ali 519 - Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn al-Rabi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated to us, from Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, he said: Ali - may Allah be pleased with him - said: When I washed the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), I went to look for what comes from the dead, and I saw nothing. I said: You were pure alive and dead. And al-Zuhri narrated this hadith from Said from Ali. And some companions of al-Zuhri narrated it from al-Zuhri from Said: That Ali - may Allah be pleased with him - when he washed the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - without saying: From Ali - may Allah be pleased with him."
"548","And from what Yahya ibn Said narrated from Said from Ali 520 - He said: al-Hasan ibn al-Sabbah al-Bazzaz and Ibrahim ibn Said narrated to us, they said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Said, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, from Ali - may Allah be pleased with him - he said: I never saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) joining his parents for anyone except Sad; for on the day of Uhud he kept saying: Shoot, may my father and mother be sacrificed for you. And we know no one narrating it from Yahya ibn Said, from Said, from Ali, except Ibn Uyaynah; others narrate it from Said ibn al-Musayyib from Sad."
"549","And from what Abd al-Rahman ibn Harmalah narrated from Said from Ali 521 - Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said al-Qattan narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Harmalah narrated to us, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, he said: Ali and Uthman - may Allah be pleased with them both - met; Uthman used to forbid tamattu, while Ali enjoined it. Uthman said: What do you intend by this? Ali - may Allah be pleased with him - said: This is something the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did; I will not abandon it."
"550","522 - Ali ibn Harb al-Mawsili narrated to us, he said: Harun ibn Imran narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Abi Dawud al-Jazari narrated to us, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, from Ali, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with the like of it. And we know no one narrating of Ibn Harmalah from Said from Ali - may Allah be pleased with him - from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except this hadith."
"551","And from what Qatadah narrated from Said from Ali 523 - Ibrahim ibn Ziyad al-Saigh narrated to us, he said: Waki ibn al-Jarrah narrated to us, from Hisham - meaning the companion of al-Dastuwai - from Qatadah, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, from Ali - may Allah be pleased with him - he said: I prepared food and invited the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He came and saw pictures in the house, so he returned. I said: O Messenger of Allah, why did you return? He said: In the house is something containing pictures, and angels do not enter a house containing pictures. And this hadith is from the best isnad narrated from Ali - may Allah be pleased with him - on that, and we know no one connecting this hadith from Qatadah, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, from Ali - may Allah be pleased with him - except Waki from Hisham. And it was narrated from Abu Talhah, Aishah, Zayd ibn Khalid, and Abu Hurayrah; we mentioned it from Ali - may Allah be pleased with him - since its isnad is sound."
"552","And from what Ali ibn Zayd narrated from Said ibn al-Musayyib from Ali 524 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Abu Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us. 525 - And Yusuf ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Waki narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ali ibn Zayd, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, from Ali - may Allah be pleased with him - he said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, shall I not guide you to the best young woman in Quraysh? He said: Do you have anything? I said: Yes, the daughter of Hamzah. He said: She is the daughter of my brother through breastfeeding, O Ali. Do you not know that Allah prohibited through breastfeeding what He prohibited through lineage? And we know no one saying: From Ali ibn Zayd, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, from Ali, except Sufyan al-Thawri; others say: From Ali ibn Zayd, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, from Ibn Abbas."
"553","526 - Muhammad ibn al-Husayn al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Malik ibn Ismail narrated to us, he said: Qays narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Imran, from Ali ibn Zayd, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, from Ali - may Allah be pleased with him - that he was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: What thing is best for a woman? They were silent. When I returned I said to Fatimah: What thing is best for women? She said: That men do not see them. I mentioned that to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: Fatimah is only a part of me - may Allah be pleased with her. And we know no isnad for this hadith from Ali - may Allah be pleased with him - except this isnad."
"554","And from what Amr ibn Murrah narrated from Said ibn al-Musayyib from Ali 527 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Jafar narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Amr ibn Murrah, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, he said: Ali and Uthman - may Allah be pleased with them both - met at Usfan; Uthman used to forbid tamattu or Umrah. He said: Ali - may Allah be pleased with him - said: What do you intend toward a matter the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did, forbidding it? Uthman said: Leave us alone from you. When Ali - may Allah be pleased with him - saw that, he entered ihram for both together."
"555","And from what al-Harith ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated from Said ibn al-Musayyib from Ali 528 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna and Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, they said: Safwan ibn Isa narrated to us, he said: al-Harith ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, from Ali: That the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Shall I not guide you to what Allah expiates sins with? Perfecting wudu in disliked things, much walking to mosques, and waiting for prayer after prayer; it washes sins away. And this hadith - thus Safwan narrated it from al-Harith from Said ibn al-Musayyib. And Anas ibn Iyad and others said: From al-Harith, from Abu al-Abbas, from Said ibn al-Musayyib; and Abu al-Abbas is unknown."
"556","529 - Umar ibn al-Khattab narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Zinad narrated to us, from al-Harith ibn Abd al-Rahman, he said: Abu al-Abbas narrated to us, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, from Ali ibn Abi Talib - may Allah be pleased with him - from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with the like of it."
"557","And from what Ubaydullah ibn Abi Rafi narrated from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him 530 - Ahmad ibn Aban al-Qurashi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from al-Hasan ibn Muhammad, from Ubaydullah ibn Abi Rafi - and he was a scribe for Ali - that he heard Ali saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent me, I and al-Miqdad and al-Zubayr, saying: Go until you come to Rawdat Khakh, for there is a traveler woman with a letter; take it from her. We set out with our horses racing until we reached the Rawdah, and there was the traveler woman. We said: Bring out the letter. She said: I have no letter. I said: You shall bring out the letter or you shall cast off your clothes. She brought it out from her braids. We brought it to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and in it was: From Hatib ibn Abi Baltaah to some of the polytheists in Makkah, informing them of some of the matters of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Hatib, what is this? He said: O Messenger of Allah, do not hasten against me. I was attached to Quraysh and was not of themselves, and those with you from the Muhajirun have relatives in Makkah protecting their relatives through them, so I wanted to make a favor among them protecting my relatives through it. I did not do that out of disbelief, nor apostasy from my religion, nor preferring disbelief. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: He has told you the truth. He - I think - Umar said: O Messenger of Allah, let me strike the neck of this hypocrite. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: How do you know? Perhaps Allah looked upon this group of the people of Badr and said: Do whatever you wish, for I have forgiven you. And this hadith was narrated from Ali ibn Abi Talib - may Allah be pleased with him - from more than this route, and this isnad is the best isnad narrated on that from Ali and the soundest. We mentioned it from Umar in the story of Hatib with other than this wording, so we mentioned it from Ali since its wording was other than that wording and its isnad was sound."
"558","531 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Abu Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith, from Zayd ibn Ali, from his father, from Ubaydullah ibn Abi Rafi, from Ali - may Allah be pleased with him."
"559","532 - And Ahmad ibn Abdah narrated it to us, he said: al-Mughirah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Ayyash ibn Abi Rabiah informed us, from his father, from Zayd ibn Ali, from his father, from Ubaydullah ibn Abi Rafi, from Ali - may Allah be pleased with him - he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood at Arafah and said: This is Arafah and it is the standing place. Then he departed when the sun set, with Usamah behind him, proceeding gently, while people struck right and left, not turning to them, saying: O people, calmness is upon you. Then he came to Jam and prayed both prayers together with them. When morning came he came to Qazah and stood, saying: This is Qazah and it is the standing place, and all of Jam is a standing place. Then he departed; when he reached the head of Muhassir he struck his she-camel and it hastened until when he passed the valley he stopped and took al-Fadl behind him. Then he came to the jamrah and stoned it, then came to the slaughter place and said: This is the slaughter place, and all of Mina is a slaughter place. A young woman faced him and said: My father is a very old man, senile, and Allahs obligatory Hajj has reached him; does it suffice that I perform Hajj for him? He said: Perform Hajj for your father. He said: He twisted al-Fadls neck. He said: al-Abbas said: Why did you twist your cousins neck? He said: I saw a young man and a young woman and did not feel safe from Satan upon them. He said: A man came to him and said: O Messenger of Allah, I departed before shaving. He said: Shave or shorten; no harm. He said: A man came to him and said: O Messenger of Allah, I slaughtered before stoning. He said: Stone; no harm. He said: Then he came to the House and circumambulated, then came to Zamzam and said: O Banu Abd al-Muttalib, your watering; were it not that people would overpower you, I would have drawn with it. And we do not know this hadith narrated with this wording except from Ali ibn Abi Talib - may Allah be pleased with him - with this isnad. And Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith was narrated from by al-Thawri, Sulayman ibn Bilal, Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Zinad, his son al-Mughirah ibn Abd al-Rahman, and others; as for this hadith, we know no one narrating it except al-Thawri and al-Mughirah ibn Abd al-Rahman."
"560","533 - Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Bukayr narrated to us, he said: Israil narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Rafi, from his father, from his uncle Ubaydullah ibn Abi Rafi, from Ali - may Allah be pleased with him - he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he saw what he disliked, said: Praise be to Allah in every circumstance; and when he saw what pleased him, he said: Praise be to Allah, by whose favor righteous deeds are completed. And we do not know this hadith narrated from Ali ibn Abi Talib - may Allah be pleased with him - except with this isnad."
"561","534 - Rizqullah ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Ishaq narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Khalid narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn al-Fadl, from Ubaydullah ibn Abi Rafi, from Ali - may Allah be pleased with him - he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: One of a group greeting suffices the group, and one of those sitting replying suffices."
"562","535 - Abdullah ibn Shabib narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Ismail - meaning Ibn Abi Habibah - narrated to us, from Dawud ibn Husayn, from al-Araj, from Ubaydullah ibn Abi Rafi, from Ali - may Allah be pleased with him - he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade me to sleep except upon witr."
"563","536 - Muhammad ibn Abd al-Malik al-Qurashi narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Abi Salamah al-Majishun narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to me, from Abd al-Rahman al-Araj, from Ubaydullah ibn Abi Rafi, from Ali - may Allah be pleased with him -: That the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he stood for prayer, said: Allahu Akbar; I have turned my face to the One who originated the heavens and earth, upright, and I am not of the polytheists. My prayer and my sacrifice and my living and my dying are for Allah, Lord of the worlds, with no partner for Him; with that I am commanded and I am of the Muslims. O Allah, You are the King, there is no god but You; You are my Lord and I am Your slave; I wronged myself and confessed my sin, so forgive me all my sins, for none forgives sins except You. Guide me to the best of character; none guides to its best except You; and turn its evil from me, for none turns its evil from me except You. At Your service and blessed; all good is in Your hands, and evil is not to You; I am by You and to You; blessed and exalted are You; I seek Your forgiveness and repent to You. And when he bowed he said: O Allah, to You I bowed, to You I submitted, in You I believed; my hearing, my sight, my bones, my marrow, and my nerves humbled to You. And when he raised his head he said: Allah hears whoever praises Him; our Lord, and to You is praise, filling the heavens and filling the earth and filling what is between them, and filling whatever You wish after that. And when he prostrated he said: O Allah, to You I prostrated, to You I submitted, in You I believed; my face prostrated to the One who created it and formed it and perfected its form, and split its hearing and sight; blessed is Allah, the best of creators. And when he greeted he said: O Allah, forgive me what I advanced and delayed, what I concealed and exceeded, and what You know better than I; You are the Advancer and Delayer; there is no god but You. And this speech was narrated in similar and close form by Muhammad ibn Maslamah, Abu Rafi, and Jabir; the most complete of them in this hadith in speech and soundest in isnad is the hadith of Ali - may Allah be pleased with him. People only bore it upon night prayer."
"564","537 - Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Said al-Qattan narrated to us, he said: Abu Usamah narrated to us, he said: Abu Kudaynah narrated to us, from Mutarrif, from al-Shabi, from Masruq, he said: I heard Ali - may Allah be pleased with him - saying in something: Allah and His Messenger spoke truthfully. It was said to him: Is this something you heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: War is deception. And we know no one narrating of Masruq from Ali - may Allah be pleased with him - a hadith leaning toward the musnad except this hadith."
"565","And from what Abidah al-Salmani narrated from Ali 538 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahhab narrated to us, he said: Ayyub narrated to us, from Muhammad - meaning Ibn Sirin - from Abidah al-Salmani: That Ali - may Allah be pleased with him - mentioned the Khawarij and said: Among them is a man with a small hand, or deficient hand, or deformed hand. Were it not that you would become arrogant, I would narrate to you what Allah promised those who fight them, upon the tongue of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Abidah said: I said: Did you hear this from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: Yes, by the Lord of the Kabah; yes, by the Lord of the Kabah. And a group narrated this hadith from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Abidah, from Ali - may Allah be pleased with him - among them Ayyub, Ibn Awn, Qatadah, Yunus ibn Ubayd, Awn, Abu Amr ibn al-Ala, Yazid ibn Ibrahim, and Jarir ibn Hazim. As for the hadith of Ayyub, Abd al-Wahhab narrated it."
"566","539 - And Muammal ibn Hisham also narrated it to us, he said: Ismail ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Abidah, from Ali - may Allah be pleased with him."
"567","540 - And Amr ibn Ali and Muhammad ibn Bashshar narrated it to us, they said: Muadh ibn Hisham narrated to us, from his father, from Qatadah, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Abidah, from Ali - may Allah be pleased with him."
"568","541 - And al-Fadl ibn Yaqub al-Rukhami narrated it to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn Bilal narrated to us, he said: Mubarak ibn Fadalah narrated to us, from Yunus ibn Ubayd, from Ibn Sirin, from Abidah, from Ali - may Allah be pleased with him."
"569","542 - And Muhammad ibn Mirdas narrated it to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Isa narrated to us, he said: Yunus - meaning Ibn Ubayd - narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Abidah, from Ali - may Allah be pleased with him."
"570","543 - And Abdullah ibn al-Sabbah al-Attar narrated it to us, he said: al-Mutamir ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Awf, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Abidah, from Ali - may Allah be pleased with him."
"571","544 - And Yusuf ibn Musa narrated it to us, he said: Waki narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Ibrahim, from Ibn Sirin, from Abidah, from Ali - may Allah be pleased with him."
"572","545 - And Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahim narrated it to us, he said: Shabbabah ibn Sawwar narrated to us, he said: Abu Amr ibn al-Ala narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Abidah, from Ali - may Allah be pleased with him."
"573","546 - And Muhammad ibn al-Layth al-Haddadi narrated it to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr narrated to us, from Jarir ibn Hazim, from Ibn Sirin, from Abidah, from Ali - may Allah be pleased with him - and the wording is Ayyubs: That he mentioned the Khawarij and said: Among them is a man with a small hand, or deficient hand, or deformed hand. Were it not that you would become arrogant, I would narrate to you what Allah promised those who fight you, upon the tongue of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Abidah said: I said to Ali - may Allah be pleased with him: Did you hear this from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: Yes, by the Lord of the Kabah; yes, by the Lord of the Kabah."
"574","547 - And Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated it to us, he said: Ibn Abi Adi narrated to us, from Ibn Awn, from Muhammad, from Abidah, from Ali - may Allah be pleased with him."
"575","548 - Abu al-Khattab Ziyad ibn Yahya al-Hassani narrated to us, he said: Azhar ibn Sad narrated to us, from Ibn Awn, from Muhammad, from Abidah, from Ali - may Allah be pleased with him - he said: Fatimah, may Allah be pleased with her, came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) complaining of blisters on her hand from the millstone, and he mentioned the hadith."
"576","549 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Hisham - meaning Ibn Hassan - narrated to us, from Muhammad, from Abidah, from Ali - may Allah be pleased with him -: That the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said on the day of al-Ahzab: They detained us from the middle prayer until the sun set; may Allah fill their houses and graves with fire. And we do not know this hadith narrated from Muhammad from Abidah except from the hadith of Hisham ibn Hassan. It was narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from various routes; Abdullah ibn Masud, Ibn Abbas, Samurah and others narrated it."
"577","550 - Muhammad ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Muhammad ibn Abi Razin narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Hassan narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Abidah, from Ali - may Allah be pleased with him - he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade me wearing gold rings and wearing qassi garments. And this speech was narrated from Ali - may Allah be pleased with him - from more than one route. And this isnad is a sound isnad among them, so we sufficed with it."
"578","551 - Rizqullah ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud al-Hafari narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Zaidah narrated to us, from Sufyan al-Thawri, from Hisham - meaning Ibn Hassan - from Muhammad, from Abidah, from Ali - may Allah be pleased with him - he said: Jibril - peace be upon him - came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of Badr and said: Give your companions choice between ransom or killing, on condition that as many as them will be killed next year. He said: We choose ransom, and some of us will be killed next year. And Hisham narrated this hadith from Muhammad, from Abidah, from Ali - may Allah be pleased with him. And it is not known from other than Ali - may Allah be pleased with him - and Abu Dawud al-Hafari did not narrate it with an isnad from Ibn Abi Zaidah from al-Thawri. And Ibn Awn narrated this hadith but did not narrate it with an isnad except Ibn Ararah from Azhar from Ibn Awn from Muhammad from Abidah from Ali - may Allah be pleased with him. And Bishr ibn Adam ibn Bint Azhar brought it out to me from the original book of Azhar, and in it is: From Ibn Awn, from Muhammad, from Abidah, mursal. Likewise the hadith: Fatimah - may Allah be pleased with her - came, is also mursal, though Ziyad ibn Yahya narrated to us the hadith of Fatimah from Muhammad from Abidah from Ali - may Allah be pleased with him."
"579","552 - Muhammad ibn Mamar narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Ashath narrated to us, from al-Hasan, he said: Ali - may Allah be pleased with him - said: I was a man who emitted much madhiy, so I asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about that, and he said: In it is wudu."
"580","553 - And Muhammad ibn Mamar narrated it to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Ashath narrated to us, from Muhammad, from Abidah, from Ali - may Allah be pleased with him - with the like of it. And we do not know anyone narrating it from Ashath except Rawh ibn Ubadah."
"581","554 - Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abi Adi narrated to us, from Ash'ath, from Muhammad, from Abidah, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade wearing gold rings and wearing qassi garments. And this hadith has been narrated from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, through various channels, from Muhammad, from the hadith of Hisham, from Muhammad, and Ash'ath, from Muhammad."
"582","555 - Muhammad ibn Ma'mar narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Abu Hassan al-A'raj, from Abidah al-Salmani, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said on the day of al-Ahzab: May Allah fill their houses and their graves with fire against them, as they kept us busy from the middle prayer until the sun set."
"583","And from what Zirr ibn Hubaysh narrated from Ali. 556 - Yusuf ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Asim, from Zirr, he said: I heard Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Indeed for every Prophet there is a disciple, and my disciple is al-Zubayr. And this hadith has been narrated from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, through more than one channel. And we do not know of it being narrated except from the hadith of Asim, from Zirr, and more than one narrated it from Asim, from Zirr."
"584","557 - Ahmad ibn Abdah narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Asim ibn Bahdalah, from Zirr, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said on the day of al-Ahzab: They kept us busy from the middle prayer until the sun set; may Allah fill their graves and their houses with fire."
"585","558 - And Muhammad ibn Ma'mar narrated it to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, he said: Asim ibn Bahdalah narrated to us, he said: I heard Zirr ibn Hubaysh, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: They kept us busy from the prayer until the sun set; may Allah fill their graves with fire, or their houses with fire, or their bellies with fire. And this hadith has been narrated from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, through more than one channel."
"586","559 - Hamzah ibn Awn al-Mas'udi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Qasim al-Asadi narrated to us, from Sufyan, and Sharik, and Ibn Ayyash, from Asim, from Zirr ibn Hubaysh, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was on Hira and it shook, so he said: Ten are in Paradise: Abu Bakr, and Umar, and Uthman, and Ali, and Talhah, and Abd al-Rahman, and Sa'd, and Sa'id ibn Zayd, may Allah be pleased with them all. He said: And I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: For every Prophet there is a disciple, and my disciple is al-Zubayr, may Allah be pleased with him."
"587","560 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Adi ibn Thabit, from Zirr, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: By the One who split the seed and created the soul, it is indeed the covenant of the unlettered Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to me: that none loves me except a believer, and none hates me except a hypocrite. And this hadith, we do not know of it being narrated from Ali with a better isnad than this isnad."
"588","561 - Muhammad ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Raja narrated to us, he said: Rabi'ah ibn Ubayd narrated to us, from al-Minhal ibn Amr, from Zirr ibn Hubaysh, he said: Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, was asked about the wudu of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he poured water in the courtyard, then called for water, and said: Where is the one asking about the wudu of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he washed his hands three times, and his face three times, and washed his forearms three times, then wiped his head until it was almost dripping, then washed his feet three times, then said: This is how the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to perform wudu. And we do not know of al-Minhal narrating, from Zirr, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, any musnad hadith except this hadith."
"589","562 - Abdullah ibn Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abu Khalid narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Asim, from Zirr ibn Hubaysh, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Ali, ask Allah for guidance and uprightness, and recall by guidance your guiding of the way, and by uprightness your straightening of the arrow. And this hadith, I think Abu Khalid erred in its isnad, for no one corroborated him on this hadith with this isnad; rather this hadith is only narrated from Asim ibn Kulayb, from Abu Burdah, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him."
"590","And from what Shaqiq ibn Salamah narrated from Ali. 563 - Yusuf ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Sayyar Abu al-Hakam, from Abu Wa'il, he said: A man came to Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, and said: Help me with my mukatabah, so he said: Shall I not teach you words that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) taught me, by which if you had a debt like Mount Sir, Allah would pay it off for you? Say: O Allah, suffice me with Your lawful against Your unlawful, and enrich me by Your bounty from all besides You. And this hadith, we do not know of it being narrated from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, except from this channel with this isnad."
"591","564 - Yusuf ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Sulayman al-Razi narrated to us, he said: I heard Abu Sinan, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, he said: I said to Shaqiq ibn Salamah: Narrate to me about Dhu al-Thudayyah. He said: When we fought them, Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Seek a man whose mark is such-and-such, so we sought him and did not find him, so we said to him: We did not find him, so he wept, and said: Seek him, for by Allah I have neither lied nor been lied to. He said: So we sought him and did not find him, so he wept, and said: Seek, for by Allah I have neither lied nor been lied to, so we sought him and did not find him. He said: So he mounted his grey mule and we sought him, and we found him under reeds, and when he saw him he prostrated. And we do not know of Habib ibn Abi Thabit narrating, from Shaqiq, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, except this hadith."
"592","565 - Isma'il ibn Abi al-Harith narrated to us, he said: Shabababah ibn Sawwar narrated to us, he said: Shu'ayb ibn Maymun narrated to us, from Husayn ibn Abd al-Rahman, from al-Sha'bi, from Shaqiq, he said: It was said to Ali, may Allah be pleased with him: Will you not appoint a successor over us? He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not appoint a successor, so should I appoint a successor over you? And if Allah the Blessed and Exalted wants good for the people, He will gather them upon the best of them, as He gathered them after their Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) upon the best of them. And this hadith is not narrated from Shaqiq, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, except from this channel with this isnad."
"593","And from what Suwayd ibn Ghafalah narrated from Ali. 566 - Abu Kurayb narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Yusuf ibn Abi Ishaq narrated to us, from his father, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu Qays al-Awdi, from Suwayd ibn Ghafalah, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A people will emerge at the end of time reciting the Quran, it will not pass beyond their throats; they will pass out of the religion as the arrow passes out of the game; fighting them is a duty upon every Muslim."
"594","567 - And this hadith has been narrated by Isra'il, from Abu Ishaq, from Suwayd ibn Ghafalah, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: A people will emerge at the end of time. Then he mentioned similar to it, and Isra'il did not insert, from Abu Ishaq, between Abu Ishaq and Suwayd ibn Ghafalah anyone. So Muhammad ibn Marzuq narrated to us the hadith of Isra'il, from Abdullah ibn Raja, from Isra'il."
"595","568 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Khaythamah, from Suwayd ibn Ghafalah, he said: Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: At the end of time a people will emerge, young in teeth, foolish in minds, speaking from the best speech of creation; their faith will not pass beyond their throats, so wherever you find them, kill them, for killing them is a reward for whoever kills them. And this hadith has been narrated from Ali through more than one channel, so we sufficed with this isnad. And it has been narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) through various channels; Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, and Abu Hurayrah, and Sahl ibn Hunayf and others narrated it."
"596","569 - And Muhammad ibn Ma'mar narrated it to us, he said: Qabisah ibn Uqbah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Khaythamah, from Suwayd ibn Ghafalah, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: When I narrate to you a hadith from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), for me to fall from the sky is more beloved to me than to lie about the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and when I narrate in matters between us, then war is deception. I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: There will be peoples at the end of time, young in teeth, foolish in minds, speaking from the best speech of creation, reciting the Quran, their faith not passing beyond their throats, so when you meet them, kill them, for killing them is a reward for whoever kills them on the Day of Resurrection."
"597","570 - Khallad ibn Aslam narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Abu Husayn, from Suwayd ibn Ghafalah, he said: Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, was brought some heretics, so he went out to the market, dug a pit, and burned them with fire, and raised his head to the sky, and said: Allah and His Messenger spoke the truth. Then he set out until he entered al-Rahbah, so I followed him, and when he wanted to enter the house, he said: What is with you, O Suwayd? I said: O Commander of the Faithful, a word I heard when you burned these heretics, you saying: Allah and His Messenger spoke the truth. He said: O Suwayd, when you hear me saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, then know that for me to fall from the sky is more beloved to me than to say what I did not hear from him; and when you see me speaking with likes of this, then it is only something by which I anger them, or a word like it."
"598","And from what Qays ibn Abi Hazim narrated from Ali. 571 - Abbad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us, he said: al-Sayyid ibn Isa narrated to us, from Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, he said: Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said: March with us to the remainder of al-Ahzab, march with us to what Allah and His Messenger said; we say: Allah and His Messenger spoke the truth, and they say: Allah and His Messenger lied."
"599","572 - Abbad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Arqam narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from al-Hakam, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, similar to it."
"600","573 - Khallad ibn Aslam narrated to us, he said: Hunayfah ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Sawwar ibn Mus'ab narrated to us, from Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, from Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: Alqamah ibn Ulathah entered upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he called for a head and began eating with him, then Bilal came and called him to prayer but he did not answer, so he returned and stayed in the mosque as long as Allah willed, then he came and said: The prayer, O Messenger of Allah, by Allah morning has come. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: May Allah have mercy on Bilal; were it not for Bilal we would have hoped for a concession for us between us and sunrise. Then Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Were it not that Bilal insisted, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would have eaten until Jibril said to him: Lift your hand. And this hadith, we do not know of anyone narrating it from Isma'il, from Qays, except Sawwar ibn Mus'ab, and he is weak in hadith."
